// TypeRules — THE source-level type rules of the Maxon tier: when two types AGREE, and what
// an operator's operands are allowed to be.
//
// ONE FILE, BECAUSE THERE IS ONE RULE. "Do these two types agree?" is asked from five places
// that share nothing else — the word operators (`and`/`or`/`xor`), every comparison, every
// `return`, every `if`/`while` condition, and (in SemanticCheck, the only one with the
// whole-program signatures it needs) every call argument. A second copy of the rule would not
// stay a copy. It would drift, and the drift surfaces as a program the compiler accepts on one
// path and rejects on the other — a WRONG ANSWER, not a compile error. That is not a
// hypothetical here: `4 and flag` was correctly rejected while `4 + flag` compiled to `4 + 1`
// and `takeInt(flag)` passed a bool into an int parameter, precisely because the only rule that
// existed lived on the `and`/`or`/`xor` path and nothing else asked it anything.
//
// EVERY RULE IS STATED OVER `ValueTypeTag`, NEVER `MaxonType`. A Maxon union value is a heap
// object, so a rule that took or returned a `MaxonType` would ALLOCATE on the per-operator path
// — see MaxonDialect's ValueTypeTag header, where `scale-test` charged the first cut of exactly
// that +4,165 allocations on the smallest rung. A tag is a scalar.
//
// Ported from maxon-selfhosted's `CastCategory` + `validateCompareOp` (TypeResolution.maxon:
// 1863-1898 and 2726-2740), which partitions every type into a CATEGORY and requires two
// operands to share one. The DIVERGENCE is where it runs: v1 validates at type resolution,
// shv2 at the PARSER. The parser is already the tier that must know a value's source type (a
// bool `and` is CONTROL FLOW — see Parser.emitShortCircuit — and no later pass can retrofit
// that decision), so it already holds a per-value type column. Putting the check anywhere else
// would mean a SECOND type oracle downstream, which is the very duplication this file exists to
// prevent. The one rule that genuinely cannot live in the parser — a call argument, whose
// callee may be in another file — reads the same column, published on `IrFunction.valueTags`.

// The type CLASSES the language's implicit-conversion lattice has. Two types AGREE iff they
// land in the same class.
//
//   • `numeric` — every NUMBER: `int`, `float`, `ExitCode`, and every ranged typealias.
//     Conversions WITHIN the class are implicit (specs/implicit-type-conversion.md: an int
//     promotes to a float, a float truncates to an int), which is exactly why the class — and
//     not the individual type — is the unit of agreement.
//   • `boolean` — `bool`, and nothing else. **A bool converts to nothing, and nothing converts
//     to a bool.** That single sentence is the whole rule this file enforces; `takeInt(flag)`,
//     `4 + flag`, `4 < flag` and `if 4` are one error wearing four syntaxes.
//   • `unknown` — a type the parser could not pin. Since the whole-program signature index
//     landed (`Queries.queryProgramSignatures`), it is reachable for exactly one thing: a call
//     to a function **NO FILE IN THE PROGRAM DECLARES**. It agrees with EVERYTHING — that is a
//     DEFERRAL, not a contradiction — and that deferral is now SAFE rather than merely
//     conservative: such a call is reported by `SemanticCheck.validateCall` (E3004, "call to
//     undefined function"), which reads the post-parse registry, so a program carrying an
//     `unknown` NEVER reaches codegen. Before the index, `unknown` was also the answer for a
//     callee declared in ANOTHER FILE — a perfectly good program — and THAT is what made the
//     deferral unsound: `isReady()` from a second file typed as unknown, agreed with `int`, and
//     `x + 41` returned 42 off a bool's payload.
//   • `noValue` — the class of the `void` tag: a ValueId that names no value at all. It agrees
//     with NOTHING, not even itself, which is the exact opposite of `unknown` and the reason
//     the two must never share a tag again (they did: see `ValueTypeTag.void`).
//   • `structRef` — a reference to a declared struct type. It converts to nothing and nothing
//     converts to it, which is what keeps `p + 1`, `return p` from an `ExitCode` function, `if p`
//     and `takeInt(p)` out of the language: all four are one error, and all four would have
//     compiled had a struct been filed under `named` (see MaxonType's `structRef` arm).
//
//     ⚠ **IT IS THE ONE CLASS THAT IS COARSER THAN THE TYPE, AND THAT IS A KNOWN GAP, NOT A
//     RULE.** Two DIFFERENT structs land in this one class, so `typeClassesAgree` says a `Point`
//     and a `Rect` agree — they do not. The fix is the one `typeClassOf`'s `named` arm already
//     prescribes for its own eventual break: the rules are stated over a TAG, which is a scalar
//     and carries no name, so telling two structs apart needs the parser's `valueNameIds` column
//     PUBLISHED alongside `valueTags` and read here. That is a change to every rule's signature
//     and it buys nothing at P1.0r — no program can put two struct values at one site, because a
//     struct cannot yet be a parameter (P1.4's ownership ruling), a return type, or a field. **The
//     day any of those lands, this arm is the bug.**
//   • `text` — the class of a `String` (P1.2 wave A). Like `structRef`, it converts to nothing and
//     nothing converts to it: a String is a pointer to a byte record, not a number and not a bool, so
//     `s + 1`, `return s` from an `ExitCode` function, `if s` and `takeInt(s)` are all one error and
//     all rejected here. Its ONLY operators are `==`/`!=` (a runtime byte compare, handled in
//     `Parser.emitCompare` BEFORE `comparableOperands` is ever asked) and `print` — so two texts
//     AGREE (both `text`), but they do not `comparableOperands` (ordering is meaningless), which is
//     the guard that keeps `s < t` out of the language. Unlike `structRef`, `text` is NOT coarser
//     than the type: there is exactly one String type, so two `text` tags naming one type is exact.
export enum TypeClass
	boolean
	numeric
	unknown
	noValue
	structRef
	text
	// The class of a non-capturing FUNCTION VALUE (P1.5-A1). Like `structRef`/`text`, it converts to
	// nothing and nothing converts to it — a function value is a code pointer, not a number and not a
	// bool, so `f + 1`, `return f` from an `ExitCode` function and `if f` are all one error. It AGREES
	// with another `function` and nothing else, which is exactly the type rule the spec states: "a
	// function value is only usable where a function type declared with 'typealias' is expected." Two
	// function types agreeing at the class level is A1's assignability (tag-level, not structural — the
	// arity/param-type identity a function-to-function mismatch would need arrives with a later rung).
	function
	// The class of a TYPE-PARAMETER value (P1.6-A) — `T` inside a generic type. It is OPAQUE: at this
	// trivial slice `T` is never substituted, so a `T` value may hold ANY concrete type (an int, a
	// bool, a trivial struct), and a concrete value may meet a `T` place. So it AGREES WITH ANYTHING,
	// exactly like `unknown` — a DEFERRAL, not a contradiction. Unlike `unknown` it is not "a callee no
	// file declares": it is a real, deliberately opaque type, so it gets its own class rather than
	// borrowing `unknown`'s (which carries E3004 semantics). It lets `IntBox.create(42)` pass an int to
	// a `T` parameter and `b.get() == 42` compare a `T` result as an i64.
	typeParameter
	// The class of a `Character` — one extended grapheme cluster (P1.8 slice B). Its OWN class, NOT
	// `text`, even though a Character and a String share a record: a class is what AGREES with what, and
	// `s == c` must be a type error rather than a byte compare. Like `text` it converts to nothing and
	// nothing converts to it.
	//
	// ⭐ **UNLIKE `text` IT IS ORDERED (A5m-c).** `stdlib/Character.maxon:19` declares
	// `implements … Comparable` and `:80-104` writes the order out — byte-lexicographic, shorter-prefix
	// first — while `stdlib/String.maxon:49` declares no such thing. So the two byte-record types part
	// company at exactly this rule and nowhere else: two Characters `comparableOperands`, two Strings
	// still do not. `c < d` is the `Character.compare` cascade, NOT a pointer compare.
	character
	// ⭐ **The class of an EXISTENTIAL — a value held at its INTERFACE type (P1.7a-existentials slice 1).**
	// Its OWN class, and it agrees with NOTHING but another existential — deliberately, including with the
	// `structRef` of a conforming type. A conformer reaching an interface-typed place is a WIDENING (the
	// value gains a witness half), not an agreement. Admitting `structRef` here would let one through with
	// no witness paired — the fat pointer's second half simply absent — which is a wild jump at the first
	// dispatch rather than a diagnostic.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THERE IS EXACTLY ONE WIDENING POSITION AT THIS SLICE, AND IT IS THE CALL ARGUMENT.**
	// `SemanticCheck.checkOneArgType` asks `ConformanceCheck.valueWidensToInterface` (conformance, walked
	// transitively through `extends`) and the LOWERING materializes `__witness_<conformer>.<Iface>`. Every
	// OTHER declared place that could hold a fat pointer is refused outright — a struct field, a union
	// payload, a return type and a container element, all by `Parser.requireStorableDeclaredType` — because
	// none of them has room for the second word. So there is no fourth door to keep in step with, and this
	// class agreeing with nothing is what routes every other position to a diagnostic.
	//
	// ⚠ **TWO EXISTENTIALS AGREEING AT THE CLASS LEVEL IS TAG-LEVEL ASSIGNABILITY AND NOTHING MORE — the
	// INTERFACE identity is `Parser.aggregateNameOf`'s to compare, and it must.** A `Derived` value reaching
	// a `Base`-typed binding agrees here, and its witness half addresses `Derived`'s table while the place
	// dispatches by numbering `Base`'s: MEASURED before that arm existed, a program that compiled clean and
	// returned 2 where the answer is 1.
	interfaceValue
end 'TypeClass'

// The class a source-level type belongs to. Exhaustive over `ValueTypeTag`, so a new type
// cannot be added without being placed in the lattice.
function typeClassOf(tag ValueTypeTag) returns TypeClass
	return match tag 'k'
		boolean gives TypeClass.boolean
		integer gives TypeClass.numeric
		float gives TypeClass.numeric
		// A `named` type is a ranged typealias, and EVERY alias shv2 can declare is NUMERIC:
		// `Parser.readTypeAliasDeclaration` accepts `int(low to high)` and `float(low to high)` — it
		// rejects every other form with a positioned `unsupported` — and hands `RangedTypeAlias.create`
		// a literal `MaxonType.integer` or `MaxonType.float` as the underlying type. Both are
		// `TypeClass.numeric` (the two arms above), so this arm stays correct: a float alias is numeric
		// exactly as an int one is, and the promotion design puts them in one class deliberately.
		//
		// It remains a THEOREM about the current compiler, not a guess — its one KILL condition is now
		// a `bool` alias, which the parser refuses (a `bool` has no range). The day a NON-numeric alias
		// (bool, or a String/struct alias) becomes legal, this arm must resolve through the alias
		// registry — which needs the alias's NAME id, which the parser already computes
		// (`Parser.valueNameIds`) and would then have to publish alongside the tags.
		named gives TypeClass.numeric
		exitCode gives TypeClass.numeric
		unresolved gives TypeClass.unknown
		void gives TypeClass.noValue
		// A declared struct type. NOT `numeric` — a struct is a POINTER, and the arm above is a
		// theorem about ALIASES that a struct would have falsified. See TypeClass's `structRef`.
		structRef gives TypeClass.structRef
		// A String. NOT `numeric` (it is a pointer, not a number) and NOT `structRef` (it is a
		// builtin, not a declared `type`, and must not be admitted to `p == q`'s reference-equality
		// rejection or a struct's field lookup). Its own class, agreeing only with itself.
		string gives TypeClass.text
		// A function value. Its own class — see TypeClass's `function` arm. A function value fits a
		// function-typed place (both `function`) and nothing else (E3005 everywhere else).
		function gives TypeClass.function
		// A type parameter is OPAQUE and agrees with anything (P1.6-A) — its own deferral class.
		typeParameter gives TypeClass.typeParameter
		// A generic INSTANCE is a struct pointer, so it shares the `structRef` class (a reference that
		// converts to nothing). It reaches here only if a place is declared with a generic-instance
		// alias — the trivial slice never mints a genericInstance-tagged VALUE (a factory returns
		// `Self`, i.e. `structRef`), so this is the sound answer for the type, not a hot path.
		genericInstance gives TypeClass.structRef
		// A Character. NOT `text` — see TypeClass's `character` arm: sharing the String RECORD is a
		// REPRESENTATION fact, and a class is an AGREEMENT fact. Agreeing with `text` here would make
		// `s == c` a silent byte compare and `print(c)` legal.
		character gives TypeClass.character
		// An EXISTENTIAL — a value at its interface type. NOT `structRef`, even though its VALUE half is a
		// struct pointer: see TypeClass's `interfaceValue` for why a conformer must not simply agree.
		interfaceRef gives TypeClass.interfaceValue
	end 'k'
end 'typeClassOf'

// **THE decision.** Every type-agreement question in the compiler bottoms out here, and
// nothing else answers one.
//
// The two special classes pull in OPPOSITE directions, and the order they are tested in is
// therefore load-bearing:
//   • `noValue` agrees with NOTHING — including a second `noValue` (`noop() == noop()` compares
//     two things that are not there). Tested FIRST, so a non-value can never be admitted by the
//     deferral below.
//   • `unknown` agrees with ANYTHING — a deferral, not a judgment (see TypeClass).
// Everything else agrees iff it is the same class.
function typeClassesAgree(left TypeClass, right TypeClass) returns bool
	if left == TypeClass.noValue or right == TypeClass.noValue 'nonValue'
		return false
	end 'nonValue'
	if left == TypeClass.unknown or right == TypeClass.unknown 'deferred'
		return true
	end 'deferred'
	// A TYPE PARAMETER agrees with anything (P1.6-A) — the same deferral `unknown` gets, for the
	// different reason that `T` is a real, opaque type that ANY concrete type may inhabit at this
	// trivial slice (no substitution). Tested after `noValue` so a `T` never meets a non-value.
	//
	// ⚠ **A COMPARISON NEVER REACHES THIS DEFERRAL FOR A VALUE OF AN *ENCLOSING* TYPE PARAMETER.** All six
	// comparison operators on such a value are intercepted upstream and dispatched through the parameter's
	// `Equatable`/`Comparable` witness (`Parser.emitCompare`, P1.7a slice 2b/2b-ii) — because letting them
	// through here would compare two opaque WORDS, i.e. two POINTERS for a struct `T`, a silent wrong answer.
	// ⛔⛔ **AND A COERCION SITE NO LONGER REACHES IT WITH A CONCRETE AGGREGATE ON THE OTHER SIDE EITHER
	// (W179).** This header used to end *"the deferral is still right for … every non-comparison agreement
	// question (an argument, a `return`, an assignment)"*, and that sentence named the exact case it was wrong
	// about: `checkDeclaredType` now refuses an opaque value meeting a declared `String`/struct/function/
	// `Character` BEFORE asking anything here, because the answer is DIRECTIONAL and a symmetric rule cannot
	// carry a direction. The reason lives on that guard; what belongs here is only that the deferral below is
	// no longer the last word for those pairs.
	//
	// The deferral is still right for what DOES reach it: a concrete generic-method result in a non-generic
	// caller, a `T` meeting a `T`, a concrete value meeting an opaque PLACE (the reverse direction, which the
	// instantiation settles at the call site), and every scalar pairing.
	if left == TypeClass.typeParameter or right == TypeClass.typeParameter 'typeParam'
		return true
	end 'typeParam'
	return left == right
end 'typeClassesAgree'

// Do two VALUES' types agree? The form the symmetric rules ask in: a word operator's two
// operands, a comparison's two operands, a `return`'s value against the declared return type,
// a call argument against its parameter.
export function typesAgree(left ValueTypeTag, right ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	return typeClassesAgree(typeClassOf(left), right: typeClassOf(right))
end 'typesAgree'

// May a value of type `tag` stand where a value of class `required` is demanded? The form the
// one-sided rules ask in — the operator names a DOMAIN rather than a second operand: `+` and
// `shl` and unary `-` require a number, an `if` requires a bool. It is the same decision, asked
// of a class instead of a second type.
export function typeClassAdmits(required TypeClass, tag ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	return typeClassesAgree(required, right: typeClassOf(tag))
end 'typeClassAdmits'

// ⭐ **A CAST HAS NO LEGAL CONVERSION WHEN IT CROSSES BETWEEN INCOMPATIBLE KINDS (E3009).** A managed
// AGGREGATE — a String (`text`), a struct/generic instance (`structRef`), a function value (`function`) —
// is a POINTER. There is no number it *is* (`"x" as Age`, `p as Age`, `f as Age`, `42 as SomeString`) and
// no OTHER aggregate it *is* (`p as String`, `f as String`): the cast describes no conversion, so it is
// rejected outright rather than let through as a representational no-op. The no-op was the bug — it typed
// the aggregate's pointer as the target and either miscompiled (a range check on a struct pointer; or a
// String drop run on a struct box, a DOUBLE FREE that faults the runtime MM) or surfaced only downstream
// at the next type-checked position.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AND IT IS ALSO THE RULE FOR THE TWO SCALAR CLASSES, WHICH IT ONCE WAVED THROUGH.** Its own header
// used to claim *"different concrete classes convert ONLY when both are scalars (int↔float, bool↔int)"*,
// and that premise is FALSE against `specs/type-casting.md`: **a `bool` converts to nothing and nothing
// converts to a `bool`** — the very sentence `TypeClass`'s `boolean` arm states — so `true as Integer`,
// `true as Float`, `0 as bool` and `0.0 as bool` are all E3009. MEASURED before the fix: `b as Integer`
// for a `let b = true` PRINTED `true`, because the cast was a complete no-op that left a bool payload
// wearing an integer type; `7 as bool` printed `7`. A silent wrong answer, in the one place a cast is
// supposed to be the type check.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AND ONE PAIR INSIDE THE `numeric` CLASS IS LOSSY: `float → int`.** A class cannot see it — the two
// are deliberately one class, or `5 + 2.0` would not compile — so it is asked of the TAGS, through
// `isLossyFloatToInt`: the SAME call `checkDeclaredType` makes for the implicit doors, so the explicit `as`
// and every implicit conversion cannot come to disagree about it (that header records the two times they
// did). `5.9 as Integer` SILENTLY TRUNCATED to 5 before this call existed. The other direction —
// `int → float` — is a WIDENING and stays legal: it is what `int-to-float` and `byte-to-float` pin.
//
// It is the ONE cast-legality rule, and it is bounded to what a TAG can decide:
//   • BOTH ends must be CONCRETE (an aggregate or a scalar). A cast touching an opaque `T`
//     (`typeParameter`) or an unresolved type (`unknown`/`noValue`) is left to the existing rules — NOT
//     rejected here.
//   • DIFFERENT concrete classes never convert. Symmetric in the two ends — a cast is symmetric in what it
//     may cross — and that now covers `boolean` against `numeric` as well as anything against an aggregate.
//   • The SAME class is an identity or a real conversion, EXCEPT the lossy `float → int` above. The
//     `structRef` COARSENESS a tag cannot yet split rides the same arm — two different structs share
//     `structRef`, so `Point as Rect` is not caught here (that needs the `valueNameIds` publication
//     `TypeClass.structRef` already names as its fix).
//
// ⚠⚠ **PRECONDITION: BOTH TAGS MUST BE FLOAT-RESOLVED** — stated in full on `isLossyFloatToInt`, which is
// what needs it. `Parser.denotedCastTargetTag` supplies it for the TARGET of an `as`, and that same lift is
// what makes `true as Float` say "Cannot cast from bool to float" rather than naming the alias's erased
// `int` — so this door depends on it for its WORDING as well as for its verdict.
export function castHasNoLegalConversion(source ValueTypeTag, target ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	let sourceClass = typeClassOf(source)
	let targetClass = typeClassOf(target)
	let sourceConcrete = typeClassIsAggregate(sourceClass) or typeClassIsScalar(sourceClass)
	let targetConcrete = typeClassIsAggregate(targetClass) or typeClassIsScalar(targetClass)
	if not sourceConcrete or not targetConcrete 'opaque'
		return false
	end 'opaque'

	if sourceClass != targetClass 'differentKinds'
		return true
	end 'differentKinds'

	return isLossyFloatToInt(source, declared: target)
end 'castHasNoLegalConversion'

// A managed AGGREGATE type class — a String (`text`), a struct or generic instance (`structRef`), or a
// function value (`function`): a pointer, not a scalar. Exhaustive over `TypeClass`, so a new class
// cannot slip through as a silent `false`.
function typeClassIsAggregate(c TypeClass) returns bool
	return match c 'c'
		structRef gives true
		text gives true
		function gives true
		// A Character is a POINTER to a byte record, exactly as a String is — so `c as Age` and
		// `42 as Character` describe no conversion and are E3009, for `text`'s reason.
		character gives true
		boolean gives false
		numeric gives false
		unknown gives false
		noValue gives false
		typeParameter gives false
		// An EXISTENTIAL is a pointer pair, not a scalar — `structRef`'s answer, so `x as Age` on an
		// interface-typed value and `42 as Backend` are both E3009 rather than a silent reinterpretation of
		// the value half.
		interfaceValue gives true
	end 'c'
end 'typeClassIsAggregate'

// A SCALAR type class — a number (`numeric`: int/float/ranged-alias/ExitCode) or a `bool`. A scalar
// fits in a register; the two classes an `as` may legally convert BETWEEN, and the two that make a
// nonsense cast when crossed with an aggregate. Exhaustive over `TypeClass`.
function typeClassIsScalar(c TypeClass) returns bool
	return match c 'c'
		numeric gives true
		boolean gives true
		structRef gives false
		text gives false
		function gives false
		// A Character is a record pointer, not a register-sized number — `text`'s answer.
		character gives false
		unknown gives false
		noValue gives false
		typeParameter gives false
		// An existential is a pointer pair, not a register-sized number — `structRef`'s answer.
		interfaceValue gives false
	end 'c'
end 'typeClassIsScalar'

// --- The COERCION SITES ---
//
// ⭐ **A COERCION SITE IS NOT A SYMMETRIC ONE, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE WHOLE OF THIS SECTION.**
//
// `typesAgree` asks whether two values may MEET — `a == b`, `a and b`. Neither side is in charge,
// and that is right for an operator. But four places in this compiler ask a DIFFERENT question:
// may this VALUE stand where THAT DECLARED TYPE is demanded? A call argument against its
// parameter, a `return` against its function's declared return type, an assignment against its
// binding's type, a store against a global's. There, one side is a promise the program already
// made, and the other has to keep it.
//
// The two questions had ONE rule, and the difference is exactly where the language's contradiction
// lived: agreement is symmetric, so it cannot see a direction, and a lossy conversion is nothing
// but a direction. `float` and `int` agree — they must, or `5 + 2.0` would not compile — so
// `takeInt(3.7)` agreed too, and the float reached codegen as an f64 in a GPR.

// The verdict on a value meeting a DECLARED type.
//
// An `enum`, not a `union`, for the reason `BinOpOperands` gives: no arm carries a payload, a
// Maxon union value is a heap object even for a payload-free case, and this is asked once per
// call argument, per return, per assignment.
export enum DeclaredTypeMatch
	ok
	// Different classes — a bool where a number is declared, or the reverse. The rule
	// `typesAgree` already owned.
	mismatch
	// ⭐ **A FLOAT MEETING A DECLARED INTEGER.** The classes AGREE — both numeric — and the
	// conversion is still forbidden, which is why no symmetric rule could ever have caught it.
	//
	// It is rejected rather than truncated, and the language had already said so twice over:
	// `specs/type-casting.md` rejects the EXPLICIT `5.0 as int` with E3009 and answers "use
	// trunc/round/floor/ceil instead", and `trunc(x)` EXISTS as a builtin for exactly this. A
	// language that makes you say `trunc` when you write `as`, and then truncates silently when
	// you write nothing at all, has two rules for one conversion and one of them is invisible.
	// (`specs/implicit-type-conversion.md` used to be the invisible one: `float → int: Truncate
	// toward zero`. P1.0d.4 deleted that row.)
	//
	// ⚠ THE OTHER DIRECTION IS FINE AND IS NOT HERE. `int → float` is WIDENING and loses nothing,
	// `type-casting.md` blesses even the explicit `100 as float`, and `arithResultTag` promotes on
	// it. Only the lossy direction is a lie.
	narrowsFloatToInt
	// ⭐ **A FUNCTION VALUE MEETING A DECLARED NON-FUNCTION TYPE** (P1.5-A1) — `loc = dbl`, `slot = dbl`,
	// `return dbl` from a `returns int` function, a function arg to an int parameter. Its OWN verdict, not
	// `mismatch`, because the spec's E3005 for it has SPECIFIC advice ("a function value is only usable
	// where a function type declared with 'typealias' is expected"), and — the reason it lives HERE rather
	// than as an `== function` check at each site — a function value into a FUNCTION place is `ok` while
	// into anything else is this, and stating that boundary once is what keeps the return site and the
	// assignment sites from ever disagreeing about it. Each site emits its own noun'd message
	// (`FunctionValueMisuseSuffix` is the shared tail); the CONDITION is decided once, here.
	functionIntoNonFunction
end 'DeclaredTypeMatch'

// **THE COERCION RULE.** Every site where a value meets a declared type asks this, and none of
// them restates it — which is what keeps a `return` and a call argument from disagreeing about
// what a float may become.
//
// The order is load-bearing: a class mismatch is reported as a mismatch (`return flag` from a
// `returns int` is not a "narrowing"), so agreement is established FIRST and the narrowing rule
// then runs on a pair already known to be two numbers.
// ⭐ **IS THIS A FUNCTION VALUE MEETING A FUNCTION-TYPED PLACE?** The gate every coercion-site door asks
// before recording one for the whole-program shape check. It is here beside `checkDeclaredType` because it
// is the SAME question one arm of that verdict answers, asked from the other side: `functionIntoNonFunction`
// is a function value meeting a NON-function place and is decided on the spot, while function-meets-function
// is the one pairing no tag comparison can settle — two different function types share the `function` tag
// exactly as two different structs share `structRef`.
//
// It is a `bool` and it is asked FIRST, before anything is built, for the reason
// `ProgramSignatures.isFunctionAlias` is a bool twin: these doors are every assignment and every `return` in
// the program, and the overwhelming majority are neither side a function.
export function isFunctionTypeDoor(declared ValueTypeTag, got ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	return declared == ValueTypeTag.function and got == ValueTypeTag.function
end 'isFunctionTypeDoor'

export function checkDeclaredType(value ValueTypeTag, declared ValueTypeTag) returns DeclaredTypeMatch
	// A function value fits a function-typed place and nothing else (P1.5-A1). Tested FIRST, so a function
	// value meeting a non-function place gets its specific verdict rather than the generic class
	// `mismatch` (both would be E3005, but the function one carries the "use a function typealias" advice).
	// A function into a FUNCTION place falls through to agree below; a NON-function into a function place is
	// an ordinary class `mismatch`, which is why the guard is one-sided.
	//
	// ⭐ **AN OPAQUE TYPE-PARAMETER PLACE IS NOT "A NON-FUNCTION PLACE" — IT IS A PLACE WHOSE TYPE IS
	// DECIDED SOMEWHERE ELSE (W43b).** `T` is one machine word and a function code pointer is one machine
	// word (`ProgramSignatures.slotTypeFitsOneWord` admits "a function alias" by name), so whether a
	// function value belongs there is settled by the INSTANTIATION — `Map with (String, UnaryOp)` binds
	// `Value` to a function alias and `Map with (String, int)` does not — and `checkTypeParameterArgsAt`
	// asks exactly that, against the SUBSTITUTED argument, at every concrete call site. Deciding it here
	// from the FORMAL's shape is the alternative that ruling already rejected for floats
	// (`checkTypeParameterArgsAt`'s ⚠), and it refused `specs/map.md`'s `function-valued.dispatch` — a
	// `Map<String, FunctionType>` the oracle compiles and runs.
	if value == ValueTypeTag.function and declared != ValueTypeTag.function and declared != ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'functionValue'
		return DeclaredTypeMatch.functionIntoNonFunction
	end 'functionValue'

	// ⭐⭐ **AN OPAQUE TYPE PARAMETER MEETING A CONCRETE AGGREGATE PLACE (W179).** A generic body compiles ONCE,
	// for the DECLARATION VIEW, where a `T` is one machine word whose representation no `with` has fixed. A
	// concrete aggregate place is the claim that the word is a POINTER to a particular record — it will be
	// dereferenced, stored under that type, and DROPPED by that type's destructor — and a shared body has
	// nothing with which to keep that claim. It is refused here rather than at any one door because it is the
	// SAME question at all four (`SemanticCheck.checkOneArgType`, the `return`, the global store, the scalar
	// field store), and answering it at one of them is how they came to give four different answers. MEASURED
	// on the merge base, one program per door: the managed FIELD store was refused (`Parser.
	// requireManagedValueMatches` compares the tags EXACTLY, so `self.s = t` is refused at every
	// instantiation); the `return` was refused for an unrelated reason by the descriptor door (E2015, which
	// this now answers ahead of); and the call ARGUMENT and the GLOBAL store were accepted with no diagnostic
	// at all — `takeText(t)` and `slot = t` both compiled and faulted, `0xC0000005`.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS DIRECTIONAL, WHICH IS WHY IT CANNOT LIVE IN `typeClassesAgree`.** The REVERSE pairing — a
	// concrete value meeting an opaque PLACE — is a different question with a different answer: the
	// instantiation fixes it at the call site (`SemanticCheck.checkTypeParameterArgsAt` substitutes and then
	// asks this same verdict), and `specs-shv2/generic-types.md`'s `managed-string-arg` is that direction
	// working. A symmetric rule cannot tell the two apart, and the deferral it holds is still correct for
	// every other pair that reaches it.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND IT IS NOT DECIDED FROM THE INSTANTIATIONS, DELIBERATELY.** `Outer with String` — where `T` IS
	// the declared `String` — is refused exactly as `Outer with Integer` is. Admitting it would need an
	// ALL-fold over every `with` in the program and would make a body's validity change when an unrelated
	// file adds a second instantiation. The bootstrap oracle refuses both spellings at the same position with
	// the same E3005, and the managed field door above has always refused both; this is the third answer
	// agreeing rather than a new rule.
	//
	// ⚠ **`interfaceValue` IS EXCLUDED AND THAT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT.** An existential place is a WIDENING
	// position, not an agreement one — `checkOneArgType`'s `existentialParam` arm returns before the tag rule
	// is asked at all — so whether an opaque `T` may widen into one is `existentialWideningVerdict`'s
	// question, decided against the conformances. The SCALAR classes are excluded for their own reason: a
	// word moving into a word-shaped `int`/`bool` slot dereferences nothing and drops nothing, and
	// `specs-shv2/array-declared-record.md`'s `the-self-spelling-of-a-corpus-served-member-reaches-the-same-body`
	// is a committed program that returns an opaque element as its ranged-int alias.
	if value == ValueTypeTag.typeParameter 'opaqueValue'
		let declaredClass = typeClassOf(declared)
		if typeClassIsAggregate(declaredClass) and declaredClass != TypeClass.interfaceValue 'aConcreteRecord'
			return DeclaredTypeMatch.mismatch
		end 'aConcreteRecord'
	end 'opaqueValue'

	if not typesAgree(value, right: declared) 'disagree'
		return DeclaredTypeMatch.mismatch
	end 'disagree'

	if isLossyFloatToInt(value, declared: declared) 'narrowing'
		return DeclaredTypeMatch.narrowsFloatToInt
	end 'narrowing'

	return DeclaredTypeMatch.ok
end 'checkDeclaredType'

// ⭐ **THE OBLIGATION AN `ok` VERDICT CARRIES.** `checkDeclaredType` is a pure verdict — it answers
// whether a value MAY stand in a declared slot and it cannot convert anything, because it sees two tags
// and no value. `int → float` is the one coercion an `ok` verdict leaves WORK BEHIND, and every site
// that asked the verdict, accepted `ok` and then went on using its own unconverted value put a raw
// two's-complement integer where an f64 was declared: `Self{v: 3}` read back as 1.5e-323, `return 3`
// from a `returns float` handed the backend an i64 in a GPR (a cross-register-file panic), a `p.v = 3`
// stored 3 raw bytes into an XMM slot. Six doors, one omission.
//
// It is stated HERE, once, beside the verdict it qualifies, because the obligation is a property of the
// RULE and not of any site — a site that restated it is a site that can forget it. The two CONVERSIONS
// are per-REPRESENTATION and live where their representation does: `Parser.coerceToDeclared` for an SSA
// value (a `cvtsi2sd` via `MaxonUnaryOp.promoteToFloat`) and `Parser.constFloatBits` for a parse-time
// literal (the host's own int→f64). Both are idempotent, so neither caller has to work out whether it is
// already holding a float.
//
// ⚠ **PRECONDITION 1: the verdict is already `ok`.** This asks the DIRECTION and nothing else, so it must
// never be the gate on its own. Asked ahead of the verdict, it would convert values the language rejects
// outright.
//
// ⚠⚠ **PRECONDITION 2, AND THE VERDICT DOES NOT SUPPLY IT: the VALUE's tag must already be lifted.**
// `tagIsIntegral` answers `true` for EVERY `named` ranged alias, float ones included, so this answers
// `true` for a float-alias value meeting a `float` — and `checkDeclaredType(named, float)` answers `ok`,
// so the verdict waves it through. An int alias converting is correct; a FLOAT alias converting is a
// `cvtsi2sd` over an f64 bit pattern, a silent wrong answer. The rule cannot separate them: `named` is
// one tag over both representations, and this file has no alias registry to ask.
//
// What separates them is a PARSER invariant — every producer of a float-alias value lifts its tag to
// `float` (`Parser.resolveFloatAliasType`, `ProgramSignatures.fieldTypeOf`) — and `Parser.coerceToDeclared`
// PANICS if an unlifted one reaches it. Do not restate that safeguard as "the verdict handles it": it does
// not, and saying so here is what a reader would believe.
//
// ⚠ **AND IT IS NOT THE COMPARISON RULE.** `p.v == 3` stays E3005 (`Parser.emitCompare` states why): a
// promotion inside a comparison is invisible, because the result is a bool either way. Only the sites
// that STORE a value — an argument, a `return`, an assignment, a field — carry this obligation.
export function widensIntToFloat(value ValueTypeTag, declared ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	return tagIsIntegral(value) and tagIsFloat(declared)
end 'widensIntToFloat'

// ⭐⭐ **THE LOSSY DIRECTION, WRITTEN ONCE — the mirror of `widensIntToFloat` above, and the rule BOTH
// cast doors and every implicit door decide by.** A float dropped into an integer loses its fraction, and
// Maxon refuses the conversion in every spelling rather than performing it silently: `checkDeclaredType`
// turns it into `DeclaredTypeMatch.narrowsFloatToInt` for the IMPLICIT doors (a call argument, a `return`,
// an assignment, a field store — reported through `implicitNarrowingMessage`), and
// `castHasNoLegalConversion` turns it into E3009 for the EXPLICIT `as`. The language's answer in both is
// `trunc`/`round`/`floor`/`ceil`.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS ONE FUNCTION BECAUSE THE TWO DOORS DISAGREEING ABOUT ONE CONVERSION IS A DEFECT THIS FILE HAS
// ALREADY SHIPPED — TWICE, IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS.** `specs/implicit-type-conversion.md` once carried a
// `float → int: Truncate toward zero` row while `specs/type-casting.md` rejected the explicit `5.0 as int`
// (deleted at P1.0d.4); then the explicit door was the silent one — `5.9 as Integer` truncated to 5 while
// `takeInt(5.9)` was refused. Both were one rule with two copies. A cross-reference in prose is not a
// mechanism; a shared call is.
//
// ⚠⚠ **PRECONDITION: BOTH TAGS MUST BE FLOAT-RESOLVED** — a ranged FLOAT alias must arrive as `float`, not
// as `named`. `tagIsIntegral` answers `true` for EVERY `named` alias (its own header says why it cannot do
// otherwise), so an unlifted float alias reads as an integer on either side: as the TARGET it would make
// `f as Float` — the identity conversion — the lossy one, and as the VALUE it would hide a genuine
// narrowing. The VALUE side is the parser invariant every float-alias producer obeys
// (`Parser.resolveFloatAliasType`); the TARGET side of an `as` is `Parser.denotedCastTargetTag`'s job.
function isLossyFloatToInt(value ValueTypeTag, declared ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	return tagIsFloat(value) and tagIsIntegral(declared)
end 'isLossyFloatToInt'

// **THE WORDS**, written once. Two files report this — `Queries.reportParseError` for the three
// sites the parser owns (`return`, assignment, global store) and `SemanticCheck.checkArgTypes` for
// the call argument it cannot — and they must not phrase one rule two ways. The message is the
// user's whole explanation of a rejection they did not ask for, so it names the LOSS (why), and it
// names `trunc` (what to do instead), which is what `specs/type-casting.md` already answers for the
// explicit `5.0 as int`.
//
// `round`/`floor`/`ceil` are named because they are the LANGUAGE's answer (type-casting.md lists
// all four) — and as of P1.0d.4-C all four are shv2's too (specs/{floor,ceil,round,sqrt}.md), so
// every function this message names is one the reader can call. `trunc` is named first because it
// is the one that answers the question the rejection actually asked. (`abs` is a fifth intrinsic of
// the same family but is deliberately NOT named here: it is not a narrowing, so it answers nothing
// this rejection asked.)
export function implicitNarrowingMessage(sourceType String, targetType String) returns String
	return "cannot implicitly convert '{sourceType}' to '{targetType}': the conversion is lossy and must be explicit — use trunc(x) to truncate toward zero (or round/floor/ceil)"
end 'implicitNarrowingMessage'

// ⭐ **THE ONE EXPLANATION OF THE FUNCTION-VALUE TYPE RULE** (P1.5-A1), written once. It is the tail of
// every E3005 a function value provokes — an assignment, a global store, a return, a call argument — and
// those sites must not phrase one rule several ways. It names WHERE a function value IS legal (a
// function-typed place declared with `typealias`), which is the whole content of the rule the spec
// states: "a function value is only usable where a function type declared with 'typealias' is expected."
export let FunctionValueMisuseSuffix = "a function value is only usable where a function type declared with 'typealias' is expected"

// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE EXPLANATION OF THE OPAQUE-VALUE RULE `checkDeclaredType` DECIDES ABOVE (W179)**, written once
// beside the verdict for `FunctionValueMisuseSuffix`'s reason. It exists because this refusal is the one in
// this file a CORRECT-LOOKING program provokes: `Outer with String` binds `T` to the very type the place
// declares, and it is still refused — so a bare "expected 'String', got 'type parameter'" leaves the reader
// with a contradiction rather than a cure. The tail states the fact (the body is checked once, against a view
// no `with` has reached) and then offers the two cures, BOTH MEASURED on the rung's own reproducer: declaring
// the callee's parameter at the type parameter too compiles and runs, and so does making the call from a
// concrete instantiation.
//
// ⚠ **ONLY THE ARGUMENT DOOR QUOTES IT TODAY.** The verdict is shared by four doors, but the sentences for
// the `return` and the global/local store are rendered in `Queries.renderParseError` and the field store's in
// `Parser.slotMismatchError` — outside this rung's file set — so those three still say only the head. That is
// a sentence owed, not a second rule: the tail lives here precisely so adopting it there is a reference and
// never a re-authoring.
export let OpaqueValueMisuseSuffix = "a generic body is compiled ONCE, against the declaration view, so a type parameter is an opaque machine word here and nothing has fixed what it points at — this is refused even where every 'with' in the program binds it to that very type. Declare the place at the type parameter too, or make the call from a concrete instantiation, where the type argument is known"

// ⭐ **TWO NAMED AGGREGATES ARE THE SAME TYPE IFF THEIR INTERNED NAMES ARE EQUAL** — the ONE
// name-comparison every site that meets a value against a declared aggregate shares (OPEN #54): the
// parser's coercion sites (`return`, reassignment, `otherwise` value, struct-literal field,
// union payload) AND SemanticCheck's call ARGUMENT (the one rule that needs a callee's whole-program
// signature, so it cannot live in the parser). A struct is never a subtype of another (v1's
// `argTypesCompatible`, SemanticCheck.maxon:2527), so a value of the wrong aggregate would pass the
// scalar tag check — `structRef == structRef` holds for ANY two structs — and later be dropped under
// the DECLARED type's destructor: a wild free.
//
// It lives HERE, beside `checkDeclaredType`, because it is a coercion-site type rule and a rule with
// two homes drifts. The NAME-EXTRACTION legitimately differs by tier only in WHICH interner it reads —
// the parser resolves an id against its file-local interner, SemanticCheck against the merged
// `project.typeNames` — but both handle the SAME two aggregate cases (`structRef` struct, `named`
// registered union/enum): the parser's `aggregateNameOf` and SemanticCheck's `aggregateNameFor` are
// full twins. A union PARAMETER is the one place a name is unrecoverable at the SemanticCheck tier
// (`resolveTypes` erased its `named` to `integer`), so the parser stashes it pre-erasure on the
// signature (`FuncSignature.paramAggregateNames`, OPEN #54 Slice B2). The COMPARISON RULE is this one
// function, called by both.
//
// `expected`/`got` are aggregate-name results, so an EMPTY one means "not a named aggregate". Two
// values CONFLICT whenever their aggregate IDENTITIES differ: two DIFFERENT named aggregates (both
// non-empty, unequal names), OR an aggregate meeting a SCALAR (exactly one empty). The scalar case is
// the one a tag check cannot see, because `ValueTypeTag.named` is OVERLOADED — a boxed union VALUE and
// a ranged-int alias SCALAR share it, so `checkDeclaredType(named, named)` wrongly AGREES for a union
// standing where an alias is declared. Only two SCALARS (both empty) defer to the caller's tag check,
// which the name check runs AFTER — never instead of — so a genuine class mismatch keeps its tag message.
// ⭐ **IS THE PARSER'S TAG STILL RIGHT FOR THIS MEMBER'S DECLARED RETURN? — asked so that RESOLUTION cannot
// be mistaken for disagreement.** The value's tag was minted PRE-resolution and the member's return type is
// read POST-resolution, so the two spell one type two ways all the time: a `returns ExitCode` call is tagged
// `named`/`exitCode` at the parse and `exitCode` in the registry, and a ranged alias is `named` against
// `integer`. Comparing them literally — or by storage width, which was the first attempt — reports every
// alias-returning overload as a disagreement (measured: `closure-capture.block-local-overload-arg`, whose two
// overloads BOTH return `ExitCode`, was refused with "'int' and 'int'").
//
// ⭐⭐ **TWO ASKERS, AND THEY ASK IT AT OPPOSITE ENDS OF THE SAME PASS BOUNDARY.**
// `SemanticCheck.requireOverloadResultTagAgrees` asks it of a value the parser already typed against the
// member resolution chose — the CHECK. `Parser.maxonReturnTypesMatch` asks it of two DECLARED return types,
// to find out whether the whole-program index's one-entry-per-key answer can be trusted for this name at all
// — the GATE. A set the gate calls agreeing is exactly a set the check can never report, and that is why the
// two share this rule rather than each spelling one.
//
// So agreement is the language's own coercion rule, plus the one thing that rule cannot see: a float and an
// int AGREE under it (they must, or `5 + 2.0` would not compile) while living in DIFFERENT REGISTER FILES, so
// a value tagged `integer` standing for a `float` return is a cross-file read whatever the rule says.
export function overloadResultTagAgrees(current ValueTypeTag, declared ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	return checkDeclaredType(current, declared: declared) == DeclaredTypeMatch.ok and tagIsFloat(current) == tagIsFloat(declared)
end 'overloadResultTagAgrees'

export function namedAggregatesConflict(expected ByteArray, got ByteArray) returns bool
	return not (expected.isEmpty() and got.isEmpty()) and not got.equals(expected)
end 'namedAggregatesConflict'

// ⭐⭐ **CAN THIS DECLARED TAG HOLD A NAMED AGGREGATE AT ALL? — the half of the rule above that its
// callers were SKIPPING, and the reason an aggregate reached a scalar parameter in silence.**
//
// `namedAggregatesConflict` answers all four quadrants correctly, including "exactly one empty", which is
// the aggregate-meets-scalar case its own header calls the one a tag check cannot see. But both callers
// gated the ASK on the DECLARED side being an aggregate (`if not expected.isEmpty()`), so the quadrant
// where the declared side is a SCALAR and the value is an AGGREGATE was never asked — and the tag check
// cannot refuse it either, because `resolveTypes` erases a boxed-union PARAMETER to `integer` while the
// ARGUMENT keeps its `named` tag, and `checkDeclaredType(named, integer)` is a legal widening (that is how
// a ranged alias reaches an `int`). MEASURED, with no overload in the program at all:
//
//     union Shape circle(r Integer) square(s Integer) end 'Shape'
//     function takesInt(x Integer) returns Integer  return x + 1  end 'takesInt'
//     … let sh = Shape.circle(5)  return takesInt(sh)     ⇒ compiled, exit 25
//
// i.e. the boxed union's payload word was passed as an integer and had arithmetic done on it.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS DELIBERATELY THE FOUR DEFINITE SCALARS AND NOTHING ELSE.** Answering `true` for a tag that
// legitimately admits an aggregate turns this into a FALSE REJECTION of a valid program, and three tags
// do admit one: `typeParameter` (a generic's `T` takes any concrete type — `Box.create(v T)` handed a
// struct is the whole point of P1.6), `unresolved` (agrees with everything by deferral) and `named`
// (whose aggregate name comes back empty only when `aggregateNameFor` did not RECOGNIZE it, which is not
// the same fact as "it is a scalar"). `structRef`/`genericInstance`/`string`/`character`/`function` are
// already decided by the non-empty branch or by their own rules. So the narrow four is the only set that
// is provably safe, and it is the set the measured hole needs.
//
// A full match, never a range arm or a `default`: a tag appended to `ValueTypeTag` must be classified
// deliberately, and this file's own `tagIsIntegral` records what a range arm cost the last time.
export function tagAdmitsNoAggregate(tag ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	return match tag 'k'
		boolean gives true
		integer gives true
		float gives true
		exitCode gives true
		named gives false
		unresolved gives false
		void gives false
		structRef gives false
		string gives false
		function gives false
		typeParameter gives false
		genericInstance gives false
		character gives false
		// An existential's VALUE half is an aggregate reference (every conformer shv2 admits is a declared
		// struct), so this tag admits one — `false`, with `structRef`'s reason.
		interfaceRef gives false
	end 'k'
end 'tagAdmitsNoAggregate'

// True for every tag that is DEFINITELY an integer — `int`, a ranged alias, `ExitCode`. It is the
// `numeric` class minus `float`, and it is the half a float cannot reach without dropping its
// fraction.
//
// A `match`, not `typeClassAdmits(numeric, …) and not tagIsFloat(…)`, and that is this file's own
// lesson applied: the composed form answers TRUE for `unresolved` (which agrees with everything by
// deferral) and would call `takeUnknown(3.7)` a narrowing — naming a conversion for a type nobody
// has pinned. `notOperatorFor` reintroduced a silent fall-through through a RANGE arm; naming
// every tag is the only spelling that cannot.
export function tagIsIntegral(tag ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	return match tag 'k'
		boolean gives false
		integer gives true
		float gives false
		// A `named` type is a ranged alias — as of F1 an `int(low to high)` OR a `float(low to high)`
		// one (`Parser.readTypeAliasDeclaration`). A float alias is NOT integral, so unlike `typeClassOf`
		// (where both aliases are numeric) this arm is no longer a theorem. It stays `true` deliberately:
		// a float-alias value carries a `named` tag ONLY in a param/field position, every one of which
		// F1 defers to the float ABI (F2), and the int reading here is what turns a float op on such a
		// value into a type error (or an x64 ABI panic) rather than a wrong answer — while keeping
		// `takeInt(x Integer)` an integer parameter. Reporting `false` for a float alias needs `named`
		// resolved through the alias registry, which is that F2 work.
		named gives true
		exitCode gives true
		// NOT integral, and this is the arm the composed spelling gets wrong: `unresolved` agrees
		// with everything, so it must not be mistaken for a type a float has to convert INTO.
		unresolved gives false
		void gives false
		// A struct is a pointer, and a pointer is not a number the language will convert anything
		// INTO. Nothing narrows to a struct, so this arm is never the target of a coercion — it is
		// `false` because the statement "a struct is an integer" is false, not because the site is
		// unreachable.
		structRef gives false
		// A String is a pointer, not a number the language will convert anything INTO. Nothing narrows
		// to a String, so `false` here is the statement "a String is not an integer".
		string gives false
		// A function value is a code pointer, not a number the language will convert anything INTO.
		// Nothing narrows to a function, so `false` here is the statement "a function is not an integer".
		function gives false
		// A type parameter is OPAQUE (P1.6-A): nothing narrows INTO a `T`, so `false` keeps a
		// `float`-into-`T` from reading as a lossy conversion (`checkDeclaredType`). A generic instance
		// is a struct pointer, not an integer.
		typeParameter gives false
		genericInstance gives false
		// A Character is a record pointer, not a magnitude — `string`'s answer, and what makes
		// `c shl 1` / `c and 3` the same rejection `s shl 1` gets.
		character gives false
		// An existential is a fat pointer, not a magnitude — `structRef`'s answer, and the statement
		// "an interface value is not an integer" rather than a claim about reachability.
		interfaceRef gives false
	end 'k'
end 'tagIsIntegral'

// The name a type prints under in a diagnostic: the RESOLVED PRIMITIVE, never the alias the
// source wrote. That is what both reference compilers print — the C# bootstrap reports a
// parameter declared `Integer` (a `typealias Integer = int(i64.min to i64.max)`) as
// `expected 'int'`, and specs/implicit-type-conversion.md's `no-bool-to-int` case gates that
// text byte-for-byte.
export function typeTagName(tag ValueTypeTag) returns String
	return match tag 'k'
		boolean gives "bool"
		integer gives "int"
		float gives "float"
		// Both render as `int`: `ExitCode` is an unsigned 32-bit ranged int, and a `named` alias is a
		// ranged int — or, as of F1, a ranged float, which this arm also prints as `int` (the same int
		// reading `tagIsIntegral`/`valueTagToStdType` give a `named` value in the param/field cases F1
		// defers to the float ABI at F2). The bootstrap agrees — it reports `return <bool>` from a
		// `returns ExitCode` function as "declared to return 'int'".
		named gives "int"
		exitCode gives "int"
		// A type the parser could not pin — today, only a call to a function no file declares
		// (see TypeClass). It AGREES with everything, so it is never the side that FAILS a rule —
		// but it can be the OTHER side of one (`f() + flag`, where the bool is what is wrong), and
		// that message still has to name it. "unknown" is the honest word: shv2 genuinely does not
		// know, and the program is being rejected anyway (E3004, at the call site).
		unresolved gives "unknown"
		// A call to a VOID function, used where a value belongs. `Parser.parseCall` rejects that at
		// the call site with the bootstrap's own text ("Function 'noop' does not return a value").
		// ⚠ The SECOND route this arm was written against has since appeared, so it is no longer
		// unreachable: `SemanticCheck.requireOverloadVoidResult` prints it for an overload set whose
		// members disagree about whether they return a value at all (W49 wave 4).
		void gives "void"
		// ⚠ It names the KIND, not the type: `return p` from an `ExitCode` function reports "got
		// 'struct'" where the bootstrap reports "got 'Point'". A tag is a scalar and carries no
		// name (see this file's header on why every rule is stated over one), so the name is not
		// here to print. It arrives with the `valueNameIds` publication that `TypeClass.structRef`
		// already names as its own fix — one change, two things it repairs.
		structRef gives "struct"
		// The `String` builtin. Both reference compilers print the builtin's name here (the bootstrap's
		// `E3005: operator '+' is not defined for type 'String'`), so a String type error names `String`.
		string gives "String"
		// A function value. `function` is what the spec's E3005 messages interpolate as `{got}` — "cannot
		// assign a value of type 'function' to …". A tag is a scalar and carries no name (see this file's
		// header), so it names the KIND, not a specific signature — which is all the type rule needs.
		function gives "function"
		// A type parameter and a generic instance (P1.6-A). A tag carries no name, so these name the
		// KIND: "type parameter" for an opaque `T`, "struct" for a generic instance (which IS a struct
		// pointer, matching what a plain `structRef` prints).
		typeParameter gives "type parameter"
		genericInstance gives "struct"
		// The `Character` builtin. Both reference compilers print the builtin's own name here
		// (`stdlib/Character.maxon` declares `type Character`), so a Character type error names it.
		character gives "Character"
		// ⚠ It names the KIND, not the interface — `structRef`'s limitation, for `structRef`'s reason: a tag
		// is a scalar and carries no name. The four widening doors do NOT print through here; each names the
		// interface off the declared type it holds (`Parser.interfaceNameOfType`), because "does not implement
		// interface 'Runnable'" is the message the corpus pins and it needs the name.
		interfaceRef gives "interface"
	end 'k'
end 'typeTagName'

// ⭐⭐ **THE LAST STEP OF EVERY DISPLAY RENDERER: a type's own NAME when it has one, else the class word its
// tag prints under.** Both tiers' display doors — `Parser.displayTypeNameOf` and
// `SemanticCheck.typeDisplayName` — end here, and it lives beside `typeTagName` because it is the rule for
// WHEN that function's answer is the right one to print.
//
// ⚠ **THE TWO TIERS' DISPLAY DOORS ARE STILL TWINS ABOVE THIS LINE, AND ONLY ABOVE IT.** What is genuinely
// tier-bound is the LOOKUP that produces `aggregate` (`Parser.aggregateNameOf` reads the file's interner,
// `SemanticCheck.aggregateNameFor` the whole-program one) and the `genericInstance` arm each takes first.
// This tail takes neither an interner nor a tier: it takes a tag and an already-resolved name, so a second
// copy of it would be a rule with two homes and nothing forcing it. It was exactly that for one tick.
//
// An aggregate meeting a SCALAR is a real conflict both tiers report, and both references name the scalar
// there ("got 'int'"), so an empty aggregate name must print the tag word and never a bare `''`.
export function displayNameOfAggregate(tag ValueTypeTag, aggregate ByteArray) returns String
	return typeTagName(tag) if aggregate.isEmpty() else String.from(aggregate)
end 'displayNameOfAggregate'

// --- The type-DIRECTED operators ---
//
// `and` / `or` / `xor` / `not` mean different things on a `bool` than on an `int`, and the
// PARSER is the tier that decides which: it is the only one holding an expression's
// source-level type, and (for `and`/`or`) the decision is CONTROL FLOW, which no later pass
// can retrofit. These are the rules that decision reads.

// ⭐ **WHAT A BOOL IS, AS A NUMBER.** A `bool` is an i1 carrying 1 or 0, and these are those two
// values. Every reader of the convention is below or beside this line:
//
//   • the `true` / `false` literals (`Parser.parseBoolLiteral`);
//   • the SEED a short-circuit merge takes on its skipped path (`Parser.shortCircuitSeed`) — `and`
//     seeds false, `or` seeds true, so "false" and "the value `a and b` has when `a` is false" are
//     ONE constant and cannot drift apart;
//   • the payload a folded comparison yields (`foldIntCompare`);
//   • the flip a folded logical `not` performs (`foldLogicalNot`).
//
// They live HERE rather than in the parser because this file is what already answers "is this a
// bool?" (`tagIsBool`, `typeClassOf`), and because the FOLDS below need them — TypeRules is the
// lower layer, so the constants had to move down to it rather than the rules reaching up into the
// parser for them.
export let BoolTrueValue = 1 as ParsedInt
export let BoolFalseValue = 0 as ParsedInt

// The 0/1 payload of a `bool` the compiler computed. The one place a Maxon `bool` becomes the
// number a `boolean`-tagged value carries, so the two constants above are never re-spelled at a
// fold site — nor at an EMIT site: the interpolation lowering hands `stdlib`'s formatted renderers
// four `bool` arguments it decided itself (`Parser.materializeFormattedIntPart`), and those are the
// same conversion rather than a second one.
export function boolPayload(value bool) returns ParsedInt
	if value 'true'
		return BoolTrueValue
	end 'true'
	return BoolFalseValue
end 'boolPayload'

// Fold `lhs <op> rhs` over two integers the compiler already holds, yielding the comparison's
// answer as the 0/1 payload a `boolean` value carries.
//
// It returns a `ParsedInt` rather than a `bool` because its caller — the top-level constant
// evaluator — carries every constant as a (payload, tag) pair, and a comparison's payload IS that
// 0/1. Handing back a `bool` would only make the caller convert it, and the conversion is where
// the convention would get written down a second time.
//
// TOTAL, unlike `foldIntBinOp`: no comparison can fail. There is no division to trap and no count
// to reject, so there is nothing to decline and no `FoldedInt` to return.
//
// ⚠ NOTHING ELSE IN THIS COMPILER FOLDS A COMPARISON — the parser emits `MaxonOp.compare` and
// leaves the answer to the hardware's `setcc`. So this is the FIRST statement of the rule, not a
// second one. The day the parser gains a comparison fold, it must call THIS rather than restate
// it, exactly as `emitShift` and `evalShift` share one shift rule.
export function foldIntCompare(op MaxonCmpOp, lhs ParsedInt, rhs ParsedInt) returns ParsedInt
	return match op 'o'
		eq gives boolPayload(lhs == rhs)
		ne gives boolPayload(lhs != rhs)
		lt gives boolPayload(lhs < rhs)
		gt gives boolPayload(lhs > rhs)
		le gives boolPayload(lhs <= rhs)
		ge gives boolPayload(lhs >= rhs)
	end 'o'
end 'foldIntCompare'

// Fold `lhs <op> rhs` over two float BIT PATTERNS with the host's native f64 — the float twin of
// `foldIntCompare`, and the ONE place a constant float comparison's answer is computed.
//
// ⚠ **IT MUST COMPARE AS f64, NOT OVER THE RAW PATTERNS**, and that is the whole reason it exists
// beside `foldIntCompare` rather than reusing it. A double's bit pattern is sign-and-magnitude: two
// POSITIVE doubles happen to order exactly as their patterns do, so an integer compare of `3.5 > 2.5`
// is right BY ACCIDENT — but a negative operand (whose sign bit inverts the integer order) or a NaN
// (which no integer compare can call unordered) exposes it. Reinterpreting to f64
// (`__Builtins.bitsToFloat`) and letting the host compare gets `-1.0 > -2.0` and `-0.0 == +0.0` right.
//
// Both operands are float here: a mixed int/float compare is E3005 (`comparableOperands`), so the
// caller reaches this only when both sides carry float bits.
export function foldFloatCompare(op MaxonCmpOp, lhs ParsedInt, rhs ParsedInt) returns ParsedInt
	let a = __Builtins.bitsToFloat(lhs)
	let b = __Builtins.bitsToFloat(rhs)

	return match op 'o'
		eq gives boolPayload(a == b)
		ne gives boolPayload(a != b)
		lt gives boolPayload(a < b)
		gt gives boolPayload(a > b)
		le gives boolPayload(a <= b)
		ge gives boolPayload(a >= b)
	end 'o'
end 'foldFloatCompare'

// Fold a LOGICAL `not` over the 0/1 payload of a bool the compiler already holds.
//
// Separate from `foldIntUnaryOp`, which declines `logicalNot` outright — and the split is the
// rule, not an accident. That function folds over INTEGERS, where a `logicalNot` is a parser bug
// (`not 5` is the bitwise complement, `bitNot`); this one folds over the BOOL domain, where the
// operator is meaningful and total. `notOperatorFor` is what tells the two apart, and every caller
// reaches these through it rather than choosing for itself.
export function foldLogicalNot(operand ParsedInt) returns ParsedInt
	return boolPayload(operand == BoolFalseValue)
end 'foldLogicalNot'

// True for the tag of a `bool`, and ONLY for it. Distinct from
// `typeClassAdmits(TypeClass.boolean, …)`, and the difference is load-bearing: this asks "is
// this DEFINITELY a bool?", which is what the short-circuit gate and the `not` opcode choice
// need (an `unresolved` operand must NOT short-circuit, and must not be guessed at), while
// `typeClassAdmits` asks "is this ALLOWED to be a bool?", which is what a CONDITION needs (an
// `unresolved` condition is deferred, not rejected). Two questions, deliberately not one.
export function tagIsBool(tag ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	return tag == ValueTypeTag.boolean
end 'tagIsBool'

// True when a type could not be pinned at parse time — see TypeClass's `unknown`.
export function tagIsUnresolved(tag ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	return tag == ValueTypeTag.unresolved
end 'tagIsUnresolved'

// True for the tag of an `int`, and ONLY for it. The gate on the parser's constant folder: it
// folds INTEGERS, and asking "is this definitely an int?" is the same DEFINITELY question
// `tagIsBool` asks — an `unresolved` operand must not be guessed at and must not be folded.
export function tagIsInteger(tag ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	return tag == ValueTypeTag.integer
end 'tagIsInteger'

// ⭐⭐ **AN INTEGER CARRYING NO ALIAS IDENTITY — a bare `int` or an `ExitCode`, and ONLY those two.** The
// question every door asks when it wants "a plain machine integer" rather than "something integral": it is
// `tagIsIntegral` MINUS `named`, and that subtraction is the whole point. A `named` value is a ranged alias
// whose NAME is part of its type (`Array with Score` is not `Array with int`), so a door that means the
// plain integer must not absorb it.
//
// ⚠ **IT EXISTS BECAUSE THE SET WAS ALREADY WRITTEN DOWN TWICE, IN TWO SPELLINGS** —
// `Parser.enumBackingFieldDomainOf` (`resolved == ValueTypeTag.integer or resolved == ValueTypeTag.exitCode`)
// and `Parser.giveIsPromotableToFloat` (`tagIsInteger(tag) or tag == ValueTypeTag.exitCode`), each labelling
// its branch `integerDomain`/`plainInteger` for the same fact. `literalColumnTypeFor` needed it as a THIRD,
// which is the point at which one fact with several writings becomes this repo's signature bug rather than a
// coincidence.
//
// ⚠ **NOT `tagIsIntegral`, AND THAT IS MEASURED, NOT STYLISTIC.** That predicate answers TRUE for `named`,
// and a literal's element column reads a `named` element through `namedElementType` to keep the alias's
// identity (`[0 as Q, 1 as Q]` is an `Array with Q`, not an `Array with int` — see
// `Parser.arrayLiteralInstanceFor`'s ⭐ block and the packing ladder it turns on). Folding `named` in here
// would silently retype every ranged-alias literal.
//
// ⚠ **THE `boolean`-INCLUSIVE SET IS A DIFFERENT ONE AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT THIS FUNCTION.** Three sites
// spell `integer or boolean or exitCode` (`Parser.matchScrutineeIsPlainInteger`-shaped arms and
// `SemanticCheck`); they are asking "does this lower to a GPR", not "is this an integer", and merging the two
// would put a `bool` where an arithmetic domain is meant.
export function tagIsPlainInteger(tag ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	return tagIsInteger(tag) or tag == ValueTypeTag.exitCode
end 'tagIsPlainInteger'

// True for the tag of a `float`, and ONLY for it — the same DEFINITELY question `tagIsBool` and
// `tagIsInteger` ask, for the type whose operations are a different INSTRUCTION SET rather than a
// different reading of the same bits.
//
// ⚠ It is deliberately NOT `typeClassAdmits(TypeClass.numeric, …)`, and the two must never be
// confused: a float and an int share the `numeric` CLASS (that is what makes `5 + 2.0` a legal
// program rather than a type error), and share nothing at all in the hardware. The class answers
// "may these operands meet?"; this answers "which instruction does the meeting?".
export function tagIsFloat(tag ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	return tag == ValueTypeTag.float
end 'tagIsFloat'

// ⭐ **THE TAGS A BARE NUMERIC KEYWORD IS MINTED UNDER** — `int` and `float`, the two spellings
// `Parser.parseTypeReference` answers with a CONCRETE numeric arm rather than a `named` one. The
// shv2 twin of the oracle's `IrType.IsBarePrimitive`, which likewise names only the numerics.
//
// ⚠ **`boolean` IS DELIBERATELY OUT.** A `bool` is minted concrete by the very same routine, so
// "was this spelled as a keyword?" would sweep it in — but it is already a constrained type with no
// range to declare, so the ranged-typealias rule this set exists to enforce has nothing to ask of
// it. That exclusion is why this is an enumerated SET rather than the absence of some other tag.
//
// It is stated ONCE because the rule it feeds is decided against a SECOND rule that overlaps it: a
// bare `float` is in this set AND is a float, and `Parser.checkGenericArgType` resolves that overlap
// by ORDER. Were each site to spell its own subset, the site that had already subtracted the overlap
// would look identical to the site that had never known about it.
export function tagIsBareNumericKeyword(tag ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	return tagIsInteger(tag) or tagIsFloat(tag)
end 'tagIsBareNumericKeyword'

// ⭐ **DOES A BINDING OF THIS TAG OWN NO HEAP?** — asked by `Parser.requireBindingLive` of a MOVE bit, and
// the whole of what it means there: `movedFrom`/`partiallyMoved` forbid a read because a moved HEAP value
// now belongs to another owner, so on a value that owns no heap the bit must mean something else and the
// read is safe. Two such cases exist, both P1.5-B2 #88 — a consumed promise handle (integer-tagged) and a
// promise binding reassigned to a bare scalar.
//
// ⚠ **IT WAS `tagIsAsyncScalar`, AND THE RENAME IS THE POINT.** Under that name it was the async CHANNEL
// predicate, shared by the argument, parameter and result doors — and its header said so. A2 replaced all
// three with `tagRidesAsyncGprChannel` (a managed pointer rides the integer channel; a `float` does not),
// leaving one caller that had only ever spelled its own question in the async predicate's words because the
// two sets happened to coincide. Left named for the callers it no longer has, it was a header asserting a
// property nothing tested — and the next reader to "reunify" the two would have handed the async doors a
// rule about move bits.
export function tagOwnsNoHeapValue(tag ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	return tag == ValueTypeTag.integer or tag == ValueTypeTag.boolean or tag == ValueTypeTag.float
end 'tagOwnsNoHeapValue'

// ⭐⭐ **DOES THIS TAG RIDE THE GREEN THREAD'S GPR CHANNEL? — the A2 widening of the question above, and the
// question the three async doors actually ask.** A spawn's arguments travel in the GT struct's inline
// argument region (`GtOffArgInline`, i64 words the hand-assembled trampoline loads into the ABI's INTEGER
// argument registers) and its result comes back in R8. **A managed value is a heap POINTER and is therefore
// one i64 in a GPR exactly as an `int` is** — the register file was never the obstacle for it; its ownership
// was, and `Runtime/AsyncReleaser.maxon` is where that is discharged.
//
// ⛔ **A `float` IS STILL REFUSED, AND THAT IS A REGISTER-FILE FACT RATHER THAN AN OWNERSHIP ONE.** A float
// argument is passed in an XMM register (`argFpRegisterAt`) and a float result is returned in **XMM0**
// (`indirectResultRegister`), and the trampoline touches neither: it loads every slot into
// `argRegisterAt(k)` and stores R8 alone into `gt.result` (`X64GtRuntime.buildGtTrampolineChunk`). Admitting
// a float here would hand the callee an XMM register nobody filled and read a result nobody captured — a
// silent wrong answer, not a leak. Building the second channel is a change to the hand-assembled trampoline
// and to `__gt_await`'s single i64 exit, which is a different slice; no spec case in the tree spawns or
// awaits a float.
//
// ⛔ **AN EXISTENTIAL AND AN OPAQUE TYPE PARAMETER ARE REFUSED FOR A THIRD REASON: THEY ARE ONE WORD TOO
// NARROW.** Each is released through a TWO-argument entry whose first argument is a companion the GT struct
// does not carry — `__drop_existential(witness, value)` off the fat pointer's witness half,
// `__drop_type_param(descriptor, value)` off the enclosing instance's layout descriptor. The inline argument
// region holds the value half only, so a releaser could name no drop for them (`managedFieldDropCallee`
// PANICS on `interfaceRef` for exactly this reason). Carrying the companion is a second word per slot and a
// later slice.
//
// ⚠ Written as an exhaustive `match` and not as `tag != float`: the negative form FAILS OPEN — a tag
// appended to `ValueTypeTag` (four have been, each "APPENDED AT THE END") would be admitted to a channel
// nobody had asked whether it fits. Here it is a compile error until someone decides.
export function tagRidesAsyncGprChannel(tag ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	return match tag 'k'
		boolean gives true
		integer gives true
		float gives false
		// `named` covers a ranged-int alias (a GPR scalar), a declared struct read from a field and a boxed
		// union — a heap pointer in each of the last two. All three are one GPR word.
		named gives true
		exitCode gives true
		// `unresolved` is the placeholder an undeclared callee's return type gets; its doomed program is
		// rejected by SemanticCheck's E3004 before lowering, so admitting it here defers to the diagnostic
		// that names the real fault instead of blaming the channel.
		unresolved gives true
		// `void` is not a value and never reaches an argument slot; the RESULT door tests it separately
		// (a void async's R8 garbage is discarded by the awaiter) rather than through this predicate.
		void gives false
		structRef gives true
		string gives true
		// A function value is a bare `.text` address — one word, and it owns nothing.
		function gives true
		typeParameter gives false
		genericInstance gives true
		character gives true
		interfaceRef gives false
	end 'k'
end 'tagRidesAsyncGprChannel'

// The three operators whose MEANING follows their operands' type. `shl`/`shr` are absent: a
// shift is integral by definition, on both sides.
function wordOperator(op MaxonBinOp) returns bool
	return match op 'k'
		add to mod gives false
		bitAnd to bitXor gives true
		shl to shrLogical gives false
		// `min`/`max` are BUILTIN CALLS, not word operators: their meaning does not follow their
		// operands' type (both are float-only, and both operands are promoted before the op exists), and
		// neither has a bool reading to be the other half of a duality.
		min to max gives false
		// The unsigned divisions are divisions: arithmetic, with no bool reading, exactly as `div`/`mod`.
		divUnsigned to modUnsigned gives false
	end 'k'
end 'wordOperator'

// The two operators whose RIGHT operand is not an ordinary number but a shift DISTANCE, and so
// the two the shift rule below governs.
export function shiftOperator(op MaxonBinOp) returns bool
	return match op 'k'
		add to bitXor gives false
		shl to shrLogical gives true
		// `min`/`max` take a second operand that is an ordinary number, not a shift DISTANCE — both
		// sides are values to compare, and both are promoted to float.
		min to max gives false
		// A DIVISOR is an ordinary number too, whatever the divide's signedness.
		divUnsigned to modUnsigned gives false
	end 'k'
end 'shiftOperator'

// ⭐ **THE TWO OPERATORS WHOSE DIVISOR MIGHT BE ZERO** — and so the two the fallible-division rule (A1)
// governs. `Parser.emitDivOrMod` is the one emitter behind this gate, exactly as `emitShift` is behind
// `shiftOperator`, and for the same reason: `/` and `mod` have a LEGALITY question (`a / 0` is E3103) and a
// FALLIBILITY question (a possibly-zero divisor throws) that no other arithmetic operator has, so they get
// their own emitter rather than an extra branch inside `emitBinOp`.
//
// ⚠ Float `mod` does not exist (`requireBinOpOperands` refuses it), so `remainder` is integer-only — but the
// gate is over the OPERATOR, not the operand type, and both operators reach it in both domains before the
// operand rule has spoken.
export function divisionOperator(op MaxonBinOp) returns bool
	return match op 'k'
		add to mul gives false
		div to mod gives true
		bitAnd to shrLogical gives false
		// `min`/`max` divide nothing, so neither has a legality nor a fallibility question — which is
		// also what lets `emitBinaryMathIntrinsic` reach the bare emitter past `emitBinOp`'s
		// division panic.
		min to max gives false
		// The UNSIGNED pair divides too, and every rule this gate guards is about the DIVISOR, which
		// signedness does not change: `a / 0` is still E3103 and a possibly-zero divisor still throws.
		divUnsigned to modUnsigned gives true
	end 'k'
end 'divisionOperator'

// ⭐ **DOES THIS DIVISION OPERATOR READ A REMAINDER?** — `mod` and its unsigned twin, against the two
// quotients. The QUOTIENT/REMAINDER split and the SIGNED/UNSIGNED split are two independent questions
// about one operator, and every reader wants exactly one of them; asking each through its own
// predicate is what stops a four-way match being restated at each site with a chance to differ.
function divisionReadsRemainder(op MaxonBinOp) returns bool
	return match op 'k'
		add to div gives false
		mod gives true
		bitAnd to max gives false
		divUnsigned gives false
		modUnsigned gives true
	end 'k'
end 'divisionReadsRemainder'

// ⭐⭐ **IS THIS DIVISION UNSIGNED?** The opcode already IS the decision — asked once, of BOTH operands,
// and written into it by `divisionOpcodeFor` — so the fold, the `-1` overflow gate and the Std lowering
// each read one fact rather than three re-derivations of one rule. `evalShift` reads `shr` vs
// `shrLogical` the same way and says the same thing about why.
function divisionIsUnsigned(op MaxonBinOp) returns bool
	return match op 'k'
		add to max gives false
		divUnsigned to modUnsigned gives true
	end 'k'
end 'divisionIsUnsigned'

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT ONE INTEGER VALUE'S DECLARED TYPE SAYS ABOUT ITS SIGNEDNESS** — three answers, and
// the middle one is the whole reason this is not a boolean.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS A PROPERTY OF THE VALUE, NOT OF THE OPERATION, AND ITS NAME SAYS SO ONLY SINCE X11.** It
// was named for the division operand while the divide was its only consumer; the DECIMAL RENDERER
// (`Parser.materializeInterpExpr`) is the second, and it asks the identical question — *does this
// value's declared type admit a negative?* — of the identical walk. A second spelling of that
// question is exactly what this family has cost three times over (`X8` the range, `X10` the divide,
// `X11` the rendering), so the noun is the value's and both consumers read it.
export enum IntegerValueDomain
	// A DECLARED ranged type whose every value is non-negative (`rangeIsUnsigned`) — `int(0 to u64.max)`,
	// `int(1 to 8)`, `ExitCode`. The only answer that ASKS for an unsigned reading.
	declaredNonNegative
	// No declared range, but a folded constant that happens to be non-negative. It FITS an unsigned
	// reading and asks for nothing, which is exactly the distinction that keeps `w / 8` unsigned (`w`
	// declares it) while `7 / 2` — two constants and no declared type anywhere — stays the signed
	// divide this compiler already emits, buying no codegen movement for no correctness.
	provablyNonNegative
	// Everything else, and they are ONE answer because they license the same instruction: a declared
	// range admitting negatives, a negative constant, and — the case that reads as "no constraint" and
	// is not — a value with NO range at all, which means THE WHOLE OF i64.
	signed
end 'IntegerValueDomain'

// ⭐⭐ **DOES A LONE INTEGER VALUE RENDER AS UNSIGNED DECIMAL?** — the ONE-operand twin of the
// division rule below, stated here beside it because they are the same question asked of a different
// arity and must not come to disagree about the middle answer.
//
// ⚠ **`provablyNonNegative` RENDERS SIGNED, AND THAT IS THE RULE RATHER THAN A CONSERVATISM.** A
// folded `42` FITS an unsigned rendering and does not ASK for one, exactly as it does not ask for an
// unsigned divide — and because the two renderings agree on every value that fits an `i64`, reading
// it the other way would buy no correctness at all while moving the emitted converter at every
// `"{7 + 1}"` in the corpus. Only a DECLARED type asks.
export function valueRendersUnsigned(domain IntegerValueDomain) returns bool
	return domain == IntegerValueDomain.declaredNonNegative
end 'valueRendersUnsigned'

// ⭐⭐ **THE DIVISION SIGNEDNESS RULE: A `/` OR `mod` RUNS AT A SIGNEDNESS VALID FOR BOTH OPERANDS.**
//
// A ranged typealias on ONE operand may not decide the arithmetic done to the OTHER. Every other
// integer operator survives being told otherwise, because a narrowed integer binop is emitted at 64
// bits on every backend shv2 has; the divide family is the one whose width and signedness are REAL in
// the instruction, so it is the one family a one-sided reading reaches.
//
// ⚠ **NO RANGED TYPE DOES NOT MEAN "NO CONSTRAINT" — IT MEANS THE WHOLE OF `i64`**, and that is the
// half `IntegerValueDomain.signed` exists to state. Reading it as "no constraint" is what let the
// BOOTSTRAP narrow a 64-bit dividend into a 32-bit divide from the divisor's `int(1 to 8)` (X8), and
// the mirror of the same question is what left shv2 emitting a SIGNED `idiv` for two operands both
// declared non-negative: `u64.max / 3` answered 0, because `0xFFFF…FF` read as `-1`.
//
// ⭐ **A DECLARED RANGE IS WHAT ASKS FOR THE UNSIGNED DIVIDE; A FOLDED CONSTANT ONLY HAS TO FIT ONE.**
// Two non-negative constants would license an unsigned divide on their own, and `7 / 2` naming no
// ranged type anywhere is precisely the division this rule has nothing to say about. This is the
// bootstrap's rule (`2-Parser.cs`'s `DivisionOptimalType` / `UnionIntRange`, X8) with its WIDTH half
// dropped, and the drop is a fact about shv2 rather than a simplification: shv2 emits every integer
// divide at 64 bits — a narrow value sits extended in its register and its own type's range holds it —
// so the union's WIDTH has nothing to choose and only its SIGNEDNESS does. The two compilers therefore
// pick the same instruction from the same two ranges.
export function divisionOperandsAreUnsigned(dividend IntegerValueDomain, divisor IntegerValueDomain) returns bool
	return operandPairAsksForUnsigned(dividend, right: divisor)
end 'divisionOperandsAreUnsigned'

// ⭐⭐ **THE ORDERING-COMPARISON SIGNEDNESS RULE: `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=` RUN AT A SIGNEDNESS VALID FOR BOTH
// OPERANDS** — the DIVIDE's rule (above) asked of the second family whose answer the reading changes.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS THE SAME RULE AND THE SAME PAIR PREDICATE, DELIBERATELY.** `w < x` and `w / x` are refused
// an unsigned reading by the same operand and licensed by the same one, and two spellings of that would
// be free to disagree about the middle domain — which is the whole reason `IntegerValueDomain` has three
// answers rather than two. So this is a NAME for the family it governs, over one shared body.
//
// ⚠⚠ **`==`/`!=` ARE NOT HERE, AND THAT IS A FACT ABOUT THE INSTRUCTION RATHER THAN A NARROWING.**
// `cmp` writes the same ZF for the same two bit patterns however you read them, and `je`/`jne` read only
// ZF — so equality is ALREADY correct at every value, in both compilers, and routing it through this
// would move thousands of goldens to emit the identical instruction. The four ORDERING codes are the
// ones that read OF/SF (signed) or CF (unsigned) and therefore answer differently: MEASURED before this
// rule, with `big` and `three` both declared `int(0 to u64.max)` and `big` holding `u64.max`,
// `three < big` answered **false** in BOTH compilers — `jl` reading `0xFFFF…FF` as `-1`.
//
// ⚠ **THE PAIR IS WHAT MAKES IT SOUND, and a one-sided reading is the bug the BOOTSTRAP had.** Its gate
// excluded comparisons from an optimal type entirely (`2-Parser.cs`), and the arm it would otherwise have
// fallen into takes *"the FIRST ranged type either operand offers"* — a reading whose own comment
// licenses it only for `+`/`-`/`*`/the bitwise family, *"because the choice cannot change the answer"*.
// For an ordering compare it changes the answer, so a one-sided type would let a `int(0 to 100)` operand
// impose an unsigned reading on a plain `int` that admits negatives.
export function comparisonOperandsAreUnsigned(left IntegerValueDomain, right IntegerValueDomain) returns bool
	return operandPairAsksForUnsigned(left, right: right)
end 'comparisonOperandsAreUnsigned'

// ⭐ **THE SHARED BODY OF BOTH RULES ABOVE — asked of a PAIR, answered from the three domains.** Two
// clauses, and each is one of the two halves `IntegerValueDomain`'s middle answer exists to separate:
// a `signed` operand REFUSES the unsigned reading (its negatives would read as huge), and a
// `declaredNonNegative` one ASKS for it (its declaration is the only thing that can). A pair of
// `provablyNonNegative` constants neither refuses nor asks: both readings answer identically for them,
// so the rule has nothing to say and the existing signed instruction stands — which is what keeps
// `7 / 2` and `3 < 4` emitting exactly the code they emitted before either rule existed.
function operandPairAsksForUnsigned(left IntegerValueDomain, right IntegerValueDomain) returns bool
	if left == IntegerValueDomain.signed or right == IntegerValueDomain.signed 'eitherAdmitsANegative'
		return false
	end 'eitherAdmitsANegative'

	return left == IntegerValueDomain.declaredNonNegative or right == IntegerValueDomain.declaredNonNegative
end 'operandPairAsksForUnsigned'

// ⭐ **IS THIS SOURCE COMPARISON ONE OF THE FOUR WHOSE ANSWER SIGNEDNESS CHANGES?** — the operator half
// of the rule above, stated here beside it so the two cannot come to disagree about which operators the
// rule governs. `==`/`!=` read ZF alone and are identical in both readings; see the ⚠⚠ note.
export function comparisonReadsOrdering(op MaxonCmpOp) returns bool
	return match op 'o'
		eq gives false
		ne gives false
		lt to ge gives true
	end 'o'
end 'comparisonReadsOrdering'

// The opcode a SOURCE `/` or `mod` emits once its signedness is known — the DIVISION twin of
// `shiftOpcodeFor`, and here for that function's reason: `infixMaxonOp` maps `/` to `MaxonBinOp.div`
// unconditionally (it has no operands to look at), so this is where a `divUnsigned` is ever born and
// `Parser.emitDivOrMod` is its only caller.
export function divisionOpcodeFor(op MaxonBinOp, unsigned bool) returns MaxonBinOp
	if not unsigned 'keepsTheSignedReading'
		return op
	end 'keepsTheSignedReading'

	if op == MaxonBinOp.div 'quotient'
		return MaxonBinOp.divUnsigned
	end 'quotient'

	if op == MaxonBinOp.mod 'remainder'
		return MaxonBinOp.modUnsigned
	end 'remainder'

	panic("divisionOpcodeFor: {op.rawValue} is not a SOURCE division operator — only `/` and `mod` reach here from `infixMaxonOp`, and the unsigned pair is this function's OUTPUT, never its input")
end 'divisionOpcodeFor'

// ⭐⭐ **CAN A VALUE OF THIS RANGE BE ZERO?** — the type-level half of the fallible-division rule (A1), and
// the reason `int(1 to 1000)` is the language's escape hatch from a checked divide. A range excludes 0 iff it
// lies WHOLLY above it or WHOLLY below it; anything spanning 0 admits it and its divides are throwing.
//
// ⚠ **THE `low < 0` GUARD IS WHAT KEEPS THE UNSIGNED QUIRK OUT OF THE NEGATIVE ARM**, and it is load-bearing
// rather than defensive. `int(0 to u64.max)` rides with `high == -1` (the u64.max PATTERN, see
// `rangeIsUnsignedMaxUpper`), so a bare `high < 0` would read it as "wholly negative" and elide the guard on
// a range that plainly contains 0. Its `low == 0` never reaches that test, so it reads — correctly — as
// admitting zero. The mixed shape that WOULD defeat the guard (`int(-10 to u64.max)`, indistinguishable in
// this representation from `int(-10 to -1)`) is unrepresentable by construction:
// `Parser.requireRepresentableRange` rejects a negative low with a `u64.max` upper outright.
// The bootstrap's predicate is the same expression for the same reason (`2-Parser.cs:23661-23689`).
//
// FLOAT: the bounds are IEEE-754 patterns, so the comparison goes through `floatOrderKey` — which maps both
// `+0.0` and `-0.0` to 0, so a range whose bound IS a zero of either sign admits zero, which is what a
// divisor test needs. Bounds are INCLUSIVE in both domains (`float(… upto …)` is refused outright), so there
// is no exclusive-upper adjustment on either arm.
export function rangeExcludesZero(low ParsedInt, high ParsedInt, isFloat bool) returns bool
	if isFloat 'float'
		return floatOrderKey(low) > 0 or floatOrderKey(high) < 0
	end 'float'

	return low > 0 or (low < 0 and high < 0)
end 'rangeExcludesZero'

// ⭐⭐ **CAN A VALUE OF THIS RANGE BE `-1`?** — `idiv`'s SECOND hazard, and the type-level half of the
// `mod` overflow rule (A1x), sitting beside the zero test because a division's divisor is asked BOTH.
// `x64`'s `idiv` raises `#DE` for two unrelated reasons, and the fallible-division rule only ever
// covered one of them: `int(i64.min to -1)` excludes 0 — so it earns a BARE divide — and is `-1` on
// every value it admits, which is exactly the pair that faulted. Wholly above `-1` or wholly below it
// is the same shape of answer `rangeExcludesZero` gives, one bound over.
//
// ⚠ **INTEGER ONLY, and that is the LANGUAGE's restriction rather than an omission**: float `mod` does
// not exist (`requireBinOpOperands` refuses it), and float `/` cannot fault at all — `divsd` answers
// `±inf`, which is why the zero test needs a float arm and this one has nothing to say. Its callers ask
// it only on the integer path.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE UNSIGNED-MAX-UPPER SHAPE MUST ANSWER `false`, AND A NAIVE `low > -1` WOULD SAY `true`.**
// `int(0 to u64.max)` rides with `high == UnsignedFullUpperPattern` — the `-1` PATTERN — so it admits
// the bit pattern `-1` as the huge unsigned `u64.max`, and a signed `idiv` reads that pattern as `-1`
// and faults on it. `low == 0` would otherwise place the range "wholly above -1" and elide the guard on
// the one range that most plainly contains the hazard. It is the exact dual of the `low < 0` guard in
// `rangeExcludesZero`, and it is load-bearing for the same reason: the representation, not the range,
// is what makes the naive test wrong. `rangeIsUnsignedMaxUpper` is the ONE place that shape is
// recognized, so this asks it rather than restating it.
export function rangeExcludesNegativeOne(low ParsedInt, high ParsedInt) returns bool
	if rangeIsUnsignedMaxUpper(low, high: high) 'admitsTheMinusOnePattern'
		return false
	end 'admitsTheMinusOnePattern'

	return low > OverflowingDivisorPattern or high < OverflowingDivisorPattern
end 'rangeExcludesNegativeOne'

// ⭐ THE SHIFT RULE — THE CANONICAL STATEMENT OF WHAT `shl`/`shr` MEAN IN THIS COMPILER, and the
// only one. Four readers, and none of them restates it:
//
//   • the PARSER folds a count it can see, rejects a NEGATIVE one (E2054 — `Parser.emitShift`),
//     and rewrites a count of 64-or-more into the shift it actually names;
//   • the PARSER again, for a count it CANNOT see: it emits the guard (`Parser.emitGuardedShift`)
//     that saturates the count at run time, because the hardware would mask it;
//   • `foldConstOperands` folds a shift immediate only when the instruction takes it as written
//     (`constFitsBinOpImmediate` → `shiftCountIsUnguarded`);
//   • the x64 lowering types its imm8 shift operand by the same bound (TargetDialect's
//     `ShiftCount`).
//
// Maxon follows **Go**, whose rule is deliberately NOT the hardware's:
//
//   • **THE COUNT IS NOT MASKED.** "There is no upper limit on the shift count. Shifts behave as
//     if the left operand is shifted n times by 1 for a shift count of n." So `x shl 64` is 0 —
//     every bit has been shifted out — and so is `x shl 100`. The hardware disagrees: x64 masks a
//     64-bit shift's count to its low 6 bits, so `x shl 64` computes `x shl 0` (x, UNCHANGED) and
//     `x shl 100` computes `x shl 36`. Fighting that masking is the whole content of this rule.
//
//   • **THE LEFT OPERAND'S TYPE DECIDES HOW A RIGHT SHIFT FILLS.** Go: "the shift operators
//     implement arithmetic shifts if the left operand is a signed integer and logical shifts if
//     it is an unsigned integer". A shift is NOT symmetric in its operands — the right one is a
//     DISTANCE, and its type says nothing about anything (a count declared `int(0 to 63)` is the
//     most natural way there is to write one, and it made the BOOTSTRAP zero-fill a signed
//     `shr`). Only the value being SHIFTED decides.
//
//     **And Maxon HAS an unsigned type: a ranged alias whose LOW BOUND IS 0.** `int(0 to u64.max)`
//     is Maxon's `uint64` — it holds values whose bit 63 is set while the type calls them
//     non-negative — so `u64.max shr 60` is **15**, not -1. `rangeIsUnsigned` is the one place
//     that is decided, and `shiftFillsWithSign` the one place it is applied.
//
//     Every other range answers the same either way, and that is worth knowing rather than
//     relying on: a value of `int(0 to 100)` is provably non-negative, so an arithmetic and a
//     logical shift of it agree bit for bit. `int(0 to u64.max)` is the ONLY range on which the
//     two readings can be told apart — which is exactly why it went unnoticed, and why the spec
//     case that pins it must use it.
//
//     It was written down TWICE and the two copies disagreed: this comment used to say "every
//     Maxon int is signed — there is no unsigned integer type", while `Parser.parseTypeAlias`
//     accepted `int(0 to u64.max)` and the bootstrap zero-filled it. shv2 answered -1 and the
//     bootstrap 15, for the same program.
//
//   • **A NEGATIVE COUNT IS AN ERROR**, never a shift the other way — masked, `a shl -1` silently
//     computed `a shl 63`, the MAXIMUM LEFT shift, a wrong answer with the opposite sign. A
//     constant one is E2054; a runtime one panics in Go (see `Parser.emitGuardedShift` for why
//     shv2 cannot panic yet, and what it does instead).
//
// It lives HERE, among the source-level operand rules, because the shift's meaning is a
// FRONT-END fact. The lower tiers ask for it rather than re-deciding it — which is the only thing
// that keeps the count the parser accepts and the count the backend can encode from drifting.
export let MinShiftCount = 0 as ParsedInt

// The width of the value being shifted. A count of `ShiftCountBits` or more shifts every bit out,
// which is LEGAL and well-defined. It is not the top of a legal range — there is no top.
export let ShiftCountBits = 64 as ParsedInt

// The largest count the shift INSTRUCTION distinguishes, and so the largest that may reach an
// unguarded `shl`/`sar`. Anything above it must be saturated by the compiler, never handed to the
// hardware, which would mask it back into range.
//
// ⭐ DERIVED, NOT SPELLED. It was a literal `63` beside a literal `64`, which is ONE hardware fact —
// the operand width — written down twice with nothing making the copies agree. The two are not
// independently choosable: the hardware masks a count to `ShiftCountBits - 1`, so any other value
// here admits a count the mask would fold back into range, which is the `a shl -1 → a shl 63` class
// of silent wrong answer E2054 exists to close. Stating the width once and subtracting makes that
// drift UNREPRESENTABLE rather than merely asserted — so THESE TWO need no consistency check, and
// none is written. (The bootstrap's constant evaluator folds a top-level `let` from another
// top-level `let`; it is only `.rawValue` it declines, which is why `RegBits.FirstXmmRegisterNumber`
// must stay a literal and take an assert instead.)
//
// ⚠ **BUT THE 63 IS STILL WRITTEN ONE MORE TIME, AND THAT COPY IS UNREACHABLE FROM HERE**:
// `TargetDialect.ShiftCount` is `int(0 to 63)`. It is a RANGE BOUND, so it cannot name this constant
// (E2010) and no assert can read it — the same dead end `RegBits.RegNum` documents. It is the FORCED
// restatement, it says so itself, and it must move with this line. **Do not read "unrepresentable"
// above as "there is only one copy":** it means the `63`/`64` pair here cannot disagree. The Target
// tier's copy is a third number and it moves BY HAND.
export let MaxUnguardedShiftCount = (ShiftCountBits - 1) as ParsedInt

// The one count that is not a shift at all.
export function shiftCountIsNegative(count ParsedInt) returns bool
	return count < MinShiftCount
end 'shiftCountIsNegative'

// True iff the instruction may be handed this count DIRECTLY — it is non-negative, and the
// hardware's mask would be a no-op on it. Every other count needs the guard.
export function shiftCountIsUnguarded(count ParsedInt) returns bool
	return count >= MinShiftCount and count <= MaxUnguardedShiftCount
end 'shiftCountIsUnguarded'

// **IS THIS RANGED TYPE UNSIGNED?** THE one place that is answered, for the whole compiler.
//
// A range whose low bound is 0 or above describes only non-negative values, which is what
// "unsigned" means. The bootstrap decides it the same way and from the same fact — its
// `ComputeOptimalIntType` picks a U8/U16/U32/U64 storage type exactly when `lower >= 0` — so the
// two compilers cannot disagree about which types zero-fill.
//
// It takes the LOW bound alone, and never the high one: `int(0 to 100)` and `int(0 to u64.max)`
// are both unsigned, and the difference between them is a WIDTH question that no rule in this
// file asks.
export function rangeIsUnsigned(low ParsedInt) returns bool
	return low >= 0
end 'rangeIsUnsigned'

// --- Ranged-typealias narrowing checks (P1.9) ---
//
// A ranged alias's bounds are stored INCLUSIVE as i64 bit patterns on `RangedTypeAlias`
// (`upto` normalized to `high - 1` at parse; an unsigned upper such as `u64.max` rides as
// its signed pattern `-1`). The predicates below decide, from those two i64s alone,
// whether a NARROWING conversion (`as` cast / ranged `return`) needs a runtime guard and
// which bounds it must test — mirroring the bootstrap's `EmitRuntimeRangeCheck`
// (2-Parser.cs:18108-18127).
//
// ⚠ SIGNED COMPARES, in a CASCADE, and that is a shv2 constraint, not a design choice: the
// Std/Maxon tiers carry ONLY signed integer compares (`StdCmpPred` has no `ugt`/`ult`) and
// there is no `setcc`, so `InsertRangeChecks` emits a CASCADE of fused signed compare+branch —
// one block per needed bound, each `cmp` fused with its `condBranch` — never the bootstrap's
// `cmp`+`cmp`+`or` over materialized bools. For every range the enabled corpus uses, and every
// range with a `0` (or negative) low bound, a value fails the signed lower/upper check exactly
// where its unsigned image would, so the verdict is the same.
//
// ⭐ THE UNSIGNED-MAX UPPER SHAPE — `int(N>0 to u64.max)` — IS THE ONE RANGE SIGNED COMPARES
// CANNOT EXPRESS DIRECTLY, and it is handled by a two-check cascade rather than left as a gap.
// Its upper rides as `-1`, so `rangeNeedsUpperCheck` elides the upper and a naive single lower
// check `value < N` (signed) wrongly REJECTS a bit-63-set value that is unsigned-in-range
// (`1 shl 63 as int(5 to u64.max)` is a HUGE unsigned, in range). The fix stays within signed
// compares: out-of-range for this shape is `value >= 0 AND value < N` (a non-negative value
// below the low bound), so `boundChecks` emits `value < 0 → IN RANGE, value < N → panic` and
// `literalInRange` reads `value < 0 OR value >= low`. This makes shv2's runtime check agree
// with its own literal check AND with the bootstrap's LITERAL check (which already reads the
// bound unsigned); it DIVERGES only from the bootstrap's RUNTIME check, which keeps the naive
// signed lower bound and so panics on the same value — a bootstrap inconsistency, not a model
// to copy. `rangeIsUnsignedMaxUpper` is the one place the shape is recognized.

// The i64 pattern an unsigned `u64.max` upper bound rides as — named so the `-1`-means-
// `u64.max` reinterpretation is not a bare literal (see RangedTypeAlias, and the sentinel case
// in specs/ranged-typealias.md).
let UnsignedFullUpperPattern = -1 as ParsedInt

// The unsigned-decimal spelling of that same sentinel, for display. `ParsedInt` is a signed i64
// with no unsigned-render helper, and `u64.max` is the only writable bound that stores negative
// (every `uN.max` for N<64 is positive; a negative low is unwritable) — so this one named
// constant is correct and sufficient. Matches the bootstrap's `FormatRange`, which renders the
// bound via `(ulong)`.
let UnsignedFullUpperDecimal = "18446744073709551615"

// The finite extremes `f32`/`f64`'s `.min` / `.max` denote in a `float(...)` range declaration,
// carried as the IEEE-754 **f64** bit pattern the `ParsedInt` bound field holds. A float alias
// resolves to `MaxonType.float` (= f64) with NO width narrowing (v1 TypeResolution.maxon:90 does the
// same — int narrows via an optimal-width choice, float never does), so an `f32` alias's bounds are
// the f64 VALUES of the f32 extremes, not an f32 encoding. `.min` is the most-negative finite and
// `.max` the most-positive — the sign bit apart, which is why `negatedFloatBits` relates the pair.
//
// Spelled as decimal bit patterns rather than `f64.max as ParsedInt`: an `as` cast would TRUNCATE the
// float VALUE to an integer, not reinterpret its bits, so the type-identifier spelling the integer
// bounds use (`u32.max as ParsedInt`) cannot express a float bound. A `.min`/`.max` pair differs only
// in the sign bit (`negatedFloatBits` is the runtime form of that relation), but a top-level `let`
// takes no call, so each is written out.
//
// They live HERE rather than in the parser because TWO parties read them: the parser, resolving a
// `f64.max` bound token, and `rangeIsFull` below, recognizing the span that needs no guard.
//
// ⚠ These are NOT v1's `floatTypeBoundsTable` f32 values (Parser.maxon:21112): v1 stored
// `f32.max = 5727507168572555263` (= 0x4F7C316C97453FFF), which decodes to 7.97e74 — GARBAGE, never
// the f32 extreme 3.4e38. v1 never caught it because a full-range alias is exempt from range checks,
// so nothing ever read the bound. v1's f64 pair IS correct and matches below.
export let F64MaxBound = 9218868437227405311 as ParsedInt  // 0x7FEF_FFFF_FFFF_FFFF  = +1.7976931348623157e308
export let F64MinBound = -4503599627370497 as ParsedInt  // 0xFFEF_FFFF_FFFF_FFFF  = -1.7976931348623157e308
export let F32MaxBound = 5183643170566569984 as ParsedInt  // 0x47EF_FFFF_E000_0000  = +3.4028234663852886e38
export let F32MinBound = -4039728866288205824 as ParsedInt  // 0xC7EF_FFFF_E000_0000  = -3.4028234663852886e38

// ⭐ **IS THIS ALIAS'S DOMAIN THE FLOAT ONE?** THE one place that is decided, because EVERY range
// predicate below forks on it and the fork is not cosmetic: an integer alias's `low`/`high` are
// NUMBERS, a float alias's are IEEE-754 BIT PATTERNS, and the two do not compare the same way.
//
// It reads `underlying` — the `int`/`float` keyword the alias was declared over — which
// `RangedTypeAlias` has always carried and which the guard emitter simply never asked. Until this
// rung the question had no second asker: the parser filtered float aliases out before a check site
// could be recorded, so `InsertRangeChecks` never met one. That filter is gone, and this is what
// replaced it.
export function aliasIsFloat(alias RangedTypeAlias) returns bool
	return tagIsFloat(maxonTypeTag(alias.underlying))
end 'aliasIsFloat'

// ⭐ **THE STRIDE A VALUE OF THIS ALIAS OCCUPIES AS AN ARRAY ELEMENT.** The alias-keyed twin of
// `SignatureIndex.arrayElementSize`, which asks the same question of a container INSTANCE — and both compose
// it through the one `elementStrideOf`, so a stride cannot mean one thing on the value that fills an element
// and another on the instance that stores it.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS ASKED OF EVERY `as`, AND WHAT IS BOUNDED IS THE ANSWER'S COST — NOT THE QUESTION.** Both callers
// reach `Parser.storageDiffersFromAlias`: the array-literal element door asks it of an instance's slot, and
// `Parser.mayRetypeForNarrowerStorage` asks it of an `as` target on every cast. That is safe only because a
// yes now RENAMES the value in place (`retypeValue`, no op) instead of minting a second one. It was briefly a
// sixth arm of `applyCast`'s MINT list, and that broke two programs outright — see
// `Parser.castChangesTheElementPacking`'s ⛔⛔ block for the measurement.
//
// ⚠ **THE FLOAT ARM IS `aliasChangesTheDivisionProof`'s, for its reason and not a rounding of it:** a ranged
// FLOAT alias's `low`/`high` are IEEE-754 BIT PATTERNS, which `rangedAliasStorageBytes` would read as
// ordinary integers and answer a narrow slot for. Maxon's `float` IS f64, so a float element is a machine
// word whatever bounds were written on it.
export function aliasElementStride(alias RangedTypeAlias) returns ElementStride
	if aliasIsFloat(alias) 'aFloatDomain'
		return MachineWordBytes
	end 'aFloatDomain'

	return elementStrideOf(rangedAliasPackedBits(alias.low, high: alias.high), storageBytes: rangedAliasStorageBytes(alias.low, high: alias.high))
end 'aliasElementStride'

// ⚠⚠ **A DOUBLE'S BIT PATTERN DOES NOT ORDER LIKE ITS VALUE.** IEEE-754 is sign-and-magnitude, so
// among NEGATIVES the integer order is exactly REVERSED (-1.0's pattern is numerically larger than
// -100.0's), and `-0.0`'s pattern is `i64.min` while `+0.0`'s is zero. This maps a pattern to an i64
// that orders the way the VALUE does: a non-negative pattern keeps its bits, and a negative one is
// reflected through `i64.min`, which both restores the ordering among negatives and puts every
// negative below every non-negative. `-0.0` and `+0.0` both map to 0, so they compare EQUAL — which
// is what IEEE-754 says and what a range bound needs.
//
// The reflection cannot overflow: `bits` is in `[i64.min, -1]` on that path, so `i64.min - bits` is
// in `[0, i64.min + 1]`.
//
// NaN has no place in this order and none can reach it: a literal that overflows is E2011 before it
// becomes one, and no writable bound names one. shv2 faces the same trap in the constant folder's float
// comparison (`foldFloatCompare`, which compares as real f64 for exactly this reason), the Std dialect
// (`StdDialect`'s `binOpImm`) and `FoldConstOperands` — this is the fourth party that has to know it,
// and it answers it as an order-preserving KEY where `foldFloatCompare` answers it as a comparison.
export function floatOrderKey(bits ParsedInt) returns ParsedInt
	if bits >= 0 'positive'
		return bits
	end 'positive'
	return i64.min - bits
end 'floatOrderKey'

// True when the alias covers every 64-bit PATTERN its underlying type can hold, so no RUNTIME guard is
// needed.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT DOES NOT EXEMPT THE COMPILE-TIME LITERAL CHECK, AND IT USED TO — WHICH IS HOW A WRITTEN `-1`
// GOT INTO AN `int(0 to u64.max)` DOOR.** "Every pattern is admissible" is a statement about VALUES the
// machine can hold, and a written `-1` is not refused for its pattern: it is refused because the source
// wrote a number below a declared lower bound of 0. The two questions were conflated in one predicate,
// so `needsRuntimeGuard` returned before it could look at the literal at all and `take(-1)` printed
// 18446744073709551615 with no diagnostic. `needsRuntimeGuard` now asks the literal FIRST and this
// second; see `literalInRange`'s `writtenNegative`.
//
// INTEGER: two spans cover the whole 64-bit space — the signed-full `int(i64.min to i64.max)` and
// the unsigned-full `int(0 to u64.max)` (whose upper rides as the signed `-1`). A byte/u16/u32
// sub-range is NOT full here: shv2 flows every ranged value at the i64 base with no per-alias
// storage width, so only the two 64-bit spans are unconditionally safe. `ExitCode`/`Byte`-style
// sub-ranges therefore still take a check for a NON-literal value — every enabled case that casts
// into one uses a literal, so the compile-time path elides it and no runtime code differs.
//
// FLOAT: the full span is `float(f64.min to f64.max)`, compared against the f64 BIT PATTERNS — the
// integer test would answer `false` for it (`F64MinBound` is a small negative i64, nowhere near
// `i64.min`) and every full-range float alias would then carry a pointless guard whose signed
// compares would REJECT ordinary values. `float(f32.min to f32.max)` is deliberately NOT full: it is
// exactly the narrowing #19 is about.
export function rangeIsFull(low ParsedInt, high ParsedInt, isFloat bool) returns bool
	if isFloat 'float'
		return low == F64MinBound and high == F64MaxBound
	end 'float'
	if low == i64.min and high == i64.max 'signedFull'
		return true
	end 'signedFull'
	return low == 0 and high == UnsignedFullUpperPattern
end 'rangeIsFull'

// **THE UNSIGNED-MAX UPPER SHAPE** — a range whose upper is `u64.max` (riding as the signed
// `-1`) over a non-negative low bound: `int(0 to u64.max)`, `int(5 to u64.max)`. THE one place
// this shape is recognized, shared by the runtime cascade (`InsertRangeChecks.boundChecks`) and
// the compile-time literal check (`literalInRange`) so they cannot come to disagree about it.
//
// A non-negative low bound (`rangeIsUnsigned`) is what makes the range purely UNSIGNED: every
// admissible value is non-negative in unsigned terms, and a bit-63-set value (negative signed)
// is a huge unsigned that the range ADMITS. The full-unsigned `int(0 to u64.max)` (low == 0)
// is included: `rangeIsFull` elides it before the runtime cascade ever asks, but the literal
// check calls this directly with no such gate, and there the low == 0 reading — in range for
// every value — is exactly right.
export function rangeIsUnsignedMaxUpper(low ParsedInt, high ParsedInt) returns bool
	return high == UnsignedFullUpperPattern and rangeIsUnsigned(low)
end 'rangeIsUnsignedMaxUpper'

// ⭐⭐ **DOES AN *INCLUSIVE* COUNTED LOOP'S TOP BOUND MAKE ITS STEP WRAP INSTEAD OF ITS TEST FAIL? (EC7)**
// `for i in a to b` runs the body AT `b`, steps past it, and tests again — so at the top of the domain the
// test reads, that step wraps to the domain's BOTTOM, the test passes, and the loop never leaves. The
// counter then takes values no interval over `[a, b]` contains, which is why `Parser.mintCountedBodyCounter`
// states no interval for such a loop rather than one that stops being true after a wrap.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THERE ARE TWO TOPS, BECAUSE THE TEST HAS TWO READINGS — AND REFUSING ONLY THE SIGNED ONE WAS A
// MEASURED WRONG ANSWER (EC7 review).** `Parser.comparisonSignedness` picks a signedness valid for both
// operands, so `i64.max` is the top under the SIGNED reading and `u64.max` — riding as the same `-1` pattern
// this file's unsigned-upper shape is recognized by — is the top under the UNSIGNED one. MEASURED with only
// `i64.max` refused: `for i in ((0 - 5) as int(0 to u64.max)) to u64.max` compiled its test as `jcc above`,
// walked past `-1` into `0, 1, …`, and an `as int(-5 to -1)` inside the body was elided against the interval
// `[-5, -1]` — a value outside its declared range, no panic, exit 0, where every compiler without the
// elision panics on the sixth trip.
//
// ⚠ The EXCLUSIVE form needs no such rule and it is not an oversight: `upto` stops the first time the test
// fails, which it does at `bound` itself under either reading, so its counter never reaches a step that
// wraps.
export function inclusiveTopBoundWraps(bound ParsedInt) returns bool
	return bound == i64.max or bound == UnsignedFullUpperPattern
end 'inclusiveTopBoundWraps'

// Does the LOWER bound need a runtime compare?
//
// INTEGER: only when it is above the smallest i64 — a `low == i64.min` can never be violated.
// (Bootstrap: `IntLower > long.MinValue`.)
//
// FLOAT: only when it is above the most-negative finite double. `F64MinBound` is a small NEGATIVE
// i64 as a bit pattern, so the integer test would call every float lower bound necessary — and, far
// worse, would call `float(f64.min to X)`'s bound necessary while comparing the wrong way round.
export function rangeNeedsLowerCheck(low ParsedInt, isFloat bool) returns bool
	if isFloat 'float'
		return low != F64MinBound
	end 'float'
	return low > i64.min
end 'rangeNeedsLowerCheck'

// Does the UPPER bound need a runtime compare?
//
// INTEGER: only below i64.max — an upper of i64.max can never be violated — and only when the bound
// is a REAL one rather than the wrapped `u64.max`, which is unbounded in signed space and whose
// compare `boundChecks` replaces with the sign-plus-lower cascade (see `rangeIsUnsignedMaxUpper`).
//
// ⚠⚠ **A NEGATIVE STORED UPPER IS THE `u64.max` SENTINEL IN EXACTLY ONE SHAPE, AND THIS ASKED ONLY THE
// BOUND'S SIGN — so a WHOLLY NEGATIVE range got no upper compare at all.** The old rule was
// `high >= 0 and high < i64.max` (the bootstrap's `IntUpper >= 0 && IntUpper < long.MaxValue`, copied
// with it), which elided the compare for `int(-100 to -1)` and `int(i64.min to -2)` — ranges whose
// upper is ordinary: `-1` is the largest value `int(-100 to -1)` admits and `0` is outside it.
// `int(i64.min to -2)` was the total case, because its `low == i64.min` needs no compare either, so the
// range carried an EMPTY check list and a plain `as` cast admitted anything. **MEASURED: `0 as
// int(-100 to -1)` was admitted at runtime while the LITERAL `0 as int(-100 to -1)` was E3005** — the two
// halves of one rule disagreeing about one alias, because `literalInRange` asks
// `rangeIsUnsignedMaxUpper` and this asked the sign. The consequences reached past the binding into
// `Parser.divisorProof`, which trusts a declared range: `int(-100 to -1)` excludes 0 so `100 / d` is a
// bare `idiv` (an uncatchable `integer divide by zero`), and `int(i64.min to -2)` excludes -1 so
// `i64.min mod d` is one too (`integer overflow` — the very fault A1x removes). Pinned by
// `ranged-typealias.md`'s `negative-upper-bound-cast-is-checked` and `safety.md`'s two
// `negative-range-cast-guard-fires-before-the-*` cases.
//
// ⇒ the sentinel question is DELEGATED, not restated: `rangeIsUnsignedMaxUpper` is the one place the
// shape is recognized, and it is `high == -1 AND low >= 0`. The pairing cannot be ambiguous, because a
// negative low beside a `u64.max` upper is refused at DECLARATION (`int(-1 to u64.max)` is
// "not representable in 64 bits"), so a stored `high == -1` under a negative low is always a real `-1`.
//
// ⚠ It takes `low` for that reason, unlike `rangeNeedsLowerCheck`, whose rule genuinely reads one bound.
// Asking the shape here rather than leaning on `boundChecks`'s earlier `rangeIsUnsignedMaxUpper` arm is
// deliberate: this predicate is EXPORTED and states a rule, and a second caller reading it without that
// arm in front would elide `int(5 to u64.max)`'s cascade or emit a compare that rejects what the range
// admits.
//
// FLOAT: only below the largest finite double. The sign test the integer rule made is meaningless
// on a bit pattern — `float(-1.0 to -0.5)`'s upper is a NEGATIVE i64 and is a perfectly real bound
// that must be checked — which is the same mistake one domain over, and the float arm never made it.
export function rangeNeedsUpperCheck(low ParsedInt, high ParsedInt, isFloat bool) returns bool
	if isFloat 'float'
		return high != F64MaxBound
	end 'float'

	if rangeIsUnsignedMaxUpper(low, high: high) 'unsignedUpperIsUnbounded'
		return false
	end 'unsignedUpperIsUnbounded'

	return high < i64.max
end 'rangeNeedsUpperCheck'

// Is a literal `value` within `[low, high]` (inclusive)? The compile-time E3005 test, and the
// same verdict the bootstrap's LITERAL check gives (which reads the bound unsigned).
//
// FLOAT: the three patterns go through `floatOrderKey` so they compare as the VALUES they encode.
// A raw integer compare passes for two positive bounds and quietly inverts for a negative one —
// `float(-100.0 to -1.0)` would admit nothing and reject everything — which is precisely why
// `ranged-typealias.md` pins a negative range and a zero-straddling one.
//
// For the unsigned-max upper shape the range is UNSIGNED and its upper is unbounded, so in-range
// is `value < 0 OR value >= low`: a bit-63-set value (negative signed) is a huge unsigned that
// the range admits, and a non-negative value is in range iff it is at least `low`. Every OTHER
// integer range is the ordinary inclusive `[low, high]` — correct because a negative literal fails
// the signed lower bound exactly as its huge unsigned image would fail a modest positive upper.
//
// ⭐⭐ **`writtenNegative` IS WHAT THE 64 BITS CANNOT SAY, AND WITHOUT IT THE UNSIGNED ARM ADMITS A
// WRITTEN `-1` INTO A RANGE WHOSE LOWER BOUND IS 0.** `-1`, `u64.max` and `0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE` are all
// bit-63-set patterns; the first denotes a NEGATIVE number and the other two denote the two largest
// non-negative ones, and no test of the pattern can tell them apart. So the arm above — which reads a
// bit-63-set pattern as "a huge unsigned the range admits" — is right for the two and WRONG for the
// one, and `take(-1)` compiled silently into an `int(0 to u64.max)` parameter and printed
// 18446744073709551615. The fact comes from the SOURCE (`Parser.writtenNegativeLiterals`, the
// companion of `unsignedMaxLiterals` and marked at the same kind of site), never from the value.
//
// ⚠ It is spelled as a written-NEGATIVE mark rather than a written-non-negative one on purpose: a
// missed arrival then leaves today's answer standing, where the dual would REFUSE every folded
// bit-63-set constant the corpus already passes (an FNV offset basis, a `1 shl 63`) on the strength of
// a spelling nobody enumerated.
//
// ⚠ The `-0` case is why this is a CLAUSE and not an early `return false`: `-0` is written negative and
// denotes 0, which `int(0 to u64.max)` admits — so the non-negative arm still decides it.
export function literalInRange(value ParsedInt, low ParsedInt, high ParsedInt, isFloat bool, writtenNegative bool) returns bool
	if isFloat 'float'
		let key = floatOrderKey(value)
		return key >= floatOrderKey(low) and key <= floatOrderKey(high)
	end 'float'
	if rangeIsUnsignedMaxUpper(low, high: high) 'unsignedUpper'
		return (value < 0 and not writtenNegative) or value >= low
	end 'unsignedUpper'
	return value >= low and value <= high
end 'literalInRange'

// ⭐⭐ **DOES THE TARGET ALIAS ADMIT EVERY VALUE THE SOURCE ALIAS DOES? — the whole content of E3010,
// AND the whole content of the range guard's elision (A4f).** A cast that cannot lose a value contributes
// nothing (`Parser.reportUnneededCastBetween`): the language already widens into the target implicitly, so
// writing the `as` says something that was true anyway. Both ends are DECLARED ranged aliases by the time
// this is asked — a source with no alias name is not an unneeded cast but an ordinary one, which is what
// keeps `let x = 42` + `x as Float` legal.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE DIAGNOSTIC AND THE EMITTER MUST NOT ANSWER THIS TWICE.** E3010 saying *"'Byte' already fits in
// 'ExitCode'"* is a PROOF that the source range is inside the destination; the runtime cascade emitted
// behind that same proof tests a value that cannot fail it. (Written out, that cascade reads
// `0 ≤ x ≤ 4294967295` on WINDOWS only — `ExitCode`'s high bound is platform-shaped, see
// `Project.exitCodeAliasHigh` — and which number it carries is beside the point: what makes it dead is the
// CONTAINMENT, which holds on every target.) Before A4f the two halves were decided in different places
// from different facts — `InsertRangeChecks.needsRuntimeGuard` never saw a source range at all — so
// removing an unneeded cast ADDED a provably-dead guard.
// `rangeCoversDenotedRange` below is the spelling the guard doors ask, and it ends here.
//
// CROSS-DOMAIN: only `int → float` covers, and it covers ALWAYS — an f64 spans every i64 the corpus can
// write, and the declared float alias is the full `float(f64.min to f64.max)` in every use. The reverse
// (`float → int`) never reaches here: `castHasNoLegalConversion` refuses it as E3009 first.
//
// FLOAT: through `floatOrderKey`, NOT the raw patterns — the reference's own version compares the stored
// i64s directly, which is the sign-and-magnitude trap `floatOrderKey`'s header describes and which
// silently inverts the verdict for a negative bound (`float(-100.0 to -1.0)`). shv2 has the order key; the
// divergence is deliberate.
//
// INTEGER, and the UNSIGNED-MAX-UPPER shape is why this is not two `<=`s: `int(0 to u64.max)` rides with
// `high == -1`, so a plain signed compare would call it a SUBSET of `int(i64.min to i64.max)` and report
// the genuinely-narrowing `Word as Integer` as unneeded. The shape is recognized by the one predicate that
// recognizes it everywhere else; two ranges that disagree about carrying it are reported NOT covered
// (their union is unrepresentable in 64 bits, and over-reporting coverage is a FALSE REJECTION of a valid
// program). When BOTH carry it the uppers are equal by construction and both lows are non-negative
// (`rangeIsUnsigned` holds inside the shape), so the lows decide under an ordinary signed compare.
export function rangeCoversRange(target RangedTypeAlias, source RangedTypeAlias) returns bool
	let targetIsFloat = aliasIsFloat(target)
	// ⛔⛔ **THIS ARM USED TO ANSWER `true` FOR EVERY int→float PAIR, WHATEVER THE FLOAT'S RANGE — SO A
	// GENUINELY NARROWING CONVERSION WAS REPORTED AS AN UNNEEDED ONE.** It rested on *"the declared float
	// alias is the full `float(f64.min to f64.max)` in every use"* — a statement about the aliases the CORPUS
	// happens to declare, not about the types. Its own sibling `rangeCoversIntBounds` already declined a float
	// target rather than inherit that premise, and said so.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE FULL-RANGE ANSWER IS A LANGUAGE RULE AND IS KEPT.** `docs/LANGUAGE_REFERENCE.md:414-418`:
	// *"The compiler auto-widens at use sites, so any explicit cast must actually narrow or refine."* An
	// int→float widening into a full-range float IS reachable without writing the cast, so writing it is
	// redundant — and `specs/type-casting.md`'s `error.unneeded.widening-int-to-float` pins exactly that
	// (`Integer as JsonFloat` ⇒ E3010), in the CANONICAL corpus the oracle runs. What was wrong was applying
	// that answer to a float alias the value provably does NOT fit in.
	//
	// ⚠ **WHAT MADE IT WORTH FIXING NOW: the ranged-typealias rule removed the last spelling that dodged it.**
	// While `y as float` could be written bare the source named no alias, so E3010 stayed silent and every
	// int→float conversion was spellable. With the bare keyword refused in every type position both ends
	// necessarily name aliases, so a blanket `true` here left NO WAY AT ALL to write an explicit int→float
	// conversion. MEASURED on `specs-shv2/register-pressure.md`'s
	// `dead-def-inherits-only-its-own-register-file-float`, whose whole subject is the dead XMM def a
	// `cvtsi2sd` produces: `let unused = y as Real` ⇒ *"unneeded cast: 'Integer' already fits in 'Real'"*.
	//
	// ⚠ Its OTHER reader is the guard elision (`valueIsProvablyInRange`), and the narrower answer is right
	// there too: a full-range float target still elides, while one the source overflows now takes the check it
	// always needed. The bootstrap's twin (`TargetCoversSource`) has the un-narrowed defect and is board row
	// **G33**, which this does not close — it fixes shv2's half, the half this change makes reachable.
	if targetIsFloat != aliasIsFloat(source) 'crossDomain'
		return targetIsFloat and rangeIsFull(target.low, high: target.high, isFloat: true)
	end 'crossDomain'

	if targetIsFloat 'float'
		return floatOrderKey(target.low) <= floatOrderKey(source.low) and floatOrderKey(target.high) >= floatOrderKey(source.high)
	end 'float'

	return rangeCoversIntBounds(target, low: source.low, high: source.high)
end 'rangeCoversRange'

// ⭐⭐ **THE INTEGER HALF OF THE CONTAINMENT ABOVE, ASKED WHERE THE SOURCE IS AN INTERVAL NOTHING
// NAMES (EC7).** A counted `for i in a upto b` gives its counter a range the program never wrote down as a
// typealias, so there is no `RangedTypeAlias` to hand `rangeCoversRange` — only two bounds. The arithmetic
// is identical and lives here once; `rangeCoversRange` is this function plus the float and cross-domain
// forks, which are the only parts an ALIAS adds.
//
// ⚠ **A FLOAT TARGET IS REFUSED HERE WHERE `rangeCoversRange` ACCEPTS IT, AND THE DIVERGENCE IS THE
// PREMISE, NOT THE ARITHMETIC.** That function's cross-domain arm answers `true` for every int source
// because *"the declared float alias is the full `float(f64.min to f64.max)` in every use"* — a statement
// about the aliases the corpus DECLARES, which a nameless interval is not covered by. Under a genuinely
// narrow `float(0.0 to 1.0)` the claim is false (a counter reaching 10 converts to `10.0`, outside it), so
// the interval that names nothing declines rather than inheriting a premise it cannot check. It costs an
// elision on a shape nothing in the corpus writes.
export function rangeCoversIntBounds(target RangedTypeAlias, low ParsedInt, high ParsedInt) returns bool
	if aliasIsFloat(target) 'aFloatTarget'
		return false
	end 'aFloatTarget'

	let targetIsUnsignedMaxUpper = rangeIsUnsignedMaxUpper(target.low, high: target.high)
	if targetIsUnsignedMaxUpper != rangeIsUnsignedMaxUpper(low, high: high) 'mixedRepresentation'
		return false
	end 'mixedRepresentation'

	if targetIsUnsignedMaxUpper 'bothUnbounded'
		return target.low <= low
	end 'bothUnbounded'

	return target.low <= low and target.high >= high
end 'rangeCoversIntBounds'

// ⭐⭐ **THE SAME CONTAINMENT, ASKED WHERE THE SOURCE MAY NAME NO ALIAS AT ALL — what a range-check DOOR
// holds, and the one thing that decides whether its runtime guard is provably dead (A4f).** Every door
// that records a site has already resolved its DESTINATION alias (it needs the name for the site), and
// faces a SOURCE expression that may or may not denote one — which is exactly the pair E3010 is reported
// from, one end already unwrapped. This adds that one unwrap and nothing else, so a door reaches no
// verdict of its own and the emitter cannot come to disagree with the diagnostic.
//
// ⚠ **AN `undeclared` SOURCE PROVES NOTHING, AND THAT IS THE DIRECTION THAT KEEPS THE GUARD.** A bare
// `int` local, a folded literal, a freshly minted `trunc` result: none states any bounds, so nothing
// about it is inside anything. `false` records the site and leaves the check exactly where it was.
//
// ⚠ **WHAT THE `true` ANSWER RESTS ON: that a value denoting alias `A` is IN `A`'s range.** That is the
// induction every door in this family maintains — a cast site guards its result, a parameter is guarded
// at the callee's entry, a `return` before its `ret`, a field or element at its store — so a value handed
// on under `A` has already passed `A`'s cascade or been refused by E3005. The one producer that breaks it
// is `Array.resize`, which EXPOSES zero-initialized slots crossing no door at all (`specs-shv2/safety.md`'s
// `divide-by-zero-fault-through-a-resized-array-slot`); a wider door downstream used to catch such a slot
// BY COINCIDENCE — it fires only where the exposed `0` also falls outside the SECOND range — and a
// coincidence is not a guarantee this elision may be written against. That hole is `resize`'s to close.
export function rangeCoversDenotedRange(target RangedTypeAlias, source DeclaredAlias) returns bool
	return match source 'source'
		undeclared gives false
		declared(sourceAlias) gives rangeCoversRange(target, source: sourceAlias)
	end 'source'
end 'rangeCoversDenotedRange'

// The E3005 a literal out-of-range NARROWING reports — `Value N is outside the range of 'Alias'
// (int(lo to hi))` — worded ONCE, for BOTH the body-cast/return pass (`InsertRangeChecks`) and
// the top-level const-cast path (`Parser.applyConstCast`, via the re-raised ParseError). The two
// report the same defect from different tiers and must not phrase it two ways; the alias and its
// bounds spell through `rangedAliasValueSetDisplay`, the one place that pair renders.
//
// ⚠ **THE ALIAS IS QUOTED AS *SOURCE* SPELLS IT (N2 review), because a RANGE-CONTESTED name reaches here
// wearing the compiler's mint.** `sourceSpelledAliasName` is applied at this one site rather than at each
// tier, so neither can quote a declaration the program does not contain — MEASURED as
// `outside the range of 'Byte$0_1000' (int(0 to 1000))`, which names no line the author wrote and prints
// the real bounds beside the suffix that was supposed to carry them.
export function literalOutOfRangeMessage(value ParsedInt, aliasName String, low ParsedInt, high ParsedInt, isFloat bool) returns String
	return "Value {formatBound(value, isFloat: isFloat)} is outside the range of {rangedAliasValueSetDisplay(aliasName, low: low, high: high, isFloat: isFloat)}"
end 'literalOutOfRangeMessage'

// The E3005 an out-of-range `otherwise` DEFAULT reports — the sentence above with its own subject, since
// what is out of range is the handler's value and not a narrowing. Worded HERE, beside its twin, because
// the two say one thing about one alias: the name-plus-bounds pair spells through
// `rangedAliasValueSetDisplay`, so neither half can be printed two ways depending on which E3005 a value
// meets.
//
// ⛔ **THE MINT LEAKED THROUGH THIS ONE (found while landing N3).** `literalOutOfRangeMessage` had the strip
// and this arm — rendered inline in `Queries.reportParseError` — did not, so a program declaring
// `typealias Byte = int(300 to 1000)` beside another file's `Byte` was told `otherwise value 0 is outside
// the range of 'Byte$300_1000' (int(300 to 1000))`: a declaration the program does not contain, with the
// real bounds printed in the same sentence. One alias, two spellings, decided by which of two E3005s the
// value happened to meet.
//
// ⚠ The VALUE is spelled plainly and deliberately NOT through `formatBound`: an `otherwise` default is an
// integer LITERAL the author wrote, never the wrapped `u64.max` sentinel that helper exists to un-wrap, and
// the oracle prints it plainly too.
export function otherwiseOutOfRangeMessage(value ParsedInt, aliasName String, low ParsedInt, high ParsedInt) returns String
	return "otherwise value {value} is outside the range of {rangedAliasValueSetDisplay(aliasName, low: low, high: high, isFloat: false)}"
end 'otherwiseOutOfRangeMessage'

// ⭐ **THE E3005 A `b"…"` BYTE-STRING LITERAL'S OWN BYTE REPORTS (A3r)** — the third subject of the one
// range fact, worded here for the reason its `otherwise` sibling above is: the name-plus-bounds pair spells
// through `rangedAliasValueSetDisplay`, so no site can print an alias or a range two ways, while the subject
// stays the site's own.
//
// ⚠ **THE SUBJECT IS NOT A NARROWING, AND SAYING SO COSTS THE READER THE OFFSET.** A blob is a run of
// values the source never wrote out one by one, so `literalOutOfRangeMessage`'s `Value 223` would leave the
// author of `b"\x41\xdf\x02"` to work out WHICH byte is 223. The offset is the only thing this sentence
// carries that its sibling cannot, and it is exactly why it is a sentence rather than a reuse.
//
// ⚠ The VALUE is a byte and is spelled plainly — it is never the wrapped `u64.max` pattern `formatBound`
// exists to un-wrap. The RANGE is not: an element alias's bounds may be a float's bit patterns, so it keeps
// the alias's own domain rather than assuming the byte's.
export function byteStringByteOutOfRangeMessage(value ParsedInt, offset ParsedInt, aliasName String, low ParsedInt, high ParsedInt, isFloat bool) returns String
	return "byte {value} at offset {offset} of a `b\"…\"` byte-string literal is outside the range of {rangedAliasValueSetDisplay(aliasName, low: low, high: high, isFloat: isFloat)}"
end 'byteStringByteOutOfRangeMessage'

// ⭐ **THE E3117 A RAW-BYTE FILL REPORTS (A4a)** — the fourth subject of the one range fact, and the only
// one of the four that names NO value, because there is none to name: the bytes arrive at run time.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS A STATEMENT ABOUT THE ELEMENT, NOT ABOUT A VALUE, AND THAT IS WHAT SEPARATES IT FROM ITS
// SIBLING ABOVE.** `byteStringByteOutOfRangeMessage` can say *"byte 223 at offset 0"* because the blob is in
// the compiler's hand; here the fill is one runtime blit, so the only true sentence is that the element
// cannot hold every value a byte has. Naming the CONSTRUCT is what replaces naming the value — three of them
// reach this rule and an author needs to know which of their lines is the fill.
//
// ⚠ **IT NAMES THE ELEMENT AND NEVER THE ARRAY, AND THE FIRST DRAFT DID THE OPPOSITE AND WAS FALSE.** It
// said *"fills an 'Array with Byte'"*, which is true only of the byte view's own instance: the suite caught
// it printing that sentence about an `Array with Int` (`managed-memory-methods`). Every one of the three
// sites is a store into an ELEMENT, and that is the one subject all of them share.
//
// The name-plus-bounds pair spells through `rangedAliasValueSetDisplay`, for its siblings' reason: no site
// may print an alias or a range two ways. `isFloat` rides along for the reason it does on every other member
// of this family — the payloads cannot say whether they are numbers or IEEE-754 bit patterns. **A BYTE'S OWN
// value set is `Project.ByteValueSetLow`/`High`** and not a hand-spelled `0 to 255`, for the identical
// reason one door up: `rangeHoldsEveryByte` is the predicate this sentence reports, and a message that
// stated the bounds itself would be a second definition of what a byte is (A4c review).
// ⚠ **IT NAMES THE CURE AND THE CURE IS THE SAME AT ALL THREE SITES**, which is what lets one sentence carry
// it: widen the element. E3109 names a replacement SPELLING because its refusal has an exact one; this
// refusal has none — there is no other way to ask for a String's bytes, or to read a file into a buffer —
// so what the author has to change is the declaration, and the message says so rather than leaving it to be
// inferred from a range.
export function rawByteFillNarrowElementMessage(member String, aliasName String, low ParsedInt, high ParsedInt, isFloat bool) returns String
	return "'{member}' stores RAW bytes into an element declared {rangedAliasValueSetDisplay(aliasName, low: low, high: high, isFloat: isFloat)}, which does not hold every byte value {ByteValueSetLow} to {ByteValueSetHigh} — widen the element's declared range to store raw bytes through it"
end 'rawByteFillNarrowElementMessage'

// ⭐⭐ **E3118 — A RAW-BYTE WRITE INTO A SLOT ITS ELEMENT DOES NOT FILL (A4c).** The sibling above's neighbour,
// and the wording is what keeps the two rules from reading as one: this one is about the SLOT, so it states
// the slot's width AND the value set that width has, and only then says the element does not cover it. A
// reader who sees `1-byte slot — every value 0 to 255` beside an element declared `int(0 to 100)` has the
// whole argument in front of them.
//
// ⚠ **THE ELEMENT ARRIVES ALREADY RENDERED, which the sibling's structured payload does not need and this one
// cannot do without.** `bool`, an enum's ordinal and an opaque `T` are refused here as readily as a ranged
// alias, so there is no `low`/`high` pair every subject has — see `ParseError.rawByteWriteSlotDomain`.
//
// ⚠ **IT NAMES TWO CURES AND THE SECOND IS THE ONE MOST AUTHORS WANT**: `set` is the ARRAY surface's writer
// and range-checks the value against the element, so a program staging bytes into a container that is not a
// byte buffer has somewhere to go. E3117's refusal has no such alternative — there is no other way to ask for
// a String's bytes — which is why that message names only the declaration.
//
// ⚠ **THE SLOT'S VALUE SET IS DERIVED FROM THE WIDTH RATHER THAN CARRIED BESIDE IT**, so the two halves of
// the comparison this sentence makes cannot come to disagree — and it renders through the very
// `formatRangeDisplay` the element's own half uses, which is what makes `int(0 to 65535)` beside
// `int(0 to 1000)` the whole argument. That renderer is also the ONE party that knows a raw `-1` upper means
// `u64.max`, which the machine-word rung is: printed straight it read *"every value 0 to -1"*.
//
// ⚠⚠ **A SUB-BYTE-PACKED ELEMENT GETS THE OTHER HALF OF THE SENTENCE, AND IT IS A DIFFERENT FACT RATHER
// THAN A NARROWER ONE.** Every clause above presumes the element HAS a byte of its own to be measured
// against. A packed element does not — 8, 4 or 2 of them share one byte — so there is no slot value set to
// render (`slotUnsignedMax` would be asked for the value set of `-1` bytes and rightly panics), and "the
// element does not admit all of them" is not what is wrong: a raw byte write would land on the element AND
// on its NEIGHBOURS. Same code, because the construct refused and the cure offered are identical; different
// reason, because stating the byte-slot one here would be false.
export function rawByteWriteSlotDomainMessage(member String, elementDisplay String, slotBytes ElementStride) returns String
	if slotBytes < 0 'aSubByteFieldSharesItsByteWithItsNeighbours'
		return "'{member}' writes a RAW BYTE at a byte OFFSET, and the element {elementDisplay} is SUB-BYTE PACKED at {-slotBytes} bit(s) — it has no byte of its own, so one raw byte spans this element and the several after it. Store through the `Array` surface's `set`, which writes one element's bits and range-checks the value against it"
	end 'aSubByteFieldSharesItsByteWithItsNeighbours'

	let slotValueSet = formatRangeDisplay(SlotUnsignedMin, high: slotUnsignedMax(slotBytes as ByteOffset), isFloat: false)

	return "'{member}' writes a RAW BYTE at a byte OFFSET, so what the element's own accessors read back is any bit pattern of its {slotBytes}-byte slot — every value of {slotValueSet}. The element {elementDisplay} does not admit all of them. Widen the element's declared range to cover its whole slot, or store through the `Array` surface's `set`, which range-checks each value against the element"
end 'rawByteWriteSlotDomainMessage'

// ⭐⭐ **A RANGED ALIAS PLUS ITS DECLARED VALUE SET — `'Wide' (int(0 to 1000))` — AND EVERY SENTENCE IN THIS
// FAMILY IS BUILT ON IT (A4c review).** Five diagnostics say *"this alias, whose range is this"* and each of
// them used to compose the pair itself out of `quotedAliasName` and `formatRangeDisplay`. Their headers all
// argued that *no site may print an alias or a range two ways* — and then wrote the composition down five
// times, so nothing but that argument kept the quoting, the spacing and the parentheses in step. A4c added
// the fifth copy while introducing this function for E3118 alone; it is now the one spelling, and a change
// to how an element's value set reads moves all five together or none.
//
// ⚠ It stays a NAME plus BOUNDS and nothing more, because the five subjects differ in everything else — a
// narrowing's value, an `otherwise` default, a blob byte's offset, the construct that fills, E3118's slot.
// The sentence around the pair is each site's own; only the pair is shared. And E3118 cannot pass a
// `low`/`high` at all for a `bool` or an enum ordinal, which is why its parser picks the shape and calls
// this for the one shape that has bounds (`Parser.slotElementDisplay`).
export function rangedAliasValueSetDisplay(aliasName String, low ParsedInt, high ParsedInt, isFloat bool) returns String
	return "'{quotedAliasName(aliasName)}' ({formatRangeDisplay(low, high: high, isFloat: isFloat)})"
end 'rangedAliasValueSetDisplay'

// ⭐ **AN ALIAS NAME AS AN E3005 QUOTES IT** — `sourceSpelledAliasName` applied at the one tier a name
// becomes text an author reads, so no renderer can quote the compiler's bookkeeping and no PRODUCER has to
// remember to strip before filling in an error's payload.
function quotedAliasName(aliasName String) returns String
	return String.from(sourceSpelledAliasName(aliasName.toByteArray()))
end 'quotedAliasName'

// A single range bound (or the offending value) as source text — the SIGNED spelling.
//
// FLOAT: the bound is an f64 BIT PATTERN, so it renders through `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`'s
// `__float_textFromBits` — the shortest
// decimal that reads back as the same double, which is what the bootstrap prints. Printing the
// pattern itself would name a number nobody wrote.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT IS SPELLED `float.textFromBits`, AND THE RESERVED SPELLING IS NOT AVAILABLE HERE.** This file
// is COMPILER source, and to the compiler compiling it that is an ordinary user program: a `__` callee
// written out in a file that does not DECLARE the name is refused by
// `Parser.requireCalleeIsNotReservedName` (E3004), which is the rule
// `specs-shv2/reserved-double-underscore.md` pins as *"a `__` name may be REFERENCED from exactly one
// file, not from user code generally"*. The PRIMITIVE-STATIC spelling is the surface over that reserved
// name — `Parser.primitiveStaticCallee` mints exactly the `__float_textFromBits` bytes out of it — so
// this reaches the identical stdlib body through the door the rule leaves open, and self-compilation
// needs no exemption. `Parser.floatLiteralBits` spells its own reader the same way for the same reason.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE `u64.max` SENTINEL IS DELIBERATELY NOT SPELLED HERE, AND MOVING IT OUT IS A FIX.** This used
// to render ANY `-1` as `18446744073709551615`, which is the same one-shape fact
// `rangeNeedsUpperCheck` got wrong: a stored `-1` means `u64.max` only in the
// `rangeIsUnsignedMaxUpper` shape. A renderer holding ONE number cannot tell which it has, so it
// produced sentences that contradicted themselves — `int(-1 to -1)` reported as
// `int(18446744073709551615 to 18446744073709551615)` (a declaration nobody wrote), and a `-1` VALUE
// reported as `Value 18446744073709551615` beside signed bounds on the same line. `formatRangeDisplay`
// holds BOTH bounds and is the only party that can ask, so the reinterpretation lives there and the
// offending VALUE — which is a signed i64 the author wrote — never receives it.
function formatBound(bound ParsedInt, isFloat bool) returns String
	if isFloat 'float'
		return float.textFromBits(bound)
	end 'float'

	return "{bound}"
end 'formatBound'

// The `int(<lo> to <hi>)` / `float(<lo> to <hi>)` spelling for an E3005 message (`Value N is outside
// the range of 'Alias' (int(lo to hi))`). Each bound renders through `formatBound`, so a float bound
// shows as its decimal value — matching the bootstrap's `FormatRange` — rather than the raw pattern. An
// exclusive `upto` alias is normalized to `high - 1` at parse and no enabled case surfaces one here, so
// the `to` spelling is the only one reached.
//
// ⭐ **THE ONE PLACE THE `-1`-MEANS-`u64.max` REINTERPRETATION IS SPELLED FOR A READER**, gated on the
// one place that shape is RECOGNIZED, because only a party holding both bounds can tell the sentinel
// from a real `-1` (see `formatBound`). It applies to the UPPER alone: a negative low paired with a
// `u64.max` upper is refused at declaration, so a low bound never carries the sentinel.
function formatRangeDisplay(low ParsedInt, high ParsedInt, isFloat bool) returns String
	let keyword = "float" if isFloat else "int"
	let upper = UnsignedFullUpperDecimal if not isFloat and rangeIsUnsignedMaxUpper(low, high: high) else formatBound(high, isFloat: isFloat)

	return "{keyword}({formatBound(low, isFloat: isFloat)} to {upper})"
end 'formatRangeDisplay'

// **DOES THIS SHIFT FILL WITH THE SIGN, OR WITH ZEROS?** THE one place THAT is answered — the
// question `evalShift` (the fold), `Parser.emitShift` (the opcode) and `Parser.emitGuardedShift`
// (the runtime saturation) must never answer differently, because a fold and a codegen that
// disagree is a silent wrong answer.
//
// Only an ARITHMETIC right shift fills with the sign. A left shift vacates the LOW bits and always
// fills them with zeros, whatever the operand's signedness — so its fill is not a question at all.
//
// ⚠ `leftOperandIsUnsigned` is a property of the value being SHIFTED and NEVER of the count.
export function shiftFillsWithSign(op MaxonBinOp, leftOperandIsUnsigned bool) returns bool
	return op == MaxonBinOp.shr and not leftOperandIsUnsigned
end 'shiftFillsWithSign'

// The opcode a SOURCE shift emits, once its fill is known — the one place the answer above is
// turned into an instruction. `shl` fills the vacated LOW bits with zeros whatever its operand's
// signedness, so it is never in question; only a right shift splits, into the arithmetic `shr`
// and the logical `shrLogical`.
//
// `infixMaxonOp` maps the token `shr` to `MaxonBinOp.shr` unconditionally — it has no operand to
// look at — so this is where a `shrLogical` is ever born, and `Parser.emitShift` is its only
// caller.
export function shiftOpcodeFor(op MaxonBinOp, signFills bool) returns MaxonBinOp
	if op == MaxonBinOp.shl 'left'
		return MaxonBinOp.shl
	end 'left'

	if not shiftOperator(op) 'notAShift'
		panic("shiftOpcodeFor: {op.rawValue} is not a shift — only `shl`/`shr` reach here from `infixMaxonOp`")
	end 'notAShift'

	if signFills 'arithmetic'
		return MaxonBinOp.shr
	end 'arithmetic'

	return MaxonBinOp.shrLogical
end 'shiftOpcodeFor'

// Fold `lhs <op> count`, where `op` is an EMITTED shift opcode — `shiftOpcodeFor`'s answer, not the
// source token. `count` MUST be non-negative: a negative one is E2054, and is never folded.
//
// It takes NO `signFills` flag, and that is the point. The opcode already IS the decision — the
// rule was asked once, of the left operand, and written into it — so the fold and the codegen read
// one fact rather than two that could drift. `shr` is the arithmetic right shift by definition of
// the enum; asking it is not re-deciding anything.
//
// The saturation has two shapes because a shift has two fills: one that fills with ZEROS shifts
// every bit out, and so saturates to 0; one that fills with the SIGN leaves the sign behind, and
// `lhs shr 63` already IS the sign — which is why an out-of-range arithmetic right shift is a CLAMP
// OF THE COUNT and not a select of the result.
export function evalShift(lhs ParsedInt, count ParsedInt, op MaxonBinOp) returns ParsedInt
	if shiftCountIsNegative(count) 'negative'
		panic("evalShift: negative count {count} — a negative count is E2054 and must never reach the folder")
	end 'negative'

	if op == MaxonBinOp.shr 'arithmetic'
		// The clamp IS the saturation: `lhs shr 63` already is the sign, repeated, which is
		// exactly what shifting every bit out of a signed value leaves behind.
		if count >= ShiftCountBits 'saturate'
			return lhs shr MaxUnguardedShiftCount
		end 'saturate'

		return lhs shr count
	end 'arithmetic'

	if not shiftOperator(op) 'notAShift'
		panic("evalShift: {op.rawValue} is not a shift — only `shl`/`shr`/`shrLogical` have a count")
	end 'notAShift'

	// Both ZERO-FILLING shifts — `shl` and `shrLogical` — shift every bit out for an out-of-range
	// count, so both saturate to 0.
	if count >= ShiftCountBits 'shiftedOut'
		return 0
	end 'shiftedOut'

	if op == MaxonBinOp.shl 'left'
		return lhs shl count
	end 'left'

	// A LOGICAL right shift, folded with the only right shift a `ParsedInt` HAS — which is the
	// ARITHMETIC one, because a `ParsedInt` is signed. So: shift, then CLEAR the high bits the sign
	// replicated into. `64 - count` bits survive a shift by `count`, and the mask keeps exactly
	// those.
	//
	// `count == 0` needs no special case, and that is not luck — it is this language's own rule,
	// stated at the top of this file: `1 shl 64` shifts every bit out and is 0, so the mask is
	// `0 - 1`, every bit kept, and the shift is the identity it should be.
	let survivingBits = (1 shl (ShiftCountBits - count)) - 1
	return (lhs shr count) and survivingBits
end 'evalShift'

// The result of asking the parser's constant folder to evaluate an operator: either the integer it
// computed, or a refusal. A refusal is NOT a failure — it is the folder declining to answer a
// question whose answer would be a DIAGNOSTIC rather than a value.
export union FoldedInt
	folded(value ParsedInt)
	notFoldable
end 'FoldedInt'

// Fold `lhs <op> rhs` over two integers the parser already holds.
//
// `div`/`mod` by zero are `notFoldable`: their answer is a runtime trap, not a number, and a folder
// that invented one would be writing down a wrong answer as a constant. The SHIFTS are
// `notFoldable` here too, and deliberately: their rule is `evalShift`'s, it needs the count checked
// FIRST (a negative one is E2054), and `Parser.emitShift` is the one place that asks. A second
// shift fold here is exactly the drift this file exists to prevent.
export function foldIntBinOp(op MaxonBinOp, lhs ParsedInt, rhs ParsedInt) returns FoldedInt
	return match op 'o'
		add gives FoldedInt.folded(lhs + rhs)
		sub gives FoldedInt.folded(lhs - rhs)
		mul gives FoldedInt.folded(lhs * rhs)
		div gives foldIntDivision(op, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs)
		mod gives foldIntDivision(op, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs)
		bitAnd gives FoldedInt.folded(lhs and rhs)
		bitOr gives FoldedInt.folded(lhs or rhs)
		bitXor gives FoldedInt.folded(lhs xor rhs)
		shl to shrLogical gives FoldedInt.notFoldable
		// `min`/`max` are FLOAT-only, so neither has an integer fold to compute — and neither ever
		// reaches here anyway: `Parser.recordBinOpFold` folds only when `tagIsInteger(resultTag)`, and
		// their result tag is float by construction. The arity-1 math intrinsics decline in
		// `foldIntUnaryOp` for the same reason and in the same words (`trunc to abs gives
		// FoldedInt.notFoldable`). shv2 has no float constant domain at all yet, so there is nothing to
		// fold INTO.
		min to max gives FoldedInt.notFoldable
		// The unsigned divisions fold through the SAME door as their signed twins, which is what keeps
		// the folder and the emitter agreeing about one operator: `foldIntDivision` reads the opcode's
		// own signedness rather than being told it a second time.
		divUnsigned to modUnsigned gives foldIntDivision(op, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs)
	end 'o'
end 'foldIntBinOp'

// `div`/`mod`, which are the only binary integer operators that can FAIL — and they do not fail on the
// same inputs, which is A1x's whole thesis arriving in the constant folder.
//
// A ZERO divisor has no answer for either: `a / 0` and `a mod 0` are E3103, and folding one would be
// writing down a value the program is about to be refused for.
//
// ⭐⭐ **`i64.min / -1` HAS NO ANSWER; `i64.min mod -1` HAS ONE, AND IT IS `0` (A1x).** This function used
// to refuse the pair for BOTH operators — the same false generalization the specs carried, one tier down.
// The quotient `i64.max + 1` is unrepresentable, so `div` still declines. The REMAINDER is `0` for every
// dividend, so declining it here would leave the folder disagreeing with the language it folds for: the
// runtime answers `0` (the `mod` overflow guard), and a constant domain that called the same expression
// unknowable would lose diagnostics the runtime's answer earns — see `Parser.recordBinOpFold`.
function foldIntDivision(op MaxonBinOp, lhs ParsedInt, rhs ParsedInt) returns FoldedInt
	if rhs == 0 'noAnswerForEither'
		return FoldedInt.notFoldable
	end 'noAnswerForEither'

	if divisionIsUnsigned(op) 'unsignedReading'
		return foldUnsignedIntDivision(op, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs)
	end 'unsignedReading'

	if op == MaxonBinOp.div and lhs == i64.min and rhs == OverflowingDivisorPattern 'unrepresentableQuotient'
		return FoldedInt.notFoldable
	end 'unrepresentableQuotient'

	// The host `mod` below would trap on exactly the pair above, so the remainder's own answer at it is
	// returned here rather than computed: `a mod -1` is `a - (-1)·trunc(a / -1)` = `a - a`, and the
	// intermediate that does not fit is one the remainder never needed.
	if lhs == i64.min and rhs == OverflowingDivisorPattern 'remainderAtTheOverflowPair'
		return FoldedInt.folded(0)
	end 'remainderAtTheOverflowPair'

	// rhs is guarded non-zero (and not the i64.min/-1 overflow) above, so these never fail. The
	// divisor is any non-zero ParsedInt — BOTH signs — which no single contiguous ranged type can
	// express (non-zero is not a range), so the guaranteed-safe divide/remainder ride `try`; the
	// `otherwise` arms are unreachable, so they PANIC rather than fold a wrong answer — a silent
	// `otherwise 0` would fold to 0 if the guard above ever regressed.
	if op == MaxonBinOp.div 'quotient'
		return FoldedInt.folded(try (lhs / rhs) otherwise panic("foldIntDivision: divisor was 0 past the zero guard"))
	end 'quotient'

	return FoldedInt.folded(try (lhs mod rhs) otherwise panic("foldIntDivision: divisor was 0 past the zero guard"))
end 'foldIntDivision'

// ⭐⭐ **THE FOLDER AND THE EMITTER MUST AGREE, AND THIS IS THE HALF THAT IS EASY TO LEAVE OUT.** The
// same two bit patterns are two different numbers under the two readings, so a signed fold of an
// operator the backend emits UNSIGNED is a constant that contradicts the instruction beside it:
// `-1 / 8` is `0` read signed and `2305843009213693951` read unsigned, and the second is what the
// emitted `div` computes. MEASURED before this existed — `let c = -1 as int(0 to u64.max)` then
// `c / 8` — and a constant-folded division disagreeing with a runtime one is worse than the bug the
// signedness rule fixes, because only one of the two answers is ever visible in a given program.
//
// ⚠ **NEITHER `i64.min / -1` CASE EXISTS HERE, and that is not an omission.** Read unsigned those two
// patterns are `2^63 / (2^64 - 1)`, whose quotient is `0` and whose remainder is `2^63` — both
// perfectly representable — so the signed arm's overflow refusal has nothing to refuse. The hardware
// agrees on every target: x64's `div` reads a ZEROED RDX so no quotient can overflow, and arm64's
// `udiv` and wasm's `i64.div_u` have no overflow trap at all.
//
// The arithmetic runs in `MachineWord` — `int(0 to u64.max)`, Maxon's unsigned 64-bit type — so the
// compiler that builds THIS compiler emits for these two lines the same `div` the compiled program
// will. Both casts are guard-free in both compilers: `rangeIsFull` names the signed-full and the
// unsigned-full spans as the two that hold every 64-bit pattern, so neither direction can be out of
// range. (VERIFIED against the bootstrap on this exact idiom: `-1 as int(0 to u64.max)` divided by 3
// answers 6148914691236517205 and casts back without a check.)
function foldUnsignedIntDivision(op MaxonBinOp, lhs ParsedInt, rhs ParsedInt) returns FoldedInt
	let dividend = lhs as MachineWord
	let divisor = rhs as MachineWord

	// `rhs` is guarded non-zero by the caller, so neither divide can fail; the `otherwise` arms PANIC
	// rather than fold a wrong answer, exactly as the signed arm's do and for its stated reason.
	if divisionReadsRemainder(op) 'remainder'
		return FoldedInt.folded(try (dividend mod divisor) otherwise panic("foldUnsignedIntDivision: divisor was 0 past the zero guard"))
	end 'remainder'

	return FoldedInt.folded(try (dividend / divisor) otherwise panic("foldUnsignedIntDivision: divisor was 0 past the zero guard"))
end 'foldUnsignedIntDivision'

// Fold `lhs <op> rhs` over two float BIT PATTERNS with the host's native f64 — the float twin of
// `foldIntBinOp`, and the ONE place a constant float arithmetic answer is computed. Each operand is
// reinterpreted to f64 (`__Builtins.bitsToFloat`), the host combines them, and the single result is
// re-encoded (`__Builtins.floatToBits`) — ONE round-trip, so no double-rounding.
//
// Only `+ - * /` reach here. They are exactly the operators with an SSE form (`operatorIsIntegral` is
// false for them); `requireBinOpOperands` turns a float away from `mod` / the bit ops / the shifts
// before a fold is asked (matching shv2's own runtime, which rejects float `mod` as E3005), so the
// integral-only band is unreachable and panics rather than folding a wrong answer.
//
// ⚠ **`/` IS NOT ROUTED THROUGH THE INTEGER DIV-BY-ZERO GUARD** — a float's `rhs == 0` bit test would
// miss `-0.0` and misread the pattern for a magnitude. Instead the division rides the HOST's own trap:
// the bootstrap runtime that runs this compiler faults f64 `/ 0`, so a zero divisor surfaces as a throw
// and becomes `notFoldable` — the constant is then rejected as div-by-zero, symmetric with the integer
// path. (A documented divergence from shv2's emitted `divsd`, which yields ±inf/nan: a constant cannot,
// because the host gives no float `/0` value and this rung does not reimplement IEEE division in
// software.)
export function foldFloatBinOp(op MaxonBinOp, lhs ParsedInt, rhs ParsedInt) returns FoldedInt
	let a = __Builtins.bitsToFloat(lhs)
	let b = __Builtins.bitsToFloat(rhs)

	if op == MaxonBinOp.add 'add'
		return FoldedInt.folded(__Builtins.floatToBits(a + b))
	end 'add'

	if op == MaxonBinOp.sub 'sub'
		return FoldedInt.folded(__Builtins.floatToBits(a - b))
	end 'sub'

	if op == MaxonBinOp.mul 'mul'
		return FoldedInt.folded(__Builtins.floatToBits(a * b))
	end 'mul'

	if op == MaxonBinOp.div 'div'
		let quotient = try (a / b) otherwise 'divByZero'
			return FoldedInt.notFoldable
		end 'divByZero'

		return FoldedInt.folded(__Builtins.floatToBits(quotient))
	end 'div'

	panic("foldFloatBinOp: '{op.rawValue}' has no float reading — requireBinOpOperands turns a float away from `mod` / the bit ops / the shifts before this fold is reached")
end 'foldFloatBinOp'

// Fold a unary operator over an integer the parser already holds. `logicalNot` is a BOOL operator —
// the parser has already resolved which `not` the source meant (see MaxonUnaryOp), so meeting it on
// an integer is a parser bug, not a fold to decline.
export function foldIntUnaryOp(op MaxonUnaryOp, operand ParsedInt) returns FoldedInt
	return match op 'o'
		neg gives FoldedInt.folded(-operand)
		bitNot gives FoldedInt.folded(not operand)
		logicalNot gives FoldedInt.notFoldable
		// The CONVERSIONS never fold, and neither arm is an omission.
		//
		// `trunc` cannot ARRIVE here with an integer to fold: `Parser.emitTrunc` answers
		// `trunc(int)` with the operand itself and emits no op at all, so the only `trunc` in the
		// stream has a float operand — whose `ParsedInt` is an IEEE BIT PATTERN, not the number.
		// Folding it as an integer is precisely the wrong answer this column is gated against.
		//
		// `promoteToFloat` is unreachable for the structural reason: this folder runs only when
		// `tagIsInteger(resultTag)` (see `Parser.emitUnaryOp`), and a promotion's result tag is
		// float by construction. Constant-folding a promotion would mean computing an IEEE
		// encoding at compile time — a real thing to want, and a `ParsedInt` column that cannot
		// say which of its entries are bit patterns is not the place to put it.
		//
		// The five MATH INTRINSICS are unreachable for `promoteToFloat`'s structural reason, twice
		// over: their result tag is float by construction, and `Parser.emitMathIntrinsic` has already
		// promoted an integer argument, so neither the result nor the operand is an integer by the
		// time this folder could run. Folding them would mean evaluating
		// `sqrt`/`floor`/`ceil`/`round`/`abs` on IEEE bit patterns at compile time — a real thing to
		// want, and not something a `ParsedInt` column that cannot say which entries are bit patterns
		// may do. (`abs` alone WOULD be a pure bit operation on that pattern, but the column's
		// ambiguity is what forbids it, not the arithmetic.) `fneg` is `abs`'s case exactly — a bit
		// FLIP rather than a bit clear — and is refused for the same reason; a `-K` over a float
		// constant is folded by `evalConstUnary`'s own float arm instead.
		//
		// `floatToBits` is refused for `abs`'s reason and no other, and it is the sharpest case of it:
		// its fold WOULD BE THE IDENTITY — a float operand's `ParsedInt` already IS the bit pattern this
		// op answers with. Folding it would mean this column asserting of one entry that it holds a
		// pattern rather than a number, which is exactly the distinction its header says it cannot make.
		//
		// `bitsToFloat` is refused for `promoteToFloat`'s STRUCTURAL reason and not `floatToBits`'s: its
		// result tag is float by construction, so this folder — which runs only when
		// `tagIsInteger(resultTag)` — is never asked. Its fold would be the identity too, and from the
		// other side of the same ambiguity: the answer it would hand back is a bit pattern this column
		// would go on carrying as a number.
		trunc to bitsToFloat gives FoldedInt.notFoldable
		// The SIGN-EXTENSION family is unreachable here for a STRUCTURAL reason, and its fold would be a real
		// answer rather than an ambiguous one: `Parser.emitSignExtendFromSlot` is the only emit site, its
		// operand is the result of a runtime element-load CALL, and a call result is never a value this
		// compiler holds a constant for. It is `notFoldable` because there is nothing to fold, not because
		// folding it would be wrong — which is why it does not join the range above.
		signExtendI8 to signExtendI32 gives FoldedInt.notFoldable
	end 'o'
end 'foldIntUnaryOp'

// The result type of a WORD operator (`and`/`or`/`xor`) — the one place the bitwise/logical
// duality is resolved, ported from maxon-selfhosted's `binOpOperandType` (Parser.maxon:
// 11534-11542):
//
//   • either operand `bool` ⇒ `boolean`   — the LOGICAL reading, whose result is a bool
//   • either operand unresolved ⇒ `unresolved` — defer rather than guess; forcing `integer`
//     here is what made v1 type a `boolA or boolB` as an int and reject it against a `bool`
//     parameter
//   • otherwise ⇒ `integer`               — the BITWISE reading
//
// ⚠ THE FIRST ARM IS AN OPTIMISTIC GUESS, NOT A THEOREM, AND THE ORDER OF THE TWO ARMS IS THE
// GUESS. The operands have been checked to AGREE by the time this runs (`checkBinOpOperands`) —
// but agreement is satisfied by DEFERRAL, so `(boolean, unresolved)` still reaches here, and
// "either operand is a bool" does NOT imply "both operands are bools". The bool-first order
// therefore answers `boolean` for a pair whose right operand it could not type.
//
// **THAT USED TO BE A WRONG ANSWER, AND IS NOT ONE ANY MORE — because of what `unresolved` now
// MEANS, not because of anything in this function.** `flag and crossFileInt()` minted a merge phi
// tagged `boolean` carrying the int 7, and `if m` then branched on 7: a value the compiler
// believed was a bool and was not. The fix was UPWARD — the whole-program signature index
// (`Queries.queryProgramSignatures`) — after which a callee some file declares is never
// `unresolved`. What remains reachable is a callee NO file declares, and that program is rejected
// (E3004) before it can run, so the false tag has nothing left to lie to.
//
// Deferring here instead — checking `unresolved` FIRST — would make the tag honest, and is STILL
// DELIBERATELY NOT DONE: it would type `flag and crossFileBool()` as unknown, and `not` refuses an
// unknown operand (see `notOperatorFor`), so `if not (ready and enabled())` — a CORRECT program —
// would stop compiling. Trading a rejected correct program for an honest tag on a program that is
// already rejected is a bad trade in both directions.
function wordOpResultTag(left ValueTypeTag, right ValueTypeTag) returns ValueTypeTag
	if tagIsBool(left) or tagIsBool(right) 'logical'
		return ValueTypeTag.boolean
	end 'logical'
	if tagIsUnresolved(left) or tagIsUnresolved(right) 'defer'
		return ValueTypeTag.unresolved
	end 'defer'
	return ValueTypeTag.integer
end 'wordOpResultTag'

// ⭐ **NUMERIC PROMOTION** — the type an ARITHMETIC operator yields, and THE one place the
// int→float half of the implicit-conversion lattice is stated.
//
// `float` wins over every integer type, because that is the direction the conversion is exact
// in: every i32 and every ranged alias's value is representable as an f64, while the reverse
// truncates. specs/implicit-type-conversion.md states the rule ("an int promotes to a float") and
// specs-shv2/float-type.md's `float-promotion` pins it — `let x = 5` / `let y = 2.0` / `x + y` is
// **7.0**, not 7 and not 5.
//
// ⚠ **A PROMOTED RESULT TAG IS A PROMISE THE EMITTER MUST KEEP.** This function answers a TYPE
// question, and answering it `float` obliges whoever emits the op to have already MOVED the
// integer operand into the float domain — an i64 in a GPR and an f64 in an XMM are not two
// readings of one value, they are two different registers, and `addsd` cannot read the first.
// Saying `float` here while a raw integer still reaches the op is a SILENT WRONG ANSWER, not a
// missing feature. `Parser.emitBinOp` is the emitter, and it is what gates on this.
//
// The `unresolved` case is deliberately NOT deferred to, and this is the one asymmetry with
// `wordOpResultTag` (whose header argues the same trade at length): `integer` has been the answer
// for a mixed `(integer, unresolved)` pair since M3, an `unresolved` operand can only be a call
// no file declares (E3004, which gates the pipeline before codegen), and widening the deferral
// here would buy an honest tag on a program that is already rejected at the cost of moving a
// live rule.
function arithResultTag(left ValueTypeTag, right ValueTypeTag) returns ValueTypeTag
	if operandsAreFloat(left, right: right) 'promote'
		return ValueTypeTag.float
	end 'promote'

	return ValueTypeTag.integer
end 'arithResultTag'

// ⭐⭐ **MAY THESE TWO VALUES BE COMPARED?** — and the answer is NOT `typesAgree`, which is the
// whole reason this function exists.
//
// **ARITHMETIC PROMOTES. A COMPARISON DOES NOT.** `5 + 2.0` is 7.0 — `arithResultTag` promotes the
// int, and specs-shv2/float-type.md's `float-promotion` pins it. `f > i` is **E3005**, and
// specs/contextual-literal-typing.md pins THAT, four times over. Both are canonical Maxon and they
// are not in tension: an arithmetic operator produces a VALUE, and promoting to make one is a
// conversion the author can see in the result's type; a comparison produces a BOOL, so a promotion
// inside it is invisible — `3.5 > i` would silently answer a question about `3.5 > 2.0` that the
// author never asked, and the int's own type would be nowhere in the answer.
//
// ⚠ **`typesAgree` CANNOT BE THE LEVER, AND MUST NOT BECOME IT.** An int and a float AGREE, and
// they have to — `5 + 2.0` would not compile otherwise. Agreement is a CLASS question; this is a
// DOMAIN question, and they are the third distinct question this file now answers about one pair
// of tags (`typesAgree` for an operator's two operands, `checkDeclaredType` for a value meeting a
// declared type, this for a comparison). Collapsing any two of them re-breaks the other.
//
// ⚠ AND IT IS THE MIRROR OF `checkDeclaredType`, NOT THE SAME RULE. There, a float meeting a
// declared int is rejected in ONE direction (int → float is fine, being lossless). Here NEITHER
// direction converts. They look alike and unifying them would silently re-legalise `f > i` or
// re-break `takeFloat(5)`.
//
// P1.0d.4 INTRODUCED this bug and this is the fix: before floats, a mixed compare was unreachable,
// and the promotion added for arithmetic reached `emitCompare` because both went through one
// helper.
export function comparableOperands(left ValueTypeTag, right ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	if not typesAgree(left, right: right) 'disagree'
		return false
	end 'disagree'

	// An `unresolved` operand DEFERS here exactly as it does in `typesAgree` — it is a call to a
	// function no file declares, the program is already E3004 and never reaches codegen, and
	// naming a DOMAIN for a type nobody could pin would report a second, invented diagnostic for
	// it. Tested before the domain compare because `tagIsFloat(unresolved)` is `false`, which
	// would otherwise read as "an integer" and reject `unknown() > 3.5`.
	if tagIsUnresolved(left) or tagIsUnresolved(right) 'defer'
		return true
	end 'defer'

	// ⚠ **TWO STRUCTS AGREE AND STILL DO NOT COMPARE**, and this is the third question about one
	// pair of tags that `typesAgree` cannot answer (see the header). They share a class, so the
	// agreement gate above passes them; `tagIsFloat` is false for both, so the domain test below
	// would pass them too — and `p == q` would lower to a compare of two BOX ADDRESSES. That is
	// REFERENCE equality silently answering a question the author asked about VALUES: `Point.create(3,
	// y: 4) == Point.create(3, y: 4)` would be **false**. Structural equality is a real feature with a
	// real definition (field-by-field, recursively) and shv2 does not have it, so the honest move is
	// to reject the operator rather than to answer a different question with it.
	if operandsAreStructRef(left, right: right) 'noStructuralEquality'
		return false
	end 'noStructuralEquality'

	// ⚠⚠ **TWO EXISTENTIALS AGREE AND STILL DO NOT COMPARE — THE STRUCT RULE ABOVE, ONE TAG OVER, AND IT WAS
	// MISSING.** `typeClassOf` gives `interfaceRef` its own `TypeClass.interfaceValue`, so a pair of them passes
	// the agreement gate; neither is a float, so the domain test below passed them too — and `c == other`
	// lowered to an integer compare of the two FAT POINTERS' value halves. MEASURED on two `Wrapped(7)` boxes,
	// identically on x64 and wasm: `7 == 7` answered **false**, `7 != 7` **true**, `7 >= 7` **false**, and
	// `a(2) > b(1)` flipped its answer when the two allocations were reordered. Reference identity silently
	// answering a question about VALUES — word for word the hazard the paragraph above refuses for structs.
	//
	// ⚠ It is also the OPERATOR half of a rule whose METHOD half was already closed: dispatching a `Self`-typed
	// requirement on an existential is refused by `Parser.requireWitnessSelfArgs`, because two values held at
	// one interface may have different dynamic types and nothing can prove they match — which is exactly what
	// `==` and `<` need. Both doors now say so, from the one sentence in
	// `IrInterface.ExistentialPairUnprovableReason`.
	if operandsAreInterfaceRef(left, right: right) 'noExistentialIdentity'
		return false
	end 'noExistentialIdentity'

	// ⚠ **TWO STRINGS AGREE AND STILL DO NOT `comparableOperands`**, and this is what keeps ORDERING
	// (`s < t`, `s >= t`) out of the language. `==`/`!=` on strings never reach here — `Parser.emitCompare`
	// special-cases a both-`string` pair at its TOP, routing it to the `__str_eq` runtime call before
	// this rule is asked. So the only comparison that arrives here with a `string` operand is an
	// ORDERING one (which has no meaning on a byte pointer) or a `string`-vs-non-`string` mismatch
	// (already rejected by the agreement gate above). Both are `false`. The bootstrap rejects ordering
	// on String identically (MEASURED: *"operator '<' is not defined for type 'String'"*).
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **IT ASKS `operandsAreText`, NOT `operandsAreByteRecord`, AND THAT NARROWING IS THE WHOLE OF
	// A5m-c'S TYPE RULE.** The two predicates were one until a `Character` gained its ordering, and asking
	// the REPRESENTATION question here would refuse `c < d` for a type the corpus declares `Comparable`;
	// asking it in `lacksIntegralReading` is still right, because neither byte record is a MAGNITUDE.
	// **A rule keyed on the representation and a rule keyed on the type are two questions, and this is the
	// pair that proves it** — see `tagIsByteRecord`, which says the same thing about the memory sites.
	if operandsAreText(left, right: right) 'noStringOrdering'
		return false
	end 'noStringOrdering'

	// Two floats compare; two integers compare; two bools compare (they agreed, and neither is a
	// float). One of each does not.
	return tagIsFloat(left) == tagIsFloat(right)
end 'comparableOperands'

// **DO THESE TWO OPERANDS MEET IN THE FLOAT DOMAIN?** The promotion rule asked as a yes/no, and
// THE one place the "either side" half of it is written down.
//
// `arithResultTag` answers the same question as a TYPE, and a comparison cannot read the answer
// off a result tag — `x > y` is a bool whatever its operands were — so the predicate has to exist
// separately from the type. Two callers, one sentence: promote iff either side is already there.
export function operandsAreFloat(left ValueTypeTag, right ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	return tagIsFloat(left) or tagIsFloat(right)
end 'operandsAreFloat'

// True for the tag of a declared struct type, and ONLY for it — the same DEFINITELY question
// `tagIsBool` / `tagIsInteger` / `tagIsFloat` ask, for the type that is a POINTER rather than a
// number.
export function tagIsStructRef(tag ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	return tag == ValueTypeTag.structRef
end 'tagIsStructRef'

// True for the tag of a `String`, and ONLY for it — the same DEFINITELY question `tagIsBool` /
// `tagIsInteger` / `tagIsStructRef` ask, for the builtin that is a POINTER to a byte record and whose
// only comparison is a runtime byte `==`.
export function tagIsText(tag ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	return tag == ValueTypeTag.string
end 'tagIsText'

// ⭐⭐ **IS THIS TAG'S VALUE A POINTER TO THE FUSED BYTE RECORD?** — the `String` record
// (`StringRuntime`'s 56-byte layout: `buffer@0`, `length@8`, `capacity@16`, `parent@32`, `singleByteGraphemes@48`),
// which a `String` and a `Character` BOTH are.
//
// ⚠ **THIS IS A QUESTION ABOUT REPRESENTATION, AND `tagIsText` IS A QUESTION ABOUT TYPE. THEY WERE ONE
// PREDICATE, AND THAT WAS THE DEFECT.** Before P1.8 slice B there was exactly one byte-record type, so
// `tagIsText` answered both and nothing could tell that it was two questions wearing one name. Adding a
// second byte-record type is what separated them — and it separated them SILENTLY, because
// `tagIsText` is a one-line `tag == ValueTypeTag.string` that simply answers "no" for a new tag. Three
// of its 29 call sites would have been a LEAK (`Parser.valueIsManagedHeap` — the one owned-temp
// adoption test, so every `Character` temporary is never dropped), a WRONG DROP CALLEE
// (`Parser.decrefCalleeFor`), and a POINTER COMPARE for `c1 == c2` (`Parser.emitCompare`). All three
// are measured by sabotage — see `specs-shv2/character-ownership.md`.
//
// ⇒ **THE RULE FOR CHOOSING BETWEEN THEM.** Ask `tagIsByteRecord` when the answer decides what happens
// to the MEMORY — is it dropped (`__str_decref`), cloned (`__str_clone`), promoted by a fused-record
// blit, moved into durable storage, or blitted into an interpolation. Ask `tagIsText` when the answer
// decides what the TYPE may do — may this be `print`ed, may it be a `panic` message, is this Set's key
// a String, is this array literal's element a String, **may this be ORDERED** (`operandsAreText`, A5m-c:
// a Character is, a String is not). A site that is asking "is the memory mine?" and spells it
// `tagIsText` is the bug this comment exists to prevent recurring.
export function tagIsByteRecord(tag ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	return tagIsText(tag) or tag == ValueTypeTag.character
end 'tagIsByteRecord'

// ⭐ **DOES A VALUE OF THIS TAG HAVE AN OBJECT IDENTITY — the question `is` / `is not` asks?**
// (`specs/reference-identity.md`.) True for exactly the tags whose runtime value IS the address of one
// object, so that "the same object" is a question the machine can answer by comparing the two words:
// a declared struct's box, a generic instance's record (`Array`, `Set`, …), and the fused byte record a
// `String` and a `Character` both are.
//
// ⚠ **EVERY OTHER TAG IS REFUSED, AND THE REFUSALS ARE NOT ALL THE SAME REFUSAL. The list is exhaustive
// on purpose: a tag a reader cannot find here reads as one nobody considered.**
//
//   • `boolean` / `integer` / `float` / `exitCode` — values, with no object anywhere. The spec's own
//     wording, and the whole of what E3068 says.
//   • `typeParameter` — an opaque word inside a shared body, which may turn out to be either.
//   • `unresolved` / `void` — name no type at all.
//   • `named` — an ENUM's value IS its i64 tag, so that half is a value again. **But a `union`'s value is
//     a one-word pointer to an `__mm_alloc` box, and it is refused for a DIFFERENT reason**: no spec on
//     any of the three compilers pins an identity reading for a union, and both references refuse it by
//     the same "is the operand a struct value?" test this predicate states (2-Parser.cs:27423). Admitting
//     it would be shv2 inventing a semantics rather than porting one.
//   • `function` — likewise a genuine one-word address (a `.text` entry), and likewise unpinned. It has
//     the extra wrinkle that two spellings of one non-capturing function legitimately share an address
//     while two closures over one body do not, so "the same object" is not even settled for it.
//   • `interfaceRef` — a FAT pointer, so a one-word compare would answer about its value half alone and
//     silently ignore the witness. The same half-an-answer `emitCompare` already refuses an existential
//     pair for.
//
// ⇒ Widening this admits a POINTER COMPARE, never a type error, so a tag added here without a reading
// is a wrong answer rather than a missing one.
export function tagHasReferenceIdentity(tag ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	return tagIdentityIsNominal(tag) or tagIsByteRecord(tag)
end 'tagHasReferenceIdentity'

// Of the tags that HAVE an identity, which ones name a FAMILY of types rather than one type? A
// `structRef` is any declared struct and a `genericInstance` is any instantiation, so two operands
// sharing either tag can still be different types (`Point` against `Box`, `Array with int` against
// `Array with String`) and the declared NAME has to be compared as well. The two byte records do not:
// there is exactly one `String` type and exactly one `Character` type, so for them a matching tag IS a
// matching type — the same reason `emitCompare`'s byte-record arm asks nothing beyond `leftTag == rightTag`.
export function tagIdentityIsNominal(tag ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	return tagIsStructRef(tag) or tag == ValueTypeTag.genericInstance
end 'tagIdentityIsNominal'

// **DOES EITHER OPERAND NAME A `String`?** `operandsAreStructRef`'s twin, and it exists for the same
// reason: an operator with no reading for a String on one side has none with it on the other.
//
// ⚠ **IT ASKS THE TYPE QUESTION (`tagIsText`), NOT THE REPRESENTATION ONE, AND IT USED TO ASK THE OTHER.**
// It was `operandsAreByteRecord` — `tagIsByteRecord` on either side — with `lacksIntegralReading` and
// `comparableOperands` reading it as one sentence, which was exact only while NEITHER byte record was
// ordered. `stdlib/Character.maxon:19` declares `Comparable` and `stdlib/String.maxon:49` does not, so the
// two rules now differ and each asks its own: ORDERING is a fact about the TYPE (one byte record has it,
// the other does not), while an INTEGRAL READING is a fact about the REPRESENTATION (a pointer to a record
// is not a magnitude, whichever type it is) and stays on `tagIsByteRecord` in `lacksIntegralReading`.
//
// Equality is the one operator BOTH byte records have, and it never reaches either rule —
// `Parser.emitCompare` intercepts a matching byte-record pair at its top.
function operandsAreText(left ValueTypeTag, right ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	return tagIsText(left) or tagIsText(right)
end 'operandsAreText'

// **DOES EITHER OPERAND NAME A STRUCT?** `operandsAreFloat`'s twin, and it exists for the same
// reason: the operators that have no reading for one of them have no reading for the other, and
// both `integralOperandsFor` and `comparableOperands` have to ask.
function operandsAreStructRef(left ValueTypeTag, right ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	return tagIsStructRef(left) or tagIsStructRef(right)
end 'operandsAreStructRef'

// True for the tag of a value held at an INTERFACE type — the same DEFINITELY question `tagIsStructRef`
// asks, for the type that is a FAT POINTER rather than a plain one.
function tagIsInterfaceRef(tag ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	return tag == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef
end 'tagIsInterfaceRef'

// ⭐ **BOTH operands held at an interface — and BOTH, where `operandsAreStructRef` says EITHER.** The two
// are not the same shape and the difference is the point: an existential against an `int` is a genuine
// TYPE MISMATCH and already reads as one, while a PAIR of them agrees perfectly and still cannot be
// compared. Asking `or` here would work only because `typesAgree` runs first — one fact resting on another
// function's ordering — and it would give the mixed pair the wrong explanation at the parser's door, which
// is exactly what it did before this was one predicate rather than two spellings.
export function operandsAreInterfaceRef(left ValueTypeTag, right ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	return tagIsInterfaceRef(left) and tagIsInterfaceRef(right)
end 'operandsAreInterfaceRef'

// ⭐ **WHAT A FLOAT ZERO IS, AS BITS** — IEEE-754 `+0.0`, whose encoding is all 64 bits clear.
//
// It lives beside `BoolTrueValue`/`BoolFalseValue` because it is the same kind of fact: the one
// place a Maxon value of a given type becomes the number the compiler carries for it. Its reader is
// `Parser.emitAliasFreeCopy`, which spells a float copy as `v - 0.0` (an exact identity on every
// input, signed zeroes included) — so the zero it subtracts and the bit pattern a float literal carries
// are one constant, not two. Negation does NOT read it: `-x` is a sign-bit flip (`MaxonUnaryOp.fneg`),
// because `0.0 - (+0.0)` is `+0.0`.
export let FloatZeroBits = 0 as ParsedInt

// The type a `binOp` yields. The ARITHMETIC family (`+`/`-`/`*`/`/`/`mod`) promotes (see
// `arithResultTag`); the shifts are integral by definition on both sides (v1 says the same of
// `shl`/`shr`/`mod`: "integer-only by definition", Parser.maxon:11524). Only the WORD operators
// follow their operands' bool/int duality.
export function binOpResultTag(op MaxonBinOp, left ValueTypeTag, right ValueTypeTag) returns ValueTypeTag
	return match op 'k'
		add to mod gives arithResultTag(left, right: right)
		bitAnd to bitXor gives wordOpResultTag(left, right: right)
		shl to shrLogical gives ValueTypeTag.integer
		// The unsigned divisions answer exactly what their signed twins answer: signedness selects an
		// INSTRUCTION, and `arithResultTag` is about the DOMAIN the pair meets in, which is unmoved.
		divUnsigned to modUnsigned gives arithResultTag(left, right: right)
		// `min`/`max` PROMOTE exactly as the arithmetic band does, and each reaches this function
		// having been handed two operands already in the float domain — `Parser.emitBinaryMathIntrinsic`
		// promotes both before it emits, the way `emitMathIntrinsic` does for the arity-1 intrinsics.
		// So this answers `float` for every call the language admits. Asking `arithResultTag` rather
		// than naming `float` outright keeps ONE promotion rule: if the pair could ever meet somewhere
		// other than f64, these would meet them there too instead of asserting an answer of their own.
		min to max gives arithResultTag(left, right: right)
	end 'k'
end 'binOpResultTag'

// What is wrong with a binary operator's operands, if anything.
//
// An `enum`, NOT a `union`, and the distinction is measured rather than stylistic: no arm
// carries a payload, and **a Maxon union value is a heap object even for a payload-free case**
// (MaxonDialect's ValueTypeTag header). A union here would put one allocation on EVERY BINARY
// OPERATOR of every expression of every function — the compiler's hottest path, and the exact
// trap that column was rebuilt to escape. `scale-test` charged the union form of this verdict
// +62 allocations on the smallest rung and +1,550 on the largest; as an enum it is a scalar and
// costs nothing.
export enum BinOpOperands
	ok
	// A WORD operator whose operands land in different classes — one bool, one number. The word
	// operators are CONTEXT-DEPENDENT, not polymorphic: `bool <op> bool` is the logical reading
	// and `int <op> int` the bitwise one, but a MIXTURE is neither, and either reading silently
	// discards what the other operand meant. The int-on-the-left direction is the nastier one
	// because it looks like it works: `5 and flag` computes `5 & 1` = 1 and behaves exactly like
	// `flag`, while `4 and flag` computes `4 & 1` = 0 and is ALWAYS FALSE — same shape, opposite
	// behaviour, decided by a bit of the constant.
	mixedWordOperands
	// An ARITHMETIC / DIVISION / SHIFT operator with a non-numeric operand. These are numeric BY
	// DEFINITION on both sides: there is no reading of `4 + flag` at all. So this fires for a
	// bool on EITHER side — and for two bools (`true + false`), which merely AGREE with each
	// other and would sail past a pure agreement rule. Agreement is necessary, not sufficient.
	nonNumericOperands
	// An INTEGRAL-ONLY operator (`mod`, and the whole bit band) with an operand that has no
	// integral reading — a FLOAT or a STRUCT REFERENCE. See `operatorIsIntegral` for which
	// operators those are and why.
	//
	// ⚠ **NUMERIC IS NOT INTEGRAL, AND THIS CASE IS THE DIFFERENCE.** A float and an int share
	// `TypeClass.numeric` — that is exactly what makes `5 + 2.0` a legal program — so a float
	// sails through the `nonNumericOperands` check above and reaches an operator that has no
	// float reading. Without this case `3.14 and 2.0` is typed `integer` by `wordOpResultTag`
	// (whose bool-first/int-default chain has no float arm) and lowers to an `and` over two IEEE
	// bit patterns: it computes a number, reports nothing, and means nothing. `mod` is the same
	// shape one level down — it would ride `StdOp.mod`'s `idiv` over the same bits.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **A STRUCT REACHES IT BY THE OTHER ROUTE, AND `TypeClass` CANNOT STOP IT.** `p and q` is a
	// WORD operator, whose only rule is that its operands AGREE — and two `structRef`s agree (they
	// share a class; see `TypeClass.structRef`). So the `nonNumericOperands` gate is never even
	// asked, `wordOpResultTag`'s int-default types the result `integer`, and the pair lowers to an
	// `and` over two BOX ADDRESSES. Same shape as the float bug, arrived at from the opposite side
	// of `checkBinOpOperands`.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT CANNOT CARRY THE OFFENDING OPERAND — THIS IS AN `enum` FOR A MEASURED REASON** (see
	// the header). The raise site asks `nonIntegralOperandOf` for the tag to NAME, on the failure
	// path only, rather than the hot path paying an allocation to carry a fact only a diagnostic
	// reads. That question has ONE home, next to the rule that decides it.
	nonIntegralOperands
end 'BinOpOperands'

// **WHICH BINARY OPERATORS ARE INTEGRAL BY DEFINITION** — the ones with no float reading at all,
// and so the ones a float operand must be turned away from. THE one place that is decided.
//
// This is not a statement about what shv2 has implemented yet; it is a statement about the
// language, and every one of these is a permanent rejection rather than a gap waiting on an
// instruction. Go draws the identical line, for the identical reasons.
function operatorIsIntegral(op MaxonBinOp) returns bool
	return match op 'k'
		// `+` `-` `*` `/` all have a direct SSE form (`addsd`/`subsd`/`mulsd`/`divsd`), so a float
		// is not merely allowed here — it is the whole point of the rung.
		add to div gives false
		// `mod` does NOT, and it is the one arm that looks like it belongs with its neighbours and
		// does not. x64 has no `fmod` instruction: an IEEE remainder is a RUNTIME CALL (v1 routes
		// it through one), which is a stdlib cone this rung does not have — but the reason to
		// reject it is the LANGUAGE's, not the schedule's, so this arm does not move when the cone
		// lands.
		mod gives true
		// The bit band. A double's bits are a sign/exponent/mantissa TRIPLE, not a magnitude, so a
		// bit operation on them cannot mean what the source said — `3.14 shl 1` is not `6.28`, and
		// `3.14 and 2.0` is not any number the author had in mind.
		bitAnd to shrLogical gives true
		// `min`/`max` are the OPPOSITE of `mod`: not merely float-ADMITTING but float-ONLY, since
		// `specs/min.md` and `specs/max.md` type them `(a float, b float) float` and an integer argument
		// is PROMOTED rather than kept. `false` is therefore the answer that lets a float through, which
		// is the only kind of operand either ever has.
		min to max gives false
		// ⭐ BOTH unsigned divisions are integral, where the signed pair splits: `/` admits a float
		// because `divsd` exists, but an UNSIGNED `/` is minted only from two non-negative INTEGER
		// ranges — a ranged float alias carries tag `float`, never `named`, so it can never reach
		// `divisionOpcodeFor`'s unsigned answer. This is not a rejection the parser needs (the operand
		// rule has already run by then); it is this predicate telling the truth about the operator.
		divUnsigned to modUnsigned gives true
	end 'k'
end 'operatorIsIntegral'

// The operand rule for EVERY binary operator, in one classifier. Which of the two questions is
// asked depends only on the operator's family, and both bottom out in `typeClassesAgree`.
//
// This is the function whose ABSENCE was the bug: the mixed-word-operand rule existed, and
// nothing at all guarded arithmetic, the shifts, or the division family — so `4 + flag`,
// `4 * flag` and `flag shl 4` compiled to arithmetic on the bool's 0/1 payload and returned 5,
// 4 and 16. Every binary operator now comes through here.
export function checkBinOpOperands(op MaxonBinOp, left ValueTypeTag, right ValueTypeTag) returns BinOpOperands
	if wordOperator(op) 'word'
		if not typesAgree(left, right: right) 'mixed'
			return BinOpOperands.mixedWordOperands
		end 'mixed'
		return integralOperandsFor(op, left: left, right: right)
	end 'word'

	if not typeClassAdmits(TypeClass.numeric, tag: left) or not typeClassAdmits(TypeClass.numeric, tag: right) 'nonNumeric'
		return BinOpOperands.nonNumericOperands
	end 'nonNumeric'

	return integralOperandsFor(op, left: left, right: right)
end 'checkBinOpOperands'

// The SECOND gate every binary operator passes, after its own family's rule: an operator with no
// integral reading may not have an operand that has none either.
//
// It runs on BOTH paths rather than only the numeric one, and that is load-bearing: `and`/`or`/
// `xor` are word operators, so they never reach the numeric check — two floats (or two structs)
// AGREE with each other, which is all the word rule asks — and `3.14 and 2.0` / `p and q` would
// otherwise be accepted by the only rule that looked at them. One helper, called from both arms,
// is what keeps the two families from needing two copies of the same sentence.
//
// ⚠ It names the two domains EXPLICITLY rather than asking `not tagIsIntegral(left) or not
// tagIsIntegral(right)`, and that is `tagIsIntegral`'s own warning applied: the composed form is
// TRUE for `unresolved`, which agrees with everything by deferral — so `x and undeclaredCall()`,
// a program already reported as E3004 at its call site, would collect a second, invented
// diagnostic naming a type nobody pinned.
function integralOperandsFor(op MaxonBinOp, left ValueTypeTag, right ValueTypeTag) returns BinOpOperands
	if not operatorIsIntegral(op) 'floatAllowed'
		return BinOpOperands.ok
	end 'floatAllowed'

	if lacksIntegralReading(left) or lacksIntegralReading(right) 'noIntegralReading'
		return BinOpOperands.nonIntegralOperands
	end 'noIntegralReading'

	return BinOpOperands.ok
end 'integralOperandsFor'

// ⭐ **DOES THIS TAG HAVE NO INTEGRAL READING?** THE one statement of the domain
// `BinOpOperands.nonIntegralOperands` covers, and it has TWO readers that must never disagree:
// `integralOperandsFor` (which DECIDES the rejection) and `nonIntegralOperandOf` (which NAMES the
// operand for the message). A float's bits are a sign/exponent/mantissa triple, not a magnitude;
// a struct reference is a box's ADDRESS, not a magnitude either; a byte record's value is the
// ADDRESS of its record, which is the same objection one type further on. None is a bit pattern the
// source meant to mask or shift.
//
// ⚠ **THIS IS THE ONE RULE THAT STAYED ON `tagIsByteRecord` WHEN `comparableOperands` LEFT IT** (A5m-c),
// and the split is the point: a `Character` gained an ORDERING (`stdlib/Character.maxon:80-104`) and gained
// no arithmetic with it, because `c - 1` and `c shl 1` would be reading a record pointer as a number
// whether or not the type is orderable. Both references agree; the oracle answers `c - 1` with a refusal.
//
// ⚠ Written as the union of the two DOMAINS rather than as `not tagIsIntegral(tag)`, per
// `tagIsIntegral`'s own warning: the negated form is TRUE for `unresolved`, which agrees with
// everything by deferral, so `x and undeclaredCall()` — already reported as E3004 at its call
// site — would collect a second, invented diagnostic naming a type nobody pinned.
function lacksIntegralReading(tag ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	return tagIsFloat(tag) or tagIsStructRef(tag) or tagIsByteRecord(tag)
end 'lacksIntegralReading'

// **WHICH OPERAND THE `nonIntegralOperands` DIAGNOSTIC MUST NAME** — the first one with no
// integral reading, left before right.
//
// ⭐ It exists because the raise site used to GUESS, and the guess went stale the moment the rule
// grew: `Parser.requireBinOpOperands` hardcoded `float` under a comment calling it "the only tag
// that can trip this rule", and P1.0r admitted `structRef` to the very same rule — so `p and q`
// reported "but this one is float" about two structs. A rule and the message that reports it are
// one fact, so they are answered from one predicate (`lacksIntegralReading`) rather than from a
// theorem restated at the raise site.
//
// It is asked ONLY on the failure path, which is why the verdict stays a payload-free `enum` (see
// `BinOpOperands`): the hot path must not allocate to carry a tag that only a diagnostic reads.
//
// A precondition, not a case: every caller reached here through a `nonIntegralOperands` verdict,
// which `integralOperandsFor` returns only when one of the two answered `lacksIntegralReading`.
export function nonIntegralOperandOf(left ValueTypeTag, right ValueTypeTag) returns ValueTypeTag
	if lacksIntegralReading(left) 'leftOffends'
		return left
	end 'leftOffends'

	if lacksIntegralReading(right) 'rightOffends'
		return right
	end 'rightOffends'

	panic("nonIntegralOperandOf: neither '{typeTagName(left)}' nor '{typeTagName(right)}' lacks an integral reading — this is only asked after `integralOperandsFor` returned `nonIntegralOperands`, which it does only when one of them does")
end 'nonIntegralOperandOf'

// Which `not` a prefix `not` means, from its operand's type — the LOGICAL one on a bool (flip
// bit 0), the BITWISE one on an integer (complement all 64).
//
// AN UNRESOLVED OPERAND HAS NO SAFE ANSWER, AND SO IS NOT GIVEN ONE. `not` is the one
// type-directed operator whose two readings disagree about the VALUE, not merely about the side
// effects: `and`/`or`/`xor` fall back to the bitwise reading when they cannot pin a type, and on
// the 0/1 bool domain the bitwise answer IS the logical one (only the short-circuit elision is
// lost). `not` has no such luck — the bitwise reading of `false` is -1 and of `true` is -2, and
// BOTH read as TRUE in a condition, so `if not isReady()` would be taken unconditionally. That
// is a silent miscompile, not a degradation, and it is exactly what
// specs/short-circuit-evaluation.md's `not-over-bool-or-with-overloaded-callees` case exists to
// forbid.
//
// So a `not` whose operand's type the parser cannot determine is REJECTED with a positioned
// diagnostic. v1 avoids the choice by emitting a single `logicalNot` and resolving it in its
// lowering, which re-reads the operand type after TypeResolution — a route shv2's Std tier does
// not have, because it keeps no per-value source type (see MaxonUnaryOp).
//
// **The project-wide callee signature query this comment used to ask for has LANDED**
// (`Queries.queryProgramSignatures`), and it drained the case that used to be common: `not
// crossFileBool()` — a correct program — is now typed exactly and compiles. What is left is `not
// bogus()`, a call to a function no file declares, and refusing it is right: `not`'s two readings
// disagree about the VALUE (the bitwise complement of `false` is -1, which reads as TRUE), so
// there is nothing safe to emit, and the program is being rejected anyway.
export union NotOperator
	op(kind MaxonUnaryOp, resultTag ValueTypeTag)
	// The operand's type could not be determined, so neither reading can be chosen.
	unknownOperandType
	// The operand has neither reading — a FLOAT (an IEEE triple is not a magnitude) or a STRUCT
	// REFERENCE (a pointer is not a magnitude either). One arm, because it is one rejection and
	// one message: `not` is defined on the bool domain and on the integer domain, and on nothing
	// else. See `notOperatorFor`.
	nonIntegralOperand
end 'NotOperator'

// A `match`, not an if-chain, so the tag space is covered EXHAUSTIVELY: the fall-through form
// silently answered `bitNot` for any tag it had not been told about, which is the shape of every
// bug this file exists to prevent.
//
// ⚠⚠ **AND THE RANGE ARM PUT THE FALL-THROUGH STRAIGHT BACK.** `float` sits BETWEEN `integer` and
// `exitCode` in `ValueTypeTag`, so `integer to exitCode` silently swallowed it and answered
// `bitNot` — the exact bug the paragraph above congratulates this function for having fixed, in
// the exact function that fixed it, reintroduced by the range that replaced the fall-through.
// A `match` is only exhaustive over the arms you NAME; a range names its endpoints and adopts
// whatever is later inserted between them (MaxonDialect's band-append invariant, which has no
// compiler backstop).
//
// The old comment was not even wrong about the CLASS — `typeClassOf` really does map `float` to
// `numeric`, and that is the point: **`numeric` licenses arithmetic, it does not license
// bit-complementing an IEEE pattern.** `not 3.14` would have flipped all 64 bits of a
// sign/exponent/mantissa triple and called the result an `integer`. Caught before a float could
// reach it; it was unreachable only because no float literal parsed.
export function notOperatorFor(operand ValueTypeTag) returns NotOperator
	return match operand 'k'
		boolean gives NotOperator.op(MaxonUnaryOp.logicalNot, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.boolean)
		// Every INTEGER takes the bitwise reading (`not 5` is -6).
		integer gives NotOperator.op(MaxonUnaryOp.bitNot, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer)
		// A FLOAT has NEITHER reading, and this is the arm the range used to swallow. It is not a
		// missing feature waiting on an instruction: `not` on a double is meaningless in the
		// source language, exactly as `3.14 and 2.0` is (see `BinOpOperands.nonIntegralOperands`),
		// and no hardware would help.
		float gives NotOperator.nonIntegralOperand
		// `named` is a ranged typealias. As of F1 (`Parser.readTypeAliasDeclaration`) it may be a
		// `float(low to high)` one, for which the bitwise reading is WRONG — a double has no `not`,
		// exactly as the `float` arm above records — so this is no longer the theorem `typeClassOf`'s
		// is. It stays bitwise because a float alias is tagged `named` only in a param/field position F1
		// defers to the float ABI (F2): a `not` on such a value crosses register files and the x64
		// allocator PANICS before any wrong answer, rather than complementing a double. `exitCode` is
		// `int(0 to u32.max)`, an integer for real. Making a float alias take the `float` arm's answer
		// needs `named` resolved through the alias registry — that F2 work.
		named to exitCode gives NotOperator.op(MaxonUnaryOp.bitNot, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.integer)
		// `unresolved` — a call to a function no file declares (there is no reading to choose).
		// `void` — a call to a function that returns nothing; `Parser.parseCall` rejects it at the
		// call site, so it cannot arrive here, and if a second route to a void value ever appeared
		// this is still the only truthful answer: there is no operand to complement.
		unresolved to void gives NotOperator.unknownOperandType
		// A STRUCT REFERENCE — rejected for the FLOAT arm's reason, not the `unresolved` arm's: the
		// type is perfectly well known, and `not` simply has no reading on it. Complementing a box's
		// address is not a wrong answer waiting on an instruction, it is not a question. Had a struct
		// been filed under `named`, the arm two lines up would have complemented it and called the
		// result an `integer` — which is precisely the bug the `float` arm above records.
		structRef gives NotOperator.nonIntegralOperand
		// A STRING — rejected for the FLOAT/struct arm's reason: `not` is defined on the bool domain
		// and the integer domain, and on nothing else. Complementing a byte pointer is not a question.
		string gives NotOperator.nonIntegralOperand
		// A FUNCTION value — rejected for the struct/String arm's reason: `not` has no reading on a code
		// pointer (P1.5-A1). Complementing a `.text` address is not a question.
		function gives NotOperator.nonIntegralOperand
		// A TYPE PARAMETER / GENERIC INSTANCE (P1.6-A) — `not` has no reading on an opaque `T` or a struct
		// pointer, rejected for the struct/function arm's reason. A `T` agrees with anything for AGREEMENT,
		// but agreement is not an operator: `not t` is refused here exactly as `t + 1` is elsewhere.
		typeParameter gives NotOperator.nonIntegralOperand
		genericInstance gives NotOperator.nonIntegralOperand
		// `not c` has no reading: a Character is a record pointer, and complementing one produces a
		// number the source did not mean. `string`'s answer.
		character gives NotOperator.nonIntegralOperand
		// `not x` has no reading on an existential — the struct/String/function arm's reason, one level of
		// indirection up: complementing a fat pointer's value half is not a question.
		interfaceRef gives NotOperator.nonIntegralOperand
	end 'k'
end 'notOperatorFor'

// ⭐⭐ **DOES A VALUE'S TAG FIT A CONTAINER SLOT WHOSE DECLARED TYPE IS `declared`?** — THE rule, asked
// by every container column: a `Set`'s key, a `Map`'s key and a `Map`'s value. The three containers word
// their refusals differently (each names the type it is), but the RULE must not fork, which is why only the
// verdict lives here.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS THE TAG HALF ONLY, AND NO HASH-TABLE COLUMN CALLS IT DIRECTLY ANY MORE.** Every one of them
// reaches it through `elementTypePreserved` below, so a column with a NOMINAL identity (a `Map` value
// declared as a struct or an enum) is settled by that identity and never falls to a tag comparison that
// cannot see one. Two enums are both integral here, which is why calling in directly is a wrong answer
// rather than a missed refinement.
//
// ⚠ **`map`'s TRANSFORM IS THE FOURTH READER — BOTH HALVES OF IT — AND IT IS THE SAME QUESTION.** "May this
// value be stored in a slot declared `declared`?" is what element PRESERVATION asks of what the transform
// ANSWERS and of what it TAKES — the array's element column is the slot, and the ranged-alias measurement
// below is the very reason (a closure over `typealias Integer = int(…)` returns `named` where the element
// reads `integer`, and the narrow spelling would refuse `[1,2,3].map(function(x Integer) gives x * 2)` — the
// construct itself). Both halves reach it through `elementTypePreserved` below rather than calling here
// directly, so the RETURN half (decided in the parser) and the PARAMETER half (decided whole-program, after
// merge) cannot come to answer one question two ways.
//
// ⚠ It lives HERE rather than beside the container parsers because it acquired a reader in another tier.
// `SemanticCheck` drains the transform's PARAMETER half, and a rule the parser owned privately would have
// had to be spelled a second time there — the one-fact-twice bug this file exists to hold the cure for.
//
// ⚠ **"IS THIS AN INT?" IS `tagIsIntegral`, NOT `== ValueTypeTag.integer`, AND THE NARROW SPELLING WAS A
// MEASURED FALSE REFUSAL.** A value whose static type is a RANGED ALIAS carries the `named` tag until
// TypeResolution collapses it — which is every parameter and every field read of `typealias Integer =
// int(…)` — so `insert(v)` inside `function add(s IntSet, v Integer)` was rejected as *"`Set with int`
// requires an int key — got a 'int' value"*, a sentence that argues against itself precisely because
// `typeTagName` already reads `named` as `int`.
//
// ⚠ A Character column asks the DEFINITELY question and not `tagIsByteRecord`: a `String` value is a byte
// record too, and hashing one as a Character key would put `"a"` and `'a'` in one bucket under two types.
//
// ⛔⛔ **EVERY TAG `ProgramSignatures.slotTypeFitsOneWord` ADMITS MUST BE ANSWERED HERE — THAT GATE SAYS WHAT
// MAY *BE* A COLUMN AND THIS SAYS WHAT MAY BE *WRITTEN* TO ONE, AND A TAG PRESENT THERE AND ABSENT HERE IS
// ADMITTED AT THE `typealias` AND REFUSED AT EVERY WRITE.** MEASURED, on `Map with (String, bool)` +
// `m.upsert("a", value: true)`: `boolean` passed the gate, reached the integral fall-through below
// (`tagIsIntegral(boolean)` is FALSE by construction — a bool is not a number) and was refused as *"this
// `Map`'s value is a bool — got a 'bool' value"*, a sentence that argues against itself on a program the
// widened value column exists to admit. The two facts live in two files and nothing but this paragraph holds
// them together; widen one and come here.
function columnValueTagMatches(valueTag ValueTypeTag, declared ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	// ⭐ **THE COLUMN TAGS THAT AGREE WITH THEMSELVES AND WITH NOTHING ELSE — one arm, because they differ in
	// no other way.** A `Character`, a `bool` and a FUNCTION are each one machine word dropped by nothing,
	// which is `slotTypeFitsOneWord`'s whole question — but each is its own TYPE, so the integral
	// fall-through below is a wrong answer for all three: it would make `m.upsert("d", value: 7)` on a
	// `Map with (String, UnaryOp)` (`/specs/map.md:function-valued.dispatch`) a code pointer of 7, and
	// `value: 1` on a bool column a `true` nobody wrote. ⚠ The function spelling is reachable only because
	// the caller RESOLVES the column type first (`Parser.parseHashTableColumnArg`): the alias arrives as
	// `named`, which reads integral.
	if declared == ValueTypeTag.character or declared == ValueTypeTag.boolean or declared == ValueTypeTag.function 'agreesOnlyWithItself'
		return valueTag == declared
	end 'agreesOnlyWithItself'
	if tagIsText(declared) 'textColumn'
		return tagIsText(valueTag)
	end 'textColumn'

	// ⚠⚠ **THE LAST ARM IS THE INT ARM, AND IT IS DELIBERATELY A FALL-THROUGH RATHER THAN A `tagIsIntegral`
	// TEST WITH A PANIC PAST IT — because `declared` IS NOT CONFINED TO THE ADMITTED TAGS, and that was
	// MEASURED, not assumed.** A container's column gate runs where the container is MATERIALIZED
	// (`IntSet.create()`), so a method call on an instance this function never created reaches here with any
	// column type at all: `function add(s BadSet) s.insert(1.0) end` over `typealias BadSet = Set with float`
	// reaches it with a float column. A panic on the residual would replace that program's refusal with a
	// COMPILER CRASH, and the `insert(1)` spelling of the same program would lose its E2062 (the honest
	// diagnostic, on the type argument) to it. So the residual must ANSWER, not crash.
	//
	// ⛔ **BUT WHAT IT ANSWERS FOR A FLOAT IS NOW A WRONG REFUSAL, AND THE ARGUMENT THAT USED TO EXCUSE IT IS
	// RETRACTED.** That argument was *"a nonsense sentence, but on a program that is refused either way"* —
	// true only while `float` was no container's legal element. The `Array with float` work made a float array
	// FIRST-CLASS, so `[1.5, 2.5].map(function(v float) gives v * 2.0)` — a program that must COMPILE — comes
	// down `requireElementTransform` -> `elementTransformAnswersElement` -> here, reads FALSE off this arm and
	// is refused as *"`map`'s transform answers a float value where the container's element is a float"*: a
	// sentence that refutes itself, on a legal program. FILED AS ITS OWN RUNG — the fix is a FLOAT arm
	// (`tagIsFloat(declared)` -> `tagIsFloat(valueTag)`) ahead of this one plus the spec case that pins it,
	// and it is deliberately NOT carried by the conditional-extensions port, whose `contains` path never
	// reaches this predicate. Until then this comment states the defect rather than licensing it.
	//
	// A further ADMITTED HASH-TABLE column type must likewise be answered ABOVE this arm — by the
	// agrees-only-with-itself roster if it is its own type, by a class test of its own if it is a family — and
	// its gate is not what will stop you forgetting. `function` and `boolean` are the two that have been: a
	// `Map`'s VALUE column is no longer held to the key's three-name whitelist, so what may reach here as
	// `declared` is now the whole one-machine-word set. An AGGREGATE one never does — a nominal identity on
	// either side settles the question in `elementTypePreserved` before this predicate is consulted at all.
	return tagIsIntegral(valueTag)
end 'columnValueTagMatches'

// ⭐⭐ **DOES A TYPE STAND FOR THE CONTAINER'S OWN ELEMENT? — `map`'s element-PRESERVATION test, stated ONCE
// for the two halves of one declaration.** `stdlib/Interfaces.maxon` declares the transform
// `function(Element) returns Element`, so the question asked of what it ANSWERS and the question asked of
// what it TAKES are the same question, and the answer had better not depend on which half asks it.
//
// It is TWO questions only in the sense the PAIR makes it two:
//
//   • if EITHER side has a NOMINAL identity the question is settled by identity and by nothing else. `Point`
//     and `Size` are both `structRef`, and PRESERVING the element type means the SAME type, which only the
//     name says. A tag test would be too weak here even where it answers.
//   • only when NEITHER has one does the container-slot rule above decide, for that predicate's measured
//     reason: a ranged-int alias carries `named` where the element carries `integer`, and refusing that pair
//     would reject `[1,2,3].map(function(x Integer) gives x * 2)`, the construct itself.
//
// ⚠⚠ **"THE ELEMENT DECIDES WHICH QUESTION IS ASKED, NOT THE OTHER SIDE" STOOD HERE AND WAS A MEASURED
// CRASH (BATCH18 review).** Under it a structural element admitted *everything the container's own column
// would*, and a boxed enum's column answer is `integral` — so `[1,2,3].map(function(c Color) gives show(c))`
// over an INT array compiled clean and died `STATUS_STACK_OVERFLOW`, the ordinals 1..3 matched against a
// two-case `match`. **The identical call by DIRECT dispatch was already `E3005: expected 'Color', got
// 'int'`** — one compiler answering one question two ways, which is the defect this whole batch exists to
// close, reproduced inside its own cure. An identity on EITHER side is a fact the tag rule cannot see, so it
// is the tag rule that must stand aside, not the identity. This is the same widening
// `SemanticCheck.checkOneArgType` already made for the mirror quadrant (a SCALAR parameter meeting an
// AGGREGATE argument, which `tagAdmitsNoAggregate` guards) and for the same reason.
//
// ⚠ **EACH CALLER EXTRACTS THE IDENTITIES ITSELF, AND THE TWO EXTRACTORS ARE DELIBERATELY NOT THE SAME
// FUNCTION** — the same split `SemanticCheck.checkOneArgType` makes between one RULE and two name
// extractions. The parser compares two PRE-resolution types and reads both through `Parser.aggregateNameOf`;
// the whole-program drain compares a pre-resolution element against a RESOLVED parameter and must therefore
// read an identity that survives `resolveTypes` (see `Parser.resolvedElementIdentity`). Feeding one
// extractor's answer to the other tier is what would produce a false refusal, so the tier is the caller's to
// know and the verdict is this function's to give.
export function elementTypePreserved(elementTag ValueTypeTag, elementIdentity ByteArray, otherTag ValueTypeTag, otherIdentity ByteArray) returns bool
	if not elementIdentity.isEmpty() or not otherIdentity.isEmpty() 'eitherSideIsNominal'
		return elementIdentity.equals(otherIdentity)
	end 'eitherSideIsNominal'

	return columnValueTagMatches(otherTag, declared: elementTag)
end 'elementTypePreserved'
